The synthesis of a MOF material always starts with the strategic selection of precursor materials. Besides an organic building block, an inorganic compound providing metal ions is necessary, which most commonly is a metal salt, as it easily dissolves. A relatively new and different approach is the use of metal oxides (MOs). Even though their use is challenging due to low solubility issues and slow and non-homogeneous conversion, by carefully optimizing the experimental conditions the MO-to-MOF conversion can be carried out preserving the morphology of the MO. Thus, the use of MO precursors, which offers a huge number of different morphologies that can be synthesized with high precision, potentially provides an easy and smart way for the precise synthesis of different MOF morphologies. This strategy opens the door to morphologies that cannot be achieved using reactions based on metal salt precursors or other approaches.
Moreover, the careful carbonization of those MOF materials offers to translate this morphology control to porous carbon.11 MOF materials could be used as ‘‘all-in-one’’ templates/ precursors that contain both inorganic and organic constituents which can be pre-selected for preparation of targeted porous carbon materials, so called MOF-derived carbon (MDC), with specific morphologies and a plethora of applications, e.g. in energy storage, catalysis, adsorption/separation or pharmacy. MO, MOF and MDC are closely connected, which facilitates synthetic avenues from MO over MOF to MDC with control over morphology from the first building blocks to the final carbon material (Fig. 2). As such, control over MO morphology allows architecture direction over the whole synthesis route. This approach enables additional control over morphology and access to complex material architectures that are challenging to achieve via MOFs alone.12,13
In this review, we summarize the morphology control methods of MOF/MDC with particular emphasis on the use of ‘MO as primary architecture directing agents’. Starting from morphology control of MO itself, we cover the transformation of MO into MOF and finally into MDC. To do so, we categorize the reported MO/MOF/MDC products according to their synthetic strategies and particle morphologies (0D, 1D, 2D, 3D and hierarchical architectures). The synthetic methods, mechanisms, and their advantages and limitations are discussed comprehensively. Morphology controlled MOF and MDC materials have already shown great potential in a number of applications such as batteries, supercapacitors, fuel cells, adsorption, catalysis, sensing, and nanomedicine, which will be addressed as well. Overall, the review describes the avenue from MO over MOF to obtain well-defined MDC for relevant applications.
## 2. Morphology
According to the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC), morphology is defined as shape, optical appearance, texture or topography of surface and form of phase domains in materials. The expressions morphology and shape are usually used as synonyms when describing the external structure of nanoparticles and their aggregates.14,15 However, the differences between these concepts should be emphasized. The term morphology includes physical size, flatness, roundness, sphericity and aspect ratio of the nanoparticles while the shape considers their appearance without taking into account specific dimensions (Fig. 3).
The concept of morphology may seem intuitive until one attempts to determine the appropriate classification system of particles with regular and irregular shapes employing advanced 2D and 3D microscopy imaging techniques.16,17 This system of classification should be as versatile as possible and apply to a wide range of materials. In the case of nanostructures, the greatest difficulty in describing their morphology is that they undergo continuous transformations from one form to another. However, in 1995, Gleiter18 presented the first classification system of nanostructured materials that was explained in detail by Skorokhod et al.19 It was incomplete because it did not include zero-dimensional (0D), one-dimensional (1D), two-dimensional (2D) and three-dimensional (3D) nanostructures, e.g. fullerenes, tubes, and flowers. Thus, Pokropivny and Skorokhod20 developed a modified classification system, in which they took into account the above-mentioned nanostructures. Nanomaterials were classified as 0D, 1D, 2D, and 3D depending on their specific geometrical dimensions. Fig. 4 shows a schematic representation of various nanostructures and plots of energy dependence versus density of states.21 It was found that the degree of confinement determines the electronic density of state for different types of nanostructures.
## 2.1. Zero-dimensional nanostructures
Zero-dimensional (0D) nanostructures, e.g., quantum dots, spheres, ellipsoids, and polyhedra, have all dimensions measured within the nanoscale, varying from a few to several dozen nanometres (Fig. 5). They can be amorphous (randomly distributed atoms and molecules) or crystalline (atoms and molecules organized in a defined lattice pattern) and consist of single or more chemical elements.17,21–28
Quantum dots with diameters in the range of 2–10 nanometres are shapeless, irregular polyhedra indicating unique electronic properties, which is partly a result of their high surface-to-volume ratio. In this case, facets are not welldeveloped. The presence of under-coordinated atoms and high surface energy of quantum dots make them more liable to interactions with the surroundings.21–23
Zero-dimensional spheres are formed by reducing the surface energy of various nanomaterials. They exhibit high surface area-to-volume ratio, mechanical strength and good dispersion due to the stabilization of electrostatic charges. Spheres can be created to have well-developed porosity. It is worth noting that spherical morphology is the most stable that occurs in nature. Spheres elongated in one dimension are called ellipsoids. These nanostructures can be unstable because under extreme conditions sharp ends tend to shrink whereas surfaces with smaller curvature grow leading to minimizing surface energy so that they approach spherical geometry.17,21,22,24 Nanomaterials with specific cubic crystal symmetry, enclosed by (100) facets, typically occur in the form of cubes. The high surface energy of cubes related to the presence of under-coordinated atoms is usually lowered by rounding their edges and vertices. Cubes are often employed as building blocks to generate one-dimensional superstructures having well-defined interparticle orientations and regulated electromagnetic properties.21,22 Octahedra together with cubes are the most thoroughly studied nanostructures having interesting physicochemical features. They are composed of eight (111) facets. In 2D microscopic projection, octahedra can be visible as rectangles, rhombuses, and hexagons depending on their orientation. In addition, octahedra are considered classic models for testing the surface properties of (111) facets. However, their precise synthesis is quite difficult.22,26,29
## 2.2. One-dimensional nanostructures
One-dimensional (1D) nanostructures including rods, wires, tubes, belts, hooks, ribbons, needles, fibres, helices (or spirals) and zigzags are characterized by the high ratio of one dimension over two other, which are equivalent, and in the nanoscale (length $\gg$ width or diameter).17,21,22,24,25 Many of them are presented in the literature as acicular-like structures whose length can even reach the millimetre range (Fig. 6).22
Rods exhibiting aspect ratios of 3–5 may occur as aggregates composed of separate building blocks like dots, polyhedra or platelets.22,30 Wires have aspect ratios even more than 1000 and they are described by greater flexibility and slenderness compared to rods.31 The structure of wires is so simple that there is no space for any defects, thus electrons pass through unimpeded. In contrast to rods and wires, tubes have more complex hollow structures and can be single- or multi-walled. Their properties differ depending on how they are rolled up. Belts are extremely thin and flat structures of ribbon shape with a rectangular cross-section. Moreover, they possess high specific surface areas and are therefore often used in catalysis and photocatalysis.25,39,40
Progress in developing new synthesis methods of 1D nanostructures with precisely controlled dimensions, phase purity, morphology, and chemical composition has contributed to their wide application as functional units in the design of solar cells, electronic nanodevices, supercapacitors, gas sensors or transistors. Xia et al.39 have compiled bottom-up synthetic strategies for fabrication of 1D nanostructures which involve: (i) utilization of the intrinsically anisotropic crystallographic structure of a solid to achieve 1D growth, (ii) reduction of the seed symmetry by introducing a liquid–solid interface, (iii) modification of seed growth by control of supersaturation, (iv) a capping reagent assisted procedure in order to kinetically control the growth rates of different facets of seeds, (v) application of appropriate templates exhibiting 1D morphologies to manage the 1D nanostructure creation, (vi) 0D nanostructure self-assembly and (vii) reducing the size of 1D microstructures.
It should be noted that a wide variety of materials can naturally grow into 1D nanostructures which is influenced by highly anisotropic bonding in crystallographic structures. They exhibit distinct optical, thermal, chemical and electronic properties. In addition, 1D nanostructures are interesting systems for studying new phenomena at the nanoscale and dependence of specific physicochemical properties on the shape and dimensions.
## 2.3. Two-dimensional nanostructures
Two-dimensional (2D) nanomaterials, possessing thickness in the range from a few to tens of nanometres and lateral dimension reaching up to many centimetres, have been a subject of intensive research in the last few years.17,21,22,25,41 They are described in the literature as single- or multilayered nanostructures with strong bonds in plane and weak van der Waals forces between layers which are often deposited on the surface of various substrates (e.g. silicon, nickel sheets, copper foil). 2D nanostructures typically include platelets, sheets, flakes, rings, membranes, walls, prisms, discs, and films characterized by unique geometry, well-developed surface area and high surface-to-volume ratio (Fig. 7).25,41,42 In addition, the advantages of 2D nanostructures are also the presence of many active sites, straightforward surface functionalization and the possibility to assemble into 3D complex structures. Up to now, 2D nanostructures of graphene, MOs, polymers, metal hydroxides, metal dichalcogenides, boron nitride, phosphorene, MXenes and MOFs have been synthesized with the use of strategies based on extraction, exfoliation, chemical or physical vapor deposition and various wet chemical methods.41
In 2D microscopic images, platelets can appear in the form of flat triangles, rectangles, rhombuses or hexagons which are largely dependent on the crystal lattice and growth direction of materials.43 Those having circular or irregular shapes are called discs and discoids.22,46 Films and sheets are 2D nanostructures with very high but not well-defined aspect ratios exhibiting superior mechanical properties which include hardness and strength.41,42,49 It is worth noting that during the formation of films, their interaction with appropriately selected substrate determines the initial nucleation and growth. Three basic modes of initial nucleation involve: (i) island or Volmer–Weber growth, (ii) layer or Frank–van der Merwe growth, and (iii) island-layer or Stranski–Krastonov growth. Island growth takes place when the growth species are more strongly bonded to each other than to the substrate as opposed to layer growth mode. In the latter one, first the monolayer is completely formed before the deposition of the second layer. Island-layer growth is a combination of layer growth and island growth modes.42 Rings, which are one of the leaststudied 2D nanostructures, can be fabricated by the rupture of platelets in order to generate a hole in the centre.51
Easy and precise control of 2D nanostructure composition enables exploration and optimization of their functional properties which contribute to the extension of applications in the design of novel sensors, electronic devices, photocatalysts, and reactors.
## 2.4. Three-dimensional nanostructures
Three-dimensional (3D) nanostructures are described as complex structures assembled from 0D, 1D, 2D or multiscale nanocomponents which have three random dimensions larger than $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The examples are multipods, balls (dendritic structures), cones, pillars, and hierarchical structures such as flowers, trees, branches, and urchins (Fig. 8).17,21,22,25,35,52,53 Moreover, both spheres and polyhedra (e.g. tetrahedra, cubes, octahedra, prisms) can also be classified into 3D nanostructures if they are polycrystalline and obtained via the aggregation of fundamental building blocks including dots, rods or platelets. Compared with 0D spheres and polyhedra, they are characterized by larger sizes and porosity. Their surfaces can be smooth, rough or occupied by various types of adsorbates. 3D spheres and polyhedra often occur in the form of hollow or multilayered structures.22
Multipods, exhibiting high aspect ratio, are composed of large sphere-like cores with long arms of uniform width and rounded ends which extend out from the cores. Most often the number of arms is in the range from 4 to 8. Their length is measured from the tip to the base, whereas the width is determined in the centre area of the multipod arms.22,53
Cones have height and diameter of the same order of magnitude. It should be noted that they are mainly made of carbon and occur e.g. on the surface of natural graphite.62 In turn, balls are well-ordered spherical nanostructures possessing dendritic exteriors. They can be formed by the aggregation of small particles, which causes their surface to be rough.60
Hierarchical nanostructures are defined as integrated architectures consisting of low-dimensional sub-units, e.g. 0D dots, spheres, 1D wires, rods or tubes, and 2D sheets which are arranged in a well-ordered manner. They mimic structures widely found in nature such as trees with trunks and branches, plant flowers composed of petals, and sea urchins whose surface is spiny.63,64 Synthesis of materials with hierarchical nanostructures gives the opportunity to precisely adjust their physicochemical properties for specific applications. Flowerlike structures having size in the range $100{-}500~\mathrm{nm}$ consist of several layers of inteconnected petals with relatively small thickness and different shapes.17,22,57,65 All petals are dense at the cores and radiate from the centre of flowers to the outside surface. Their shape and dimensions are very difficult to control during synthesis. Flowers are characterized by high surface-tovolume ratio and demonstrate a large number of adsorption sites as well as good charge transfer. This type of architecture can increase the use of nanomaterials in the design of novel catalysts, solar cells, biosensors (detection of conditions such as food infection, diabetes, Parkinson’s disease, Alzheimer’s disease, etc.), devices, fuel cells and drug delivery agents.17,22,65
Among the 3D nanostructures, branches have been widely studied due to their straightforward modification and applicability to various types of chemical compounds. They are composed of small arms growing radially from the major frame. Both the morphology and length of branches influence the physicochemical properties of nanomaterials. Many synthetic strategies have been described in the literature for branch-like structures, e.g. the formation of branches on a nanowire backbone by a hydrothermal method or chemical vapor deposition (CVD), and preparation of hyper-branched structures via thermal evaporation. Branches that are less densely packed grow depending on pre-deposited seeds. Together with trees, they are examples of fractal structures.61,66 Urchin-like structures are covered with radially oriented and interconnected needles of high density. They are a bit similar to multipods, but urchins have a much larger number of arms attached to the spherical cores.59,67
Despite the huge efforts to control the growth mechanism of three-dimensional nanostructures, some fundamental phenomena are not well understood. Therefore exploration of the impact of size and morphology on their physicochemical properties is still required in order to plan the optimal application of these nanomaterials.17,21,22,25
## 3. Morphology control of metal oxide nanostructures
## 3.1. General aspects
MOs are an important class of inorganic materials with diverse composition and morphology. They are widely used in the design of catalysts,68–74 sensors,75–78 solar cells,79–83 adsorbents of many contaminants,84–87 drug delivery agents,88–91 and additives for skincare products.92–95 Significant efforts over the last few years have focused on the synthesis of MO nanostructures with controlled morphology that exhibit new interesting optical, magnetic, thermal, and catalytic properties and thus can enable novel applications. Unique physicochemical properties of MO nanoparticles originate from their small size and high density of the corner or edge surface sites in conjunction with a shape effect.73,74,96 Moreover, the exposed facets of the MO nanocrystals and the high specific surface area can strongly determine their surface chemistry.96–99
From the scientific point of view, in order to fully exploit the potential of MO nanomaterials, it is necessary to understand the relation between their shape, size and the resulting properties. Accordingly, the synthesis of uniform oxide nanoparticles with precise control of morphology is crucial to characterize their specific properties. In addition, shape-controlled MO nanocrystals can be fundamental building blocks for the bottom-up assembly of novel nano- and microstructures.96,100–102 It should be noted that reducing the particle size of MOs to the nanometric scale causes their properties to be governed by high surface area-to-volume ratio and quantum confinement effects. As such, the atoms at the surface of MO nanoparticles dominate over the internal atoms. Changes in thermodynamic stability associated with decreasing size to the nanometre regime can bring about a modification of cell parameters or structural transformations. In some cases, the oxide nanoparticles can be degraded due to interactions with their surrounding environment, high surface free energy, and the large surface curvature.103 Therefore, to exhibit mechanical or structural stability, the surface free energy of nanoparticles must be lowered. As a result, phases having low stability in bulk materials can become very stable in nanostructures. Additionally, it has been revealed that the electronic structure of oxide nanomaterials could be altered from continuous electronic bands to discrete or quantized electronic levels. The size of the nanocrystals has also a significant influence on the electron-transport process.102–104 Similarly, the shape of the MO nanoparticles plays a key role in the determination of their properties. It depends on the crystalline structure of MO nanomaterials because the growth of primary particles is imposed by the symmetry and dimensionality of the structure. The orientation of the crystal facets can be adjusted with the surface atomic arrangement and coordination.98 With reducing particle size, the surface area-to-volume ratio increases and the shape could impart its own effect on the surface structure.96 Many nanostructures of MOs including spheres, cubes, octahedra, polyhedra, wires, rods, tubes, belts, sheets, platelets and complicated hierarchical structures (e.g. flowers, branches) have been reported in the literature. As mentioned in Section 2, they are classified as zerodimensional (0D), one-dimensional (1D), two-dimensional (2D) and three-dimensional (3D).
Controlled synthesis of MO nanostructures is the main strategy for achieving desired shape and size. So far, many valuable reviews21,22,96,99,101,102,104–106 have described the preparation methods of oxide nanomaterials with tunable morphology, but the topic has not been completely explored. Due to the extensive scope of the synthetic approaches, as well as the diversity of MO nanostructures and composition, a huge challenge is to create a general algorithm that would facilitate the selection of the appropriate method of synthesis and its conditions in order to obtain a specific shape and size of nanoparticles. It is necessary to compile literature data that will allow for a deep analysis of what parameters in the chosen preparation method have the greatest impact on the morphology of MO nanomaterials.
## 3.2. Synthetic strategies for metal oxide nanostructures with controlled morphology
MO nanomaterials can be fabricated with the use of two general strategies: bottom-up and top-down (Fig. 9).100,101,105 These synthetic strategies differ in the way how a specific nanostructure is created. In the bottom-up approach, MO nanomaterials are obtained from basic building blocks (e.g. atoms or molecules) that react and grow in size or self-assemble into a more complex structure. In contrast, the top-down approach involves breaking down bulk materials (e.g. cutting, grinding or etching) to the nanoscale structures.38 The advantage of the top-down approach is high yield, but it requires expensive, complex and specialized equipment to maintain specific conditions including pressure, temperature or environment. Moreover, it should be noted that the obtained nanostructures often have surface defects and non-uniform shapes which limit their applicability. The control over the size and shape of MO nanoparticles through the top-down strategy is very difficult.101,102,105 Therefore, the bottom-up wet chemical methods are often selected for preparation of MO nanostructures due to their simplicity, reproducibility, low energy consumption, easily available and cheap equipment as well as the theoretical possibility of precisely controlling all dimensions, morphology, and monodispersity of the products.21,22,101,105,106 In the wet chemical syntheses, the nucleation and growth kinetics of the seeds strongly influence the shape of the MO nanostructures. In order to control the size and morphology of the nanocrystals some critical reaction parameters such as the temperature, time, precursor and surfactant concentration, and the molar ratio of precursor to capping ligand should be precisely adjusted in the crystal nucleation-growth stages.
Understanding the formation mechanisms of nanoparticles is the key to manipulate the morphology. The creation of nanoparticles can be described by the classical Ostwald ripening mechanism, in which the growth of larger particles at the expense of smaller ones is driven by surface energy reduction. This phenomenon is primarily applied to explain the fabrication of thermodynamically stable nanocrystals with nearly spherical shapes.96,99 In the case of the controlled self-assembly of nanoparticle building blocks into more complex anisotropic nanostructures, organic capping reagents usually play a crucial role in reducing the activity of the nanocrystal surface. The organic ligand with high nanoparticle binding affinity can promote or adjust an oriented-assembly, driven by the interaction between surface-adsorbed ligands instead of between the particles themselves. The selective adsorption of the capping molecules (e.g. fatty acids, alkyl amines, amino acids, trioctylphosphine oxide, block copolymers) onto specific crystal facets of the growing nanoparticles can result in controlling the product morphology.21,22,96,99,101,105,106
In recent years, many studies have been dedicated to developing various synthetic approaches that allow the simultaneous control of the size, shape, and monodispersity of MO nanostructures with high potential applications in biomedicine, catalysis, optical sensing, and energy conversion technology. In this section, we focus on the presentation of the most important methods of MO synthesis (e.g. sol–gel, hydro/solvothermal, biogenic, thermolysis, reverse micelle, electrodeposition, two-phase route, flame spray pyrolysis). Their different aspects such as advantages, limitations and product morphology are presented in Table 1.
3.2.1. Sol–gel method. The synthesis of MO nanostructures by the sol–gel method is based on an inorganic polymerization reaction occurring through the solvolysis/condensation mechanism.22 The main step of this route comprises hydrolysis or alcoholysis of precursors (metal alkoxides, halides) to generate a colloidal solution of metal hydroxide (sol), followed by condensation leading to obtaining a metal–oxygen–metal interlocked network (gel). Then the solvent is slowly removed at elevated temperature $\left(60–100\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\right)$ . The final product with the desired crystal phase is achieved after thermal treatment causing decomposition of the residual gel.21,22,106–108 It has to be taken into account that the synthesis parameters including the concentration of precursor, nature of solvent, $\mathsf{p H}$ , and pre and post heat treatment strongly determine the morphology and properties of the MO nanostructures. The sol–gel method allows controlling the crystal phase, shape, and size of the materials obtained. Up to now, this synthetic route has been developed to fabricate a wide range of MOs e.g. $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ,109–113 $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ ,30,46,114 NiO,115,116 ${\bf V}_{2}{\bf O}_{5}$ ,117 $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ ,118 $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ ,119 and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ,34,36,120–125 characterized by diverse morphology.
Kuo et al.110 described a simple and rapid seed-mediated strategy for the synthesis of monodispersed $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanocubes with sizes varying from 40 to $420\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The nanocubes were obtained within $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. It was found that the combination of the $\mathrm{CuSO_{4}}$ precursor and sodium dodecyl sulfate played a key role in the formation of structurally welldefined $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanocubes. Pang and $\mathrm{Zeng^{111}}$ synthesized $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ spheres with uniform diameters in the range of $130{-}135~\mathrm{nm}$ by a facile sol–gel method at room temperature in the presence of hydrazine hydrate as a capping agent. The monodispersity of the spheres permitted the formation of 2D and 3D superlattices. Moreover, cuprous oxide was used as a starting solid precursor in the preparation of hollow spheres of copper sulfide (CuS) and copper selenide (CuSe). Yang and coworkers reported shape-controlled synthesis of well-defined truncated edge polyhedral $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ architectures, including truncated edge cubic and truncated edge octahedral $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ crystals. The concentrations of $\mathrm{\o{OH}^{-}}$ and $\mathbf{CH_{3}C O O^{-}}$ ions, the reaction temperature and time were tuned to control the final morphology of the products. Various shapes of materials were achieved via selective oxidative etching and preferential growth on different facets of $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}O}$ crystals. Huang et $a l.^{113}$ prepared a series of $\mathrm{{Cu_{2}O}}$ nanocrystals with systematic morphology evolution from cubic to rhombic dodecahedral structures and sizes ranging from 140 to $300\mathrm{nm}$ at room temperature by mixing an aqueous solution of $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ , sodium dodecyl sulfate surfactant, NaOH, and $\mathbf{NH}_{2}\mathbf{OH}{\cdot}\mathbf{HCl}$ reductant. With increasing amount of reductant added to the reaction system, the shapes of cuprous oxide changed from cubic to face-raised cubic, edgeand corner-truncated octahedral, all-corner-truncated rhombic dodecahedral, 100-truncated rhombic dodecahedral, and rhombic dodecahedral (Fig. 10).
Chen et al.46 demonstrated a versatile synthetic procedure for the fabrication of $\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanocrystals of different morphologies (square nanoplates, mesocrystal nanodiscs, nanopolyhedra) at room temperature with the use of manganese(II) acetate tetrahydrate and hydrazine monohydrate. The shape and size of the materials were controlled by adjusting the concentration or ratio of reactants. Synthesis of $\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles at room temperature was also realized by mixing the aqueous solution of $\bf{M n S O_{4}}$ and $\mathbf{Na}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ .30 Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images showed a $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ sample consisting of almost spherical agglomerate particles with diameters in the range $50\mathrm{-}100\ \mathrm{~nm}$ . Hydrothermal treatment of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles in acidic solution at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ led to the formation of $\mathsf{\gamma}_{\mathsf{Y}^{-}}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ nanorods with uniform diameters of $5{\mathrm{-}}10\ \mathrm{nm}$ and lengths of $100{\cdot}400\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Qiao et al.114 designed multi-step preparation of porous double-shelled hollow, solid and yolk-structured $\mathbf{Mn}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ spheres at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ . The synthetic protocol involved precipitation, controlled oxidation, selective etching and calcination.
Jang and coworkers115 synthesized various types of NiO nanostructures with flower-, slice-, and spherical shapes via a sol–gel method. $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were used as precursors and ammonium hydroxide, lithium hydroxide, and hexamethylene tetramine acted as reagents controlling the reaction conditions. The pore size distributions of NiO changed depending on the morphology of the structure and packing. Nanoslices and nanoparticles had an average pore diameter of 4 and $8\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Bimodal pore size distributions with peak maxima centered at $\sim4$ and $15\ \mathrm{nm}$ were obtained for the nanoflower-shaped NiO.
Karami and Mohammadi117 presented a very interesting poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA) based sol–gel method for the fabrication of uniform ${\bf V}_{2}{\bf O}_{5}$ nanostructures. In this procedure, the ${\bf N H}_{4}{\bf V O}_{3}$ precursor and PVA were dissolved in an ethanol : water (70 : 30) mixture to obtain the sol followed by the evaporation of the solvent resulting in a homogeneous polymeric gel. The last stage of the synthesis involved calcination at $560^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The results indicated that the amount of vanadium salt, gel-forming agent, solvent composition and synthesis additives strongly affected the morphology of the product. As the aforementioned parameters were changed, the shape of ${\bf V}_{2}{\bf O}_{5}$ nanostructures evolved from simple spherical nanoparticles to uniform nanoflakes with a mean thickness of $37~\mathrm{nm}$ . The morphology of vanadium pentoxide was controlled by the rigidity of the gel network.
Kang et al.118 focused on the sol–gel synthesis of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\gamma{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ nanoparticles in an aqueous solution without using any surfactant. The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were nearly spherical and had a narrow size distribution with an average diameter of $8.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Their size was smaller than that of other particles reported in the literature. The oxidation of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ into $\gamma{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ was performed by adjusting the pH of the hydrosol to about 3.5 and stirring for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ at $100~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ . Applying this procedure needle-like $\gamma\mathrm{-Fe}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ nanoparticles with lengths of $20{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ and widths of $4{\mathrm{-}}6~\mathrm{nm}$ were synthesized.
Itteboina and $\mathrm{Sau}^{119}$ reported the preparation of morphologycontrolled $\mathbf{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ particles by a sol–gel method employing cobalt(II) chloride hexahydrate, cobalt(II) acetate tetrahydrate, and cobalt(II) perchlorate hexahydrate as precursors. The shape of the resulting $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ particles varied from octahedral to hexagonal and planar with smooth surfaces, sharp edges or corners depending on metal salts, solvents, and capping agents used in the synthetic procedure. These parameters also played a crucial role in determining the particle size.
Sugimoto et al.120–123 conducted a series of studies focused on the application of the sol–gel method for the fabrication of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles of different sizes and shapes. Titanium tetraisopropoxide was used as a precursor and different amines including triethanolamine, diethylenetriamine, ethylenediamine, trimethylenediamine, and triethylenetetramine acted as the shape controlling agents. The morphology of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles evolved from cuboidal to ellipsoidal when the synthesis was performed at $\mathsf{p H}$ above 11 in the presence of triethanolamine. Using diethylenetriamine at $\mathsf{p H}$ above 9.5 led to the formation of ellipsoidal nanoparticles with a high aspect ratio.123 Tertiary amines, such as trimethylamine and triethylamine promoted the growth of ellipsoidal particles with lower aspect ratios. The shape of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles was also changed from roundcornered cubes to sharp-edged cubes employing sodium oleate and sodium stearate. Tuning the growth rate of the crystal planes of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ via specific adsorption of shape controlling agents under different $\mathsf{p H}$ conditions allowed obtaining nanoparticles with desired morphology.123 Kobayashi et al.36 described a sol–gel synthesis of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ material with a hollow-fibre structure like ‘‘macaroni’’ using trans- $(1R,2R).$ -1,2-cyclohexanedi-(11-aminocarbonylundecylpyridinium)hexafluorophosphate, as a template. The process involved the polymerization of titanium tetraisopropoxide and the self-assembly of the amphiphilic compound containing cationic charge moieties. Fibrous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ materials were formed by the electrostatic interaction between the anionic titania species and the cationic compound serving as a template under basic conditions. In 2003, Gundiah et al.124 applied a tripodal cholamide-based hydrogel as a template to prepare $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanotubes. The diameters of the nanotubes were controlled by adopting the appropriate hydrogelator and slightly modifying the gelation conditions.
3.2.2. Hydro/solvothermal methods. Hydro/solvothermal syntheses involve chemical reactions carried out in water or organic solvents at temperatures above their boiling points and at high pressures.24,32,101,126–140 All reactants are placed in a sealed vessel (e.g. bomb or autoclave). These approaches are suitable for the preparation of MO nanostructures with controlled size and morphology. The most commonly used precursors are aqueous solutions of salts such as metal chlorides, nitrates, sulphates or acetates. Their hydrothermal treatment can promote hydrolysis reactions.21,22,24,32,101,105,106,126–140 The shape, size, and monodispersity of the MO nanostructures can be regulated by tuning the reaction parameters (e.g. temperature, time, pressure, specific properties of solvents). It should be noted that the rapid growth of oxide monomers in highpolarity solvents can lead to the formation of large size particle aggregates. Therefore, to control the morphology and uniformity of MO particles, various surfactants acting as capping agents are introduced into the reaction mixture.96,101,106 In this case, the reaction system always consists of an oil, surfactant and aqueous phase. The presence of a surfactant can control the growth of the product.106
Li et al.24 prepared $\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanocrystals with tunable sizes and shapes in the presence of dodecanol and oleylamine surfactants under mild experimental conditions. Manganese(II) nitrate was applied as a precursor. Morphology of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ evolved from dots to rods and wires depending on the concentration of metal salt, employed surfactants, volume of the solvents and reaction time. Additionally, the as-prepared hydrophobic spherical or elongated nanoparticles were assembled into 3D $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ colloidal spheres using a facile ultrasonication strategy.
Wang et al.130 described the large-scale synthesis of $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanostructure arrays on nickel foam substrates via a hydrothermal process in a temperature range of $50\mathrm{-}90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . In this procedure, different cobalt salts including $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ were used as precursors. The shapes of the as-synthesized $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanostructures were strongly affected by the type of cobalt precursor applied and the growth parameters. Flower-like $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ structures composed of hundreds of nanorods with a length of $2{\cdot}4~{\upmu}\mathrm{m}$ were formed at $90~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ using $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as the cobalt source. In turn, at ${70~^{\circ}}\mathbf{C}$ the nanorod arrays adopted a necklace-like morphology when $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was employed as a precursor. The nanorods consisted of interconnected nanocrystallites similar to those grown at $90~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ (Fig. 11). Two-step synthesis of functional $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ with highly ordered structures of different shapes has been demonstrated by Wan and coworkers.131 Firstly cobalt acetate reacted with ethylene glycol in the presence of poly(vinyl pyrrolidone) (PVP), resulting in a cobalt oxide precursor which was then calcined at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain crystalline $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . The morphology of the products was regulated by tuning the reaction conditions (time, initial concentration of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H_{2}O})$ . The shape of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ varied from nanospheres, 2D nanoplates and 3D cabbage-like hierarchical structures, and finally to microspheres. CoO and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanotubes were also prepared by solvothermal treatment of colloidal hydroxide without using any catalyst, surfactant, or template.132 CoO nanotubes obtained had an outer diameter of $18\mathrm{nm}$ and an inner diameter of about $7\mathrm{nm}$ . They were converted to $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanotubes composed of irregular nanoparticles after annealing at $650~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air. It was shown that the duration of solvothermal treatment strongly influenced the morphology of the materials.
Xia and coworkers133 described a facile synthetic procedure based on polyol reduction for the fabrication of cuprous oxide nanostructures with well-defined shapes. Sodium chloride was added to the reaction mixture to manipulate the crystal properties and consequently to control the morphology of the product. Polycrystalline colloidal spheres with a mean diameter of $360~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ were synthesized by reducing copper nitrate with ethylene glycol at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the presence of PVP. The introduction of a small amount of sodium chloride led to obtaining $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanocubes with an average length of $410~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .
Shang et al.134 reported the fabrication of rose-like NiO nanostructures by calcination of the solvothermally obtained $\mathsf{\alpha{\mathrm{-}N i}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ with a similar morphology. TEM images indicated that the structures were made of flakes with a thickness of about $20~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Moreover, the decomposition of $\mathsf{\alpha{\mathrm{-}N i}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ at $420~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize5h}$ led to the formation of many holes between NiO flakes.
Hierarchical porous NiO nanoflowers were also synthesized by Ren and Gao.135 Firstly high-quality flower-like $\mathrm{\bfNi(OH)}_{2}$ was obtained using microwave heating of nickel nitrate hydrate in ethanol at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15\mathrm{min}$ . Then the product was calcined to form NiO nanostructures with well-developed specific surface areas. It was found that the shape of calcined NiO particles originated from the flower-like architecture of the $\mathsf{\alpha{\mathrm{-}N i}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ precursor. However, the surface of the NiO nanoflowers was rough and grainy. The petals were composed of well-defined nanoparticles with a diameter of $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ .
Flower-like ZnO nanostructures were prepared via an organic-free hydrothermal method with the use of zinc acetate as a precursor.136 The morphology of the ZnO nanostructures was strongly affected by the reaction temperature and $\mathsf{p H}$ of the solution. The typical flower-like ZnO nanostructures consisted of sword-like ZnO nanorods with width in the range of $60{-}200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and several micrometres in length.
In 2007, He et al.137 presented a simple hydrothermal route performed in the presence of poly(ethylene glycol) 400 (PEG 400) at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for the synthesis of uniform, single-crystalline $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanowires. Ferrous sulphate was selected as a precursor. The nanowires obtained had a narrow size distribution centered at $15\ \mathrm{nm}$ and a length of up to $3\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Wan et al.138 prepared uniform single-crystal $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanorods via a soft template-assisted hydrothermal route at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ In this procedure, ethylenediamine was used as a soft template and benzene as an oil membrane for preventing oxidation of ferrous ions. It was found that the as-synthesized product mainly consisted of uniform $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanorods with an average diameter of $25\ \mathrm{nm}$ and length of $200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . A single crystalline triangular $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoprisms employing a direct hydrothermal method in the presence of 1,3-propanediamine (PDA) and ethylene glycol (EG) were fabricated by Li et al.139 The synthesis was carried out in an autoclave at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ with $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ $\mathsf{6H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ as an iron source. Magnetite nanostructures were mainly composed of triangular nanoprisms with average edge length and thickness of $113~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $25\ \mathrm{\nm}$ , respectively. A small fraction of the polyhedral nanoparticles with an average size of $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ was also observed. The morphology evolution of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was controlled by tuning the volume ratio of EG to PDA.
Dinh et al.140 developed a versatile solvothermal method for obtaining titania nanocrystals with various shapes including rhombic, truncated rhombic, spherical, dog-bone, truncated and elongated rhombic or bar. In the synthetic protocol, water vapor was applied to accelerate controlled hydrolysis of titanium butoxide to form $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles, while oleic acid and oleylamine were adopted as two distinct capping surfactants. The morphology control of titania was realized by tuning the oleic acid:oleylamine ratio and reaction temperature. It was shown that the shape of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ evolved from rhombic to truncated rhombic and sphere with increasing ratio of surfactants from 4 : 6 to 5 : 5 and 6 : 4. In turn, when the molar ratio of titanium precursor to oleic acid and oleylamine was changed from 1 : 6 : 4 to 2 : 6 : 4 the morphology of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocrystals transformed from spheres to dog-bone.
3.2.3. Thermal decomposition (thermolysis). MO nanostructures with desired size and shape can be obtained by thermal decomposition of organometallic compounds in highboiling, coordinating or non-coordinating organic solvents (e.g. 1-octadecene, benzyl ether, phenyl ether, trioctylamine) containing a surfactant as a stabilizing agent at high temperature and ambient pressure.21,22,106,141–152 In this procedure, metal acetylacetonates, acetates, oxalates, cupferronates, alkoxides or carbonyls are often applied as organometallic precursors while fatty acids, aliphatic amines, alkyl phosphonic acids, and alkyl thiols can serve as capping agents.141–152 The ratio of the starting reagents such as metal–organic salts, solvents, surfactants, and specific reaction conditions (e.g. time and temperature) have a direct influence on the obtained morphology. Additionally, the choice of appropriate organometallic compounds and the balance between nucleation and growth stages are important for the synthesis of highly monocrystalline products. MO nanoparticles achieved by thermal decomposition are monodisperse in shape and size. In thermolysis, the stages of nucleation and growth occur separately, because the preformed nuclei are added to the reaction solution containing the monomers that subsequently grow on the surface of small particles without further nucleation. Consequently, precise control of the morphology of MO nanoparticles is possible.141–152
Up to now, a facile thermal decomposition method has been used to obtain samaria $\left(\mathrm{Sm}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}\right)$ nanowires and nanoplates.145 $\mathrm{Sm}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanowires with a rectangular shape were synthesized by the thermolysis of hydrated samarium(III) acetate in the presence of a mixture of oleylamine and decanoic acid under an argon atmosphere. They had a uniform thickness of about $1\mathrm{nm}$ and width of $2\:\mathrm{nm}$ , with lengths of more than $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Due to the high surface energy, the nanowires generated parallel arrays via the self-assembly process. When in the synthesis oleic acid with oleylamine was used as the surfactant, squareshaped $\mathrm{Sm}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoplates having a thickness of $\sim1\ \mathrm{nm}$ were formed. Nanoplates of other rare-earth MOs, such as $\mathbf{La}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Nd}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , ${\bf Y}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ , and $\mathrm{Pr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , were achieved employing a very similar procedure. Cao et al.146 described the fabrication of ${\bf G}{\bf d}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ nanoplates by thermal decomposition of a gadolinium–acetate precursor in hot octadecene containing oleylamine and oleic acid. Evaporation of the solvent during sample preparation for TEM analysis led to the assembly of ${\bf G}{\bf d}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ nanoplates into ribbons of stacked plates.
Hyeon et al.147 described the synthesis of highly crystalline and monodisperse $\gamma{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ with a size in the range of $4{\cdot}16~\mathrm{{nm}}$ by thermal decomposition of iron pentacarbonyl $\left(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CO})_{5}\right)$ in the presence of oleic acid. High temperature $(300~^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ aging of iron-oleic acid metal complex caused the formation of iron nanoparticles that were transformed in the next step to $\gamma\mathrm{-Fe}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ by controlled oxidation with the use of trimethylamine oxide as a mild oxidant. Moreover, monodisperse $\gamma{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ nanocrystallites with a particle size of $13\ \mathrm{nm}$ can be generated via the direct oxidation of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CO})_{5}$ with trimethylamine oxide in the presence of oleic acid. In both approaches, it is possible to obtain 2D or 3D close-packed superlattices. The authors revealed that during the thermal decomposition of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CO})_{5}$ the nucleation occurs at low temperature, whereas the growth derived from the decomposition of the iron oleate complex takes place at a higher temperature. These stages should be separated. A similar procedure was applied for the synthesis of MnO nanorods and nanospheres.148 These nanostructures were fabricated by thermal decomposition of Mn-surfactant complexes prepared by reacting $\mathbf{Mn}_{2}(\mathbf{CO})_{10}$ with oleylamine at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The morphology of the MnO nanoparticles was precisely controlled by tuning the reaction temperature. The 1D nanorods were generated when two surfactants with different stabilizing capabilities were employed under kinetically controlled conditions, such as those requiring rapid thermal decomposition at high temperature (Fig. 12a). Spherical nanoparticles were formed under thermodynamically controlled conditions, i.e. corresponding to the slow decomposition of the precursors over a long time. The sizes of MnO nanoparticles were controlled by varying the stabilizing agents (trioctylphosphine, triphenylphosphine). The diameters of MnO nanospheres were in the range of 5 to $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ . MnO nanorods had dimensions of $7~\mathrm{nm}$ (diameter) $\times\ 33\ \mathrm{nm}$ (length) and $8\ \mathrm{nm}$ (diameter) $\times~140~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ (length). Formation of monodisperse MnO nanocrystals by simple heating of manganese(II) stearate in octadecene was systematically studied by Chen et al.149 The authors observed that the shape of manganese(II) oxide nanocrystals (rice, rods, peanuts, needles, dots) can be changed by adding an activation reagent (octadecanol) or an inhibitor (stearic acid) to the system prior to heating (Fig. 12b–d). Zitoun et al.150 reported the application of the thermal decomposition approach for the production of MnO multipods (Fig. 12e–g). The $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathrm{oleate})_{2}$ prepared from manganese acetate and sodium oleate was used as a precursor. The mechanism of MnO particle formation involved two stages: (i) the nucleation of truncated octahedra or cubes and (ii) the growth of homogeneous pods. The rate of the precipitation of multipods strongly determined their overall size and arm lengths.
A significant limitation of the traditional thermolysis is the application of high-cost and toxic organometallic precursors; therefore, many research groups have started to develop alternative strategies based on cheaper and less hazardous chemical compounds.101 Salavati-Niasari et al.151 synthesized $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanorods by thermal decomposition of inexpensive cobalt oxalate of low toxicity in the presence of triphenylphosphine and oleylamine as surfactants in order to control the morphology. The average length of the obtained nanorods determined using TEM was around $500\mathrm{nm}$ whereas the diameter was about $20\mathrm{nm}$ . The authors revealed that the external faces of initial nuclei have an impact on the final morphology of the products. Binding of triphenylphosphine and oleylamine to a high energy surface as capping molecules can selectively reduce the energy of this face relative to the other external faces. Therefore, sequential growth takes place along the external faces and 1D nanostructures are formed. Seo et al.152 developed a highly effective one-pot synthetic procedure to synthesize 1D nanostructures of transition MOs including $\bf{W_{18}O_{49}}$ , $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $\bf{M n}_{3}\bf{O}_{4}$ , $\mathbf{V}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{5}$ with narrow size distributions. The well-defined nanorods were obtained through a simple reaction of metal chlorides with a mixture of oleic acid and oleylamine surfactants. As a result, stable MO monomers were formed and promoted the anisotropic growth of nanocrystals along the high energy crystallographic direction.
3.2.4. Reverse micelle (microemulsions). Reverse micelles are the hydrophilic cavities of microemulsions formed from a mixture of surfactant, water, and oil.96,153,154 In 1982 for the first time, they were applied as templates in the preparation of inorganic nanomaterials.155 The reverse micellar synthesis of nanostructures belongs to wet chemical methods and allows excellent control of size, morphology, uniformity, and dispersity of MO nanoparticles.156–163 However, the main drawback of this route is the formation of small quantities of products while consuming relatively large volumes of organic solvents.154
The most commonly used approach involves mixing two separate microemulsions containing different reactants necessary for the synthesis of the nanostructures. Nucleation occurs at the edges of micelles when the water phase inside gets saturated with reactants. The growth stage begins at the interface and then shifts into the core of the micelle. It should be noted that the intermicellar exchange limits the growth rate of nanoparticles. Another approach used for the synthesis of nanoparticles is based on the preparation of a single microemulsion and then successive diffusion of chemical compounds into the interior of reverse micelles where the proper reaction occurs. The problem with this procedure is that the composition of the microemulsion is gradually changed by adding solutions of different reactants, which causes significant modification of the micelle properties.154,156–163
The morphology of the obtained nanostructures is determined by three main factors: (i) micellar template, (ii) added anions, and (iii) molecular adsorption. A surfactant–water–oil system can create various self-assembly structures such as spheres, cylinders, interconnected cylinders, and planes termed the lamellar phase, which can transform into oniontype structures. Therefore, many nanoparticles of different shapes could be grown inside these templates.96,101,153,154
Gu et al.159 successfully synthesized 1D mesoporous ceria nanofibres and nanobelts with the use of the reverse micelle method. In an applied procedure, $\mathrm{Ce}_{2}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}$ nanofibres, acting as precursors, were prepared by rapidly pouring the reverse micelles I (Triton $\mathbf{X}{-}100$ , cyclohexane, $n$ -hexyl alcohol, $\mathrm{Ce}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}$ hydrous solution) into the reverse micelles II (Triton $\mathbf{X}{-}100$ , $n$ -hexyl alcohol, cyclohexane, $\mathrm{NH_{4}H C O_{3}}$ hydrous solution). In the next step, the mixture was aged for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ at 30 or $40~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ and then the precipitate was dried and calcined to obtain $\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ nanostructures. Studies have revealed that the aging temperature is a key parameter to control the morphology of ceria. $\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ nanofibres were formed when the aging temperature during synthesis was $30\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.$ . The diameter of the nanofibres was estimated to be $50{-}200\ \mathrm{nm}$ and the length was more than $50~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Performing the aging process at $40~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ resulted in the fabrication of $\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ nanobelts with a length of a few tens of micrometres, a width ranging from 0.5 to $5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and a thickness varying from 20 to $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Kuiry et al.160 described the preparation of $\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ nanorods by self-assembling the crystalline ceria nanoparticles utilizing a double microemulsion procedure. Microemulsion I consisted of cerium nitrate dissolved in the sodium bis(2-ethylhexyl)sulfosuccinate (AOT)-in-toluene solution, whereas microemulsion II was obtained by adding ${\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}_{2}$ to the AOT-in-toluene solution. These microemulsions were mixed and aged for a few weeks. The self-assembly process occurred during aging of the sol after the formation of $\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ nanoparticles, leading to the gradual evolution of ceria nanorods. The authors proposed a mechanism of this process involving the creation of cylindrical supra-aggregates and their subsequent growth by preferential assembly of ceria nanocrystallites along the longitudinal direction. The nanorods achieved with this procedure had a diameter of $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ and a length of $250~\mathrm{nm}$ .
Li et al.161 obtained $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles with controlled morphology and high photoactivity using a microemulsionmediated hydrothermal method. The microemulsion was prepared by introducing Triton-X-100 and water into a mixture of heptane and hexanol. Titanium isopropoxide as the precursor was directly added into the microemulsion. In this procedure, hydrothermal treatment was carried out for $^{13\mathrm{~h~}}$ at different temperatures which had a direct impact on the morphology of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . As the temperature of the hydrothermal treatment increased, the shape of titania particles changed from spherical to rod-like. The results indicated that spherical particles with diameters $6{\cdot}10\ \mathrm{nm}$ grew into rods along their preferred (101) direction confirming that the microemulsion droplet provided a large space for nucleus growth of titania due to a decrease in the interface strength and an increase in the collision efficiency between droplets at high temperature.
Bumajdad et al.162 synthesized zinc oxide nanostructures with various shapes (spherical, needle-like, acicular) using waterin-n-heptane microemulsions (relatively insensitive towards precursor incorporation) stabilized by a mixture of cationic (didodecyldimethylammonium bromide) and non-ionic $\left(\mathrm{Brij}^{\mathrm{\tiny{R}}}35\right)$ surfactants. It was found that the molar ratio of Zn precursor to precipitating agent (NaOH) plays a crucial role in the control of morphology and porosity (Fig. 13). Application of a $1{:}1$ molar ratio led to the formation of nanoparticles with a well-organized spherical shape, having an average diameter of $110~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . At a ratio of 1 : 2 $\mathrm{(Zn:NaOH)}$ , the produced zinc oxide nanoparticles preferred a needle-like shape with an average diameter of $70\mathrm{nm}$ and a length of $1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Using a ratio of 1 : 4 $\left(\mathrm{Zn}:\mathrm{NaOH}\right)$ , the nanoparticles of needle-like morphology changed into an acicular shape with an average diameter of $55\ \mathrm{~nm}$ . ZnO nanoparticles with irregular shapes were formed when the highest concentration of the precipitating agent was used. This morphology change was attributed to the concentration of $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions which affected the nucleation process.
A new modified reverse micelle method was developed to control the morphology of magnetic iron oxide nanocrystals under mild conditions.163 Magnetic nanocubes were prepared by injecting water to the reaction mixture containing iron(II) acetate and oleylamine in xylene solution. To control the morphology of the iron oxide nanocrystals, oleic acid was added as a co-surfactant. Park et al.163 showed that the strong binding affinity of oleylamine to the (100) facet of the magnetite structure caused the construction of nanocubes.163 With decreasing amount of oleylamine in the synthetic procedure, the cubic shape of iron oxide nanocrystals transformed into a spherical shape. Spherical magnetite nanocrystals with different sizes were also synthesized with the use of a mixture of oleic acid and oleylamine. It is well known that oleic acid strongly binds to the surface of oxide nanocrystals. Therefore, all facets of magnetite nanocrystal surface could be stabilized by this carboxylic acid. Reducing the difference in surface energy of the facet brought about the formation of spherical nanocrystals. Their size was modulated by tuning the amount of oleylamine, maintaining other experimental conditions constant.
3.2.5. Two-phase route. A two-phase synthesis involves the mixing of precursors with capping molecules in the reaction medium consisting of water and organic solvents.96,101,164–169 The nucleation and growth stages of MO nanoparticles occur at the interface of two immiscible liquid phases during thermal treatment. The initial nuclei grow to form capping-assisted oxide nanoparticles which are highly dispersed in the nonpolar organic solvent. Then the product is precipitated by adding excess ethanol or methanol and recovered by centrifugation and decantation. In this method, MO nanocrystals are stabilized in the solution by the organic capping layer adsorbed on their surface. In comparison to the single-phase synthetic approaches, the two-phase strategy also leads to obtaining high crystallinity products under relatively mild conditions, thanks to the presence of the aqueous phase, which increases the speed of the growth process. Due to the slow rate of nucleation and growth occurring at the liquid–liquid interface, it is possible to precisely control the particle size and morphology.96,101,164–169
Zhao et al.164 described a two-phase synthesis of $\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanocrystals that were capped with an organic ligand (dodecylamine)
and dissolved in a nonpolar solvent. In this synthetic procedure, manganese(II) stearate was used as a precursor. The morphology and size of the product were controlled by changing reaction conditions (e.g. time, temperature, activation agent concentration). The results have shown that the cubic nanocrystals were fabricated in the early stages of the anisotropic growth. With increasing reaction time, the monomer concentration was gradually reduced and the energetically unfavorable cubes transformed into spheres. The $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles obtained at relatively low temperature $\left(120^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\right)$ had a cubic shape and an average size of $8\ \mathrm{nm}$ , whereas the spheres with a diameter of about $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ were synthesized at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ .
Ji and coworkers165 developed a two-phase approach for the preparation of zirconia nanoparticles with spherical, teardrop, rod and rice shapes. The synthesis was performed by the mixing of zirconium(IV) $n$ -propoxide and oleic acid in the toluene–tert-butylamine–water system and then thermal treatment at a temperature in the range $120-180\mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . It was found that the reaction time, temperature, the nature of the capping agent, and the monomer concentration are key factors for controlling the shape of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ nanoparticles. The change of zirconia morphology was the result of both the martensitic transformation and the diffusion mechanism, which influenced the nucleation and growth, respectively.
Nguyen and ${\bf D0}^{166}$ reported different two-phase methods for the synthesis of rare earth oxides $\left(\mathrm{Sm}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}\right.$ , $\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Er}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ , ${\bf G}{\bf d}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{La}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3},\mathrm{Y}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\big)$ and transition MOs $\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\right.$ , $\mathbf{Cr}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , NiO) with controlled size and shape, through the hydrolysis of corresponding inorganic salts or metal–surfactant complexes in the water–toluene system. The advantages of these approaches are mild reaction conditions, high yields, and easy tunability of the product morphology. Depending on the nature of the metal precursors, the nanoparticles obtained had spherical, cubic, peanut, rod or hexagonal shapes. Moreover, it was observed that when the monomer concentration of cerium salts increased from 0.015 to $0.120\mathrm{~\bf~M~}$ , the shape of $\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ nanoparticles changed from nanocubes to elongated nanocubes, which could be associated with an increase in the chemical potential in bulk solution. In turn, the morphology of $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles transformed from nanocubes to nanospheres with increasing reaction time up to $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ because of the differences in chemical potential between different facets of the nanocrystals.
Nguyen et $a l.^{167}$ proposed a low cost and reproducible preparation of samaria spheres and rods from inexpensive $\mathrm{Sm}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ powders and various capping long-chain alkyl acids with the use of the two-phase hydrothermal method. The shape of nanoparticles was controlled by the reaction temperature while the diameters of rods were controlled by the alkyl chain length of capping agents. The $\mathrm{Sm}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanocrystal synthesis occurred through two mechanisms: Ostwald ripening (nucleation and growth) in the case of spherical nanoparticle generation and the oriented attachment for the formation of rods from nanoparticles.
Pan et al.168 synthesized high-quality nanocrystals of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with narrow size distributions employing a two-phase route performed in an autoclave. Titanium(IV) $n$ -propoxide was applied as a precursor, while oleic acid or stearic acid served as the capping agent. The $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocrystals were nearly spherical with an average diameter of $4.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ .
A rapid approach for producing colloidal $\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ nanocrystals using organic-ligand-assisted supercritical water as a medium was presented by Zhang et al.169 The ceria nanocrystals with uncapped surfaces showed spherical shapes with an average diameter of $7\ \mathrm{nm}$ and they were partly aggregated. Adding decanoic acid (molar ratio to ceria precursor $6{:}1\dot{}$ ) to the reaction system led to obtaining nanocubes with an average size of $6\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The morphology of the nanocrystals was controlled by tuning the interaction between decanoic acid capping molecules and various crystallographic facets of $\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ .
3.2.6. Co-precipitation method. Co-precipitation is a simple and low-cost method for the preparation of MO nanostructures, in which nucleation, growth, coarsening, and/or agglomeration processes occur simultaneously. Due to the high reaction rate it is difficult to precisely control the morphology of the formed MO nanoparticles.21,170,171 Moreover, the application of the co-precipitation method gives less opportunity to obtain a large variety of nanoparticle shapes compared to other commonly used preparation approaches. Most of them have spherical morphology. It is worth emphasizing that a high surface area-to-volume ratio, good mechanical strength, easy coatings and short pathways for diffusion of different species are the main advantages of MO spheres. Ostwald ripening and aggregation strongly affect the size, morphology, and properties of the resulting product. The co-precipitation method has been employed in the synthesis of MOs such as ZnO,172 $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ 174 $\mathbf{MnO2}^{175}\mathbf{\SnO}_{2}$ ,176 $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O},^{177}$ or MgO.178
Farahmandjou and Jurablu172 reported the fabrication of ZnO nanoparticles with the use of zinc nitrate as a precursor and potassium carbonate as a precipitant. It was observed that increasing the annealing temperature up to $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ transformed the morphology of zinc oxide particles from spherical into pyramidal. The diameters of $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanospheres were in the range of $20{-}80~\mathrm{~nm}$ , whereas pyramid-like shaped ZnO nanoparticles had sizes $40{-}100~\mathrm{nm}$ . Spherical zinc oxide nanoparticles with diameters varied from 35 to $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ were also obtained by Ahamed and Kumar.173 However, in this case, sodium hydroxide solution was employed as the precipitating medium in the synthetic protocol.
Wu et $a l.^{174}$ described the synthesis of magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles by low-temperature co-precipitation of $\mathrm{Fe(n)}$ and $\mathrm{Fe(m)}$ chlorides $(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ratio $=0.5{\dot{}}$ in NaOH solution. TEM micrographs showed that single spherical nanoparticles with an average size of $6\mathrm{nm}$ aggregated into tubular clusters.
Chen et al.175 prepared $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ with different crystallographic forms $(\alpha,~\gamma)$ and 1D morphologies via a facile quickprecipitation procedure at a low temperature $(83~^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ without using any templates or surfactants. The particles of manganese dioxide showed needle-, rod- and spindle-like shapes. Their diameters were $20{-}50\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $40{-}100\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and $30\mathrm{-}80\ \mathrm{nm}$ and lengths were $200{-}500\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $100{-}200\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and $70–160\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The formation of unique nanostructures of $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ was dependent on different reaction parameters. The authors revealed that needle- and rod-like products were composed of a few primary nanorods aggregated along the lateral faces.
Tazikeh et al.176 described the fabrication of $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ nanostructures with a spherical morphology using tin chloride, Triton X-100, and ammonia precipitant. The obtained nanoparticles possessed diameters smaller than $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The slow addition of ammonia to the reaction solution under stirring resulted in the formation of many initial nuclei. Moreover, it was noted that with increasing pH from 6 to 9, the particle sizes of tin oxide decreased.
Park et al.177 found that $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanocubes can be easily converted to polycrystalline CuO hollow nanostructures through a sequential dissolution–precipitation process by adding aqueous ammonia solution in air. The morphology of the product was tailored by adjusting the pH value of the reaction solution. It was observed that increasing the $\mathsf{p H}$ of the solution led to the formation of hollow cubes and hollow spheres with numerous needle-like branches on the surface, and urchin-like particles (Fig. 14). Magnesium oxide nanoparticles with spherical granule structures and size ranging from $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $120~\mathrm{{nm}}$ were also successfully fabricated by the co-precipitation method.178
3.2.7. Flame spray pyrolysis. In recent years, the combustion of the sprayed metal salt solution in the flame has become a promising technique for the rapid and scalable synthesis of nanostructural MOs with size ranging from 1 to $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ and extraordinary properties.179 The formation of MO particles involves four sequential stages: (i) precursor spray evaporation/decomposition; (ii) nucleation as a result of supersaturation; (iii) growth by coalescence and sintering; and (iv) particle aggregation and agglomeration. Appropriate selection of metal precursors and solvents with suitable combustion enthalpies, melting/decomposition temperatures and chemical stability plays an important role in the MO particle formation. Nitrates, acetates, acetylacetonates and alkoxides are the most commonly employed precursors in the flame spray pyrolysis process. While many of these metal salts are low-cost and readily available commercially, they do not always yield the homogeneous morphology of resulting MO particles.179
Flame spray pyrolysis allows achieving a wide range of nanostructural MOs, i.e. $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ ,179 ${\bf A l}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ ,179 $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ ,180 $\mathbf{WO}_{3}$ ,181 ${\bf V}_{2}{\bf O}_{5}$ ,182 and ZnO.183 Due to the high temperature (1500– $2200^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ of the synthetic procedure, MO nanostructures exhibit good thermal stability.
Grimm et al.180 synthesized highly dispersed $\gamma\mathrm{-Fe}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ powders with particle sizes down to $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ by combustion of iron pentacarbonyl or iron(III) acetylacetonate solutions in an oxyhydrogen flame. The iron oxide primary particles had a spherical shape but were partly agglomerated. Their structure, size, and morphology were predominantly determined by the nature of the starting solution and the conditions of the process (e.g. temperature and retention time). Spherical nanoparticles have also been obtained in the case of the ${\bf W O}_{3}$ synthesis described by Pokhrel et $a l.^{181}$ Single-crystalline ${\bf W O}_{3}$ nanospheres were fabricated using different tungsten precursors $\mathrm{(wCl_{6}}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaWCl_{4}}$ , $\mathbf{W}(\mathbf{CO})_{6})$ with the combination of highly combustible organic solvents such as benzyl alcohol and tetrahydrofuran. The average diameter of the particles obtained from all the precursors was found to be $\sim10\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The results have shown that the application of different inexpensive and unusual precursors in the flame spray pyrolysis technique opens new opportunities for the production of ultrafine single-crystalline MO nanomaterials.
Sel et al.182 reported the application of flame spray pyrolysis for the preparation of orthorhombic vanadium pentoxide $\left(\mathbf{V}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{5}\right)$ nanoparticles. In the synthetic protocol, vanadium oxotriisopropoxide dissolved in propionic acid was employed as a precursor. It was observed that spherical primary nanoparticles with an average diameter of $20\mathrm{nm}$ formed chain-like aggregate structures having polydisperse size distributions.
Height et al.183 demonstrated direct synthesis of ZnO nanorods with a controlled aspect ratio by flame spray pyrolysis using zinc naphthenate as a precursor and dopant species such as indium acetyl acetonate, stannous 2-ethyl hexanoate and lithium tert-butoxide. It was shown that tin and indium dopants had an influence on the formation of a specific zinc oxide crystal plane. They were incorporated into a certain crystal lattice, thereby disrupting the crystal growth. Nanorod formation was attributed to the higher valency and coordination of indium and tin dopants in comparison to zinc. Lithium, with an equivalent ionic radius to other dopants but lower valency than zinc, does not affect the ZnO morphology. The final rod shape was mainly associated with particle annealing and rearrangement during the flame cooling.
3.2.8. Electrodeposition. Electrochemical methods of synthesis (i.e. electrodeposition – ED, electrophoretic deposition – EPD) can be applied to obtain various MO nanostructures with desired shape and dimensions.48,49,184–192 The reaction occurs at the electrode–electrolyte interface and does not require elevated temperatures. The product is deposited on the electrode surface in the form of a coating or thin film having a specific morphology. The film structure can be controlled by changing the bath composition and parameters of the electrochemical processes. Other advantages of these methods include the high growth rate, low cost, and the possibility of using different types of substrates.48,49,184–192
Lu and Zhou188 described the fabrication of vanadium oxide films by a facile electrochemical deposition method on ITOcoated glass substrates at $+2.0$ V for $3~\mathrm{\min}$ and thermal treatment at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}$ . Vanadium(IV) oxide sulfate was used as a precursor and Triton $\mathbf{X}{-}100$ polymer surfactant with different concentrations was selected as a templating agent to control the morphology of the nanostructures. Nanorods with diameters of about $62\ \mathrm{nm}$ and lengths in the range of $200{\cdot}400\ \mathrm{\nm}$ were synthesized in the presence of $100\mathrm{~mM}$ polymer surfactant. A reduction of Triton X-100 concentration to $50\ \mathrm{mM}$ led to the formation of irregular vanadium oxide nanoparticles having different diameters of $39\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Without using the polymer surfactant after thermal treatment irregularly shaped crystal platelets were fabricated. Lu et al.189 also prepared $\mathbf{V}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{5}$ films by electrodeposition performed at $-0.7{\mathrm{~V~}}$ against the reference electrode for 20, 40, and $60\mathrm{~s~}$ . It was observed that vanadium oxide thin films deposited for $20\mathrm{~s~}$ were relatively smooth and adhered very well onto the substrate. As the deposition time increased to 40 and $60~\mathrm{s}$ , the morphology of the $\mathbf{V}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{5}$ films transformed from smooth into typical sea-island.
Hassan and Hashim48 demonstrated that the time of the process strongly affected the shape and size of the $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanostructures electrochemically deposited on Si(111) using $_{0.1\textbf{M}}$ $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ aqueous solutions at room temperature. Zinc oxide nanostructure exhibited a high-quality flake-like morphology. The authors suggested that during the synthetic procedure $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ in the solution moved toward the cathode and condensed to Zn droplets on the silicon substrate. The Zn droplets reacted with hydroxide to form ZnO. Yang et al.190 synthesized zinc oxide nanotube arrays on ITO-coated glass substrates by a two-step route. The first step involved the electrodeposition of ZnO nanorod arrays in a zinc nitrate aqueous solution. Then the core of the electrodeposited nanorods was selectively etched using potassium hydroxide solution. It was shown that the sizes of zinc oxide nanorods can be controlled by adjusting the electrochemical parameters. In turn, the concentration of alkali, dissolution time and temperature have an impact on the morphology of ZnO nanotube arrays. Upon increasing the KOH concentration, etching time to $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ and temperature to $90~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ zinc oxide nanotubes were completely formed and their inner/outer wall surfaces were quite smooth.
Chou et al.191 prepared a porous $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanostructured thin film by electrochemical deposition on nickel sheets from $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ aqueous solution and then sintering at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The morphology of the products was controlled by adjusting the $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ aqueous solution concentration. Porous $\mathbf{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ films were formed from large nanoflakes with $20{-}30~\mathrm{nm}$ in thickness and several micrometres in length or from small nanoflakes with $30{\mathrm{-}}40\ \mathrm{nm}$ in thickness and less than $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in length. A non-porous film with a smooth surface was obtained when the electrodeposition process was carried out in $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ aqueous solution with the lowest concentration of $0.0125\textbf{M}$ (Fig. 15).
Yuan et al.49 reported the fabrication of hierarchically ordered porous nickel oxide array films by electrodeposition with the use of a monolayer poly(styrene) (PS) sphere template. The framework of porous films consisted of bowls with a diameter of $1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , and the interstices between bowls were interconnected by some NiO nanoflakes. In contrast, the film prepared without the template exhibited a homogeneous dense structure.
Xiang et al.192 characterized $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ film electrodeposited on copper foil through a self-assembled PS sphere template. It was shown that the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ film prepared within $10\ \mathrm{min}$ exhibited an ordered porous structure. Well-ordered and close-packed hemispherical voids were observed in the film after the removal of the PS sphere template. Moreover, the voids in the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ film had the same diameter as the PS spheres (about $800\mathrm{nm}$ ). As the deposition time increased to $20~\mathrm{min}$ , the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ film displayed a similar porous morphology but the diameter of the spherical voids decreased to about $500~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Thus, it could be concluded that both the diameter of the PS spheres and the electrodeposition time had an impact on the geometry of the voids in the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ film.
3.2.9. Biogenic synthesis. In recent years, the biogenic synthesis of MO nanostructures using microorganisms or plant extracts has been of great interest to many scientists from around the world due to its cost-effectiveness, safety, versatility, mild conditions, and high production yield.193,194 Moreover, this approach is environmentally friendly and allows reducing or eliminating the use of toxic reaction precursors and solvents. In the synthetic procedure bacteria, fungi, yeast, and plant extracts act as reducing or stabilizing agents. The size of the nanostructures can be controlled by adjusting the reaction parameters including temperature, time, pH of the solution and the concentration of reactants. The morphology of the nanoparticles can be modulated by a selection of appropriate microorganisms or plant extracts.193,194 Biogenic methods have been developed to prepare many MOs including $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ,195,196 $\mathrm{UO}_{2}$ , ${}^{197}\mathrm{~ZnO,}^{194,198-203}\mathrm{~Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ 204–206 and CuO.207,208
Zhou et al.195 synthesized biomorphic $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ hollow spheres and tubes using two species of bacteria – Streptococcus thermophilus and Lactobacillus bulgaricus as biotemplates naturally covered with different organic functional groups (e.g. carboxyl, ester, amine, hydroxyl groups) which can easily capture cations. The original morphologies of bacteria were retained in the case of titania structures. $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ hollow spheres obtained applying Streptococcus thermophilus had a diameter of $500\mathrm{nm}$ and a shell thickness of about $15~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . It was observed that during calcination at $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ some spheres were broken demonstrating their hollow cavities. A rod-shaped Lactobacillus bulgaricus was a template in the synthesis of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ hollow tubes having a diameter in the range $200{-}300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and length of $800~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The shell thickness was measured to be $30{-}40\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Jha et al.196 reported the preparation of spherical titania particles assisted with Lactobacillus sp. and baker’s yeast (S. cerevisiae). The nanoparticles showed broad size distributions with average diameters of 25 or $12.5~\mathrm{{nm}}$ for $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ synthesized in the presence of bacterium and yeast, respectively. The duration of the reaction strongly affected the size distribution of the nanoparticles.
Suzuki et al.197 described a fabrication of $\mathbf{UO}_{2}$ nanoparticles from uranium in sediments by reduction with Desulfosporosinus spp., a Gram-positive sulphate-reducing microbe. The uranite nanoparticles were spherical and less than $3\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter. They occurred as specific aggregates and particle coatings on the bacterial cell surface.
In recent years, many studies have been focused on the biological methods of ZnO nanostructure fabrication employing plant extracts, bacterial strains or fungi. Ali et al.198 applied Aloe barbadensis Miller leaf extract with high reducing and capping potential for the biogenic synthesis of zinc oxide nanoparticles of different morphology. $\mathrm{znso_{4}}$ was adopted as a precursor in the synthetic procedure. The ZnO nanoparticles with size varying from 8 to $18~\mathrm{{nm}}$ showed spherical, oval and hexagonal shapes. Rajiv et al.199 obtained highly stable, spherical and hexagonal ZnO nanoparticles with an antifungal activity employing Parthenium hysterophorus $L.$ leaf extract. Their morphology was dependent on the concentration of the plant extract. The spherical and hexagonal nanoparticles were not uniform and had an average size of about 27 and 84 nm, respectively. Nagajyothi et al.200 demonstrated an eco-friendly and inexpensive approach for the formation of zinc oxide nanoparticles from Coptidis Rhizoma which is the dried rhizome of Coptis chinensis Franch. It was observed that ZnO nanoparticles with size ranging from 3 to $25~\mathrm{nm}$ were nearly spherical or rod-like shaped. Madan et al.201 reported facile biosynthesis of ZnO nanostructures with diverse shapes performed via low-temperature solution combustion using neem (Azadirachta indica) extract. It was found that the morphology successively evolved from mushroom-like superstructures to bullet, nanobuds, aggregated hexagonal-shaped flakes arranged in the form of cones, bundles and pine-like with increasing plant extract concentration (2, 4, 6, 10, 15, $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ). It was concluded that the shape of the zinc oxide nanostructures can be modulated by tuning the volume of neem extract added to the reaction system during the synthetic procedure. Raliya and Tarafdar202 prepared monodisperse $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanoparticles from zinc nitrate with the use of extracellular secretions of Aspergillus fumigatus TFR-8 fungus. The nanoparticles with size in the range of $\mathrm{1-7~nm}$ were oblate spherical and hexagonal. Tripathi et al.203 developed novel eco-friendly biosynthesis of $z\mathrm{no}$ nanostructures assisted with bacterial strains of Bacillus licheniformis MTCC 9555 at ambient temperature. Zinc oxide nanostructures had a 3D flower-like morphology. The size of nanoflowers varied from $200\mathrm{nm}$ to $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and they were composed of several petals with a width of about $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ and length of $400~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The basic shape of the nanoflowers was rods that agglomerated to form the final material. Venkateswarlu et al.204 presented green synthesis of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$
1D nanorods utilizing Punica Granatum rind extract, a naturally available agricultural by-product, as a reducing agent. The obtained nanorods had a mean diameter of $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ and a length above $200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Other studies of the same team were aimed at the preparation of monodisperse $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ magnetic nanoparticles of spherical shape using plantain peel extract.205 Byrne et al.206 synthesized magnetite nanoparticles by a biogenic method assisted with Geobacter sulfurreducens. It was found that the size of the nanoparticles with spherical and square shapes can be controlled within the range of $10{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ by tuning the total amount of bacteria introduced to the system at the beginning of the experiment. The higher concentration of bacteria led to decreasing nanoparticle diameters.
In 2019, Singh et al.207 reported the environmentally friendly and low-cost green synthesis of CuO nanoparticles employing Psidium guajava leaf extract as a reducing and capping agent, and copper acetate monohydrate as a metal precursor. Monodisperse CuO nanoparticles exhibited spherical morphology and uniform size distributions of $2{\cdot}6\ \mathrm{nm}$ Saravanakumar et al.208 synthesized copper oxide nanoparticles with the use of the cell-free extract of Trichoderma asperellum fungus. The size of spherical CuO nanoparticles evenly agglomerated varied from 10 to $190~\mathrm{{nm}}$ with a mean diameter of $110~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .
3.3. Morphology control of metal oxides by confinement of growth in solid matrices
The morphology of MO nanostructures can be controlled by confining the growth process of the primary particles in inorganic networks acting as a hard template. In this case, the voids of the solid matrix are filled with target precursors which undergo further transformations. The final material is obtained after removing the template.209,210 Therefore, the size and shape of the voids present in the structures of the matrix as well as the type and concentration of the precursor used strongly affect the morphology of the resulting MO nanostructures. The commonly used hard templates for the creation of MO nanostructures are zeolites, polymers, alumina membranes, ordered mesoporous silica and carbons. However, most scientific works consider the use of silica materials as solid matrices due to their well-developed surface areas, high pore volumes and diverse morphologies such as spherical, fibrous, noodle- and rod-like.209,210 This section presents an overview of representative examples employing different template approaches.
Rumplecker et al.211 focused on the synthesis of $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanostructures with the use of 2D hexagonal SBA-15 and 3D cubic KIT-6 silica materials as hard templates. It was found that the ordering and the interconnectivity of cobalt oxide nanowires were strongly determined by the amount of mesoporous silica added into the solution of the $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ precursor during the synthetic procedure. When the loading of the solid matrix increased the nanostructures changed from randomly packed, isolated $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanowires to highly ordered, mesostructured, and fully interconnected networks. Moreover, the application of cubic KIT-6 silica with specific wall thickness and degree of framework interconnectivity as a template allowed modulating the pore size of mesostructured $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ from $3\ \mathrm{nm}$ up to $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ .
Lakshmi et al.212 reported a general preparation of semiconductor MO nanotubes and fibrils via the sol–gel method applying porous alumina and polymeric filter membranes as templates. For $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures, the synthetic procedure involved dipping of an alumina membrane in a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ sol obtained by mixing a solution of titanium isopropoxide with water, ethanol and hydrochloric acid followed by drying, heating at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ and removal of the template. The results have shown that titania tubules were formed when the alumina membrane was immersed in the sol for only ${\textbf{5}}{\textbf{s}}_{:}$ , while solid $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ fibrils were obtained within $60~\mathrm{s}$ . Immersion of template for 25 s led to producing tubules with very thick walls. In all cases, the outside diameter of tubules and fibrils was about $200~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ (the diameter of the pores in the template) and a length of $50\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (the thickness of the alumina membrane). The morphology of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures was also controlled by the temperature of the membrane dipping process. Thin-walled $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ tubules and fibrils were fabricated at $5~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ and $20\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ , respectively.
Similar methods were applied for the synthesis of 1D nanostructures of other MOs such as $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{nO}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{V}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{5}$ , $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ , ZnO, and ${\bf W O}_{3}$ . Limmer et al.213 demonstrated the template-based growth of uniform oxide nanorods $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right.$ ， $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ , ${\bf B a T i O}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Sr}_{2}\mathrm{Nb}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}$ , and $\mathbf{Pb}\big(\mathrm{Zr}_{0.52}\mathrm{Ti}_{0.48}\big)\mathbf{O}_{3}\big)$ , formed by a combination of the sol–gel process and electrophoretic deposition. These nanostructures with a diameter in the range $125{-}200~\mathrm{nm}$ and length of $10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ were arranged parallel to one another over a broad area of the hydrophilic polycarbonate (PC) membrane.
The hard templating method is widely applied to obtain hollow spheres.214 The size of the inner hollow space can be modulated by the selection of an appropriate solid matrix whereas the thickness of the shell by adjusting the concentration of the reactants and changing the hydrophilicity of the template surface. Ren et al.215 described a synthesis of uniform hollow titania nanospheres with an average diameter of approximately $80\ \mathrm{~nm}$ and composed of 7 nm-sized nanoparticles employing quasi-nanosized carbonaceous spheres. Zeng et al.216 also reported a simple hard template strategy for the fabrication of multi-shelled $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ structures with the use of porous polystyrene-divinylbenzene (PS-DVB) spheres as templates. The $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ spheres had smooth surfaces and were connected with each other. It should be noted that the application of the solid matrix does not always limit the final shape of the product to hollow spheres.214
In recent years, a lot of attention has been paid to the application of MOFs both as precursors and self-sacrificial templates in the fabrication of MO nanostructures with diverse compositions and morphologies.217 The advantages of MOFs are the tunability of structural and chemical function, welldeveloped specific surface area, high porosity, good structural integrity, and various physicochemical properties.5,6,8,218,219 When MOFs are subjected to direct annealing in an oxygen atmosphere, the organic linkers decompose creating pores whereas metal ions oxidize to MOs.217
Zhang et al.220 described a simple approach to synthesize $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ polyhedra and plates via the calcination of two metal formate frameworks (MFFs) with different morphologies. It was shown that the obtained $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ samples not only inherited the original shapes of the as-prepared precursors but also were composed of many interconnected nanoparticles. In other studies, Zhao et al.221 revealed that $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanostructures retained the rhombic dodecahedral morphology of the ZIF-63 template after thermal treatment at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ in air.
Wu et al.222 synthesized porous CuO hollow architectures with an octahedral shape by annealing Cu-based MOF $([\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}],$ BTC – benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate) templates. It was found that the morphology of the CuO products can be controlled by changing the thermal treatment conditions of $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\big(\mathrm{BTC}\big)_{2}\right]$ . When the calcination process was performed at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the octahedral shape of the MOF template was well preserved in CuO samples. Moreover, their surface was very rough and contained many holes demonstrating the formation of pores. During calcination at a temperature of $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , some initial octahedra collapsed creating flower-like CuO structures whereas at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ only agglomerates with non-regular shapes were obtained. In 2015, Song et al.223 published a very interesting paper about a green strategy to fabricate CuO superstructures in which octahedral and six-prismatic Cu-MOFs prepared by the hydrothermal method were only immersed and stirred in NaOH solution without further thermal treatment. Novel 3D urchin-like and rod-like CuO superstructures consisted of nanoparticles, nanowires, and nanosheets (Fig. 16). The inner structure of MOFs strongly determined the morphology of hierarchical structures of the prepared CuO.
Zhang et al.224 prepared NiO nano/microstructures employing a facile solid-state thermolysis of a Ni-based MOF precursor obtained via a solvothermal method from 1,4-benzene-dicarboxylic acid (BDC) and $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Nickel oxide samples were composed of porous 2D sheets formed from many uniform nanoparticles. It was observed that the temperature and time of thermolysis had a significant impact on the structure and morphology of the products.
## 4. Morphology control of metal–organic frameworks
Structural designability at the molecular length scale, feasibility of chemical functionalization and accessible high surface area of MOFs are the main reasons that they are still intensively studied materials. The wide variety of components, i.e. metallic cations and organic linkers, introduces immense possibilities of constructing new types of crystalline scaffolds with desired features. It allows applying MOFs in different fields, such as adsorption,225–230 c atalysis,231,232 separation227,233–236 or even chemical sensing237–241 and drug delivery.242–249
Although plenty of reports have described the preparation of MOF-derived materials with a variety of compositions and morphologies, there are fewer syntheses that are focused on the morphology control of MOFs exclusively.250–256 It is difficult to predict in what kind of shape and size the MOF nanomaterial will be obtained, even if the topology of a single MOF unit is predicted, especially after one MOF topology can crystallize in various different structures.257 In addition, the term ‘morphology control’ is usually used in a misleading way when any homogeneous particles with definable forms are synthesized. In fact, ‘morphology control’ should be used for well-thought out strategies, purpose of which precisely leads to beforehand selected shape and size of the product. It is extremely difficult to achieve this kind of control, especially for structures such as aesthetically captivating and extraordinarily complex. The starting point for ‘morphology control’ is the deep understanding of the involved chemical reactions on a molecular level; only when one is able to gain control over the underlying chemistry is it also possible to really control and dictate the material’s morphology. The construction of MOFs usually starts with the strategic selection of precursor materials: inorganic building units (IBUs), which are mostly formed in situ from a metal precursor, and organic building units (OBUs), which are commonly polytopic acids or imidazoles. To coordinate ligands to a metal centre and form the IBU, it is necessary to use substrates that provide easily accessible metallic cations. For that, the most common ion sources used are metal salts (e.g. nitrates, chlorides), as they easily dissolve in solvents. While this straightforward dissolution often favours MOF nucleation and growth, morphology is mainly dictated by the underlying MOF topology. Strategic pathways, such as top-down synthesis and controlled SBU approaches allow controlling these topologies; however, they fail to deliberately obtain complex 3D structures. In contrast, the metal oxide (MO) precursor strategy might seem challenging due to the low solubility of MOs caused by high lattice enthalpies; however, it has substantial advantages that metal salts cannot provide (Table 2).257 Firstly, MOs can serve as substructures and templates for the final MOF, differently from metal salts, which dissolve far too easily due to the comparably high solubility. While aggressive reaction conditions facilitating MO dissolution bear similar initial reaction conditions, careful optimizations of the experimental conditions allow preserving the MO morphology during the MO-to-MOF conversion. Thorough balancing of simultaneous MO dissolution and MOF formation enables taking advantage of the comparatively stable MO shape and morphology with it serving as a self-sacrificial template. Furthermore, many available deposition and patterning techniques grant endless possibilities of diverse 3D MO structures, extending the variety of new MOF morphologies.
## 4.1. Mechanism of MO-to-MOF conversion
MO-to-MOF conversion rates are subjected to MO composition, morphology (shape and size), surface roughness and pHdependent surface charge.257–259 Tuning these MO features enables fabrication of materials with desirable MOF structures, without the need for surface functionalizations like selfassembled monolayers (SAMs).260,261 For example, it was found that the formation of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{(BTC)}_{2}$ from CuO sheets is easily feasible, whereas no HKUST-1 was obtained when CuO microcrystals were used under the same conditions.258 Moreover, choosing suitable solvent(s) in solution-based syntheses is as important for the final MOF morphology as the selection of appropriate MO nanoparticle shape and size. This is exemplified for $z\mathrm{no}$ , with mixtures of octanol and 2-methylimidazole (2-mim or Hmim) not resulting in ZIF-8 formation, whilst ethanol addition amplified MO dissolution and allowed MOF growth.262 To fully use the advantages of MOs, a good understanding of the reaction kinetics as a crucial variable of MO-toMOF conversions is important. Hence, to enable targeted and controllable synthesis relations among competing rates are the key for which the following mechanism dominates: dissolutioncrystallization or template-assisted transition, where the latter results in a controlled MO-to-MOF conversion (Fig. 17).
The rates that govern MO-to-MOF conversion are MO dissolution, metallic cations/ligands diffusion, MOF nucleation, and MOF growth. High MO dissolution rates paired with sufficient diffusion can result in random crystallization within the solution associated with local supersaturations (homogeneous nucleation).259 In this case, particles grow in the solution without the templating effect, working similarly to soluble salts but without accompanying acidic by-products. On the other hand, when the MO dissolution is balanced in a way that MOF nucleation can occur locally around the substrate (heterogeneous nucleation) without too high diffusion rates, the MO can serve as a self-sacrificial template. This shape preserving mechanism can allow the full transformation of the MO substrate into MOF and hence the perpetuation of the original morphology. The progressive metallic cation-release as a result of the MO dissolution causes a concentration gradient of IBUs from the substrate to the solution, allowing the MOF to maintain on the MO and eventually to entirely replace it. However, to obtain full conversion, rates have to be matched perfectly and most often the results of MO-to-MOF reactions are core–shell-like structures explainable by diffusion limitations as a result of a closed MOF shell around the substrates before total transformation. Accordingly, to achieve complete conversion or a desired core–shell structure respectively, for each reaction an understanding of the effect of the different reaction parameters on the system as well as the choice of a suitable MO morphology is required.
It is important to point out that the physicochemical properties of MOFs depend on their nanostructure, which is not observed for bulk materials.244,263 Thus, designing the right route for MOF assembly is a matter of relevance for scaffolding features and application. Reaction conditions and their impact on kinetics vary depending on the chosen method. Regardless, there is a general aspect for so-called structured-directing agents (SDA) including guest molecules, mineralizers, surfactants and swelling agents.264 Whereas solvent-free reactions for MOF self-assembly are feasible, it is challenging to fabricate uniform and distinguished MOF nanomaterials that way.
For most commonly used solvent-based syntheses the main concern is the choice of solvent(s), solvent ratio, precursor concentration ratio and reaction times. All of these factors can be used effectively if the appropriate temperature is applied during the processes, as well as enhancements such as radiation, sonication or electric field.
Up to now only a couple of techniques have been proposed for MO-to-MOF preparation, in which MO takes part precisely as a self-sacrificial template and metal source. It has to be differentiated from MO utilization for MOF assembly, where the role of MO is only being a non-self-sacrificial substructure for MOF assembly, i.e. inactive MO is passively responsible for surface mounted MOF morphology. However metallic cations are not released from the solid minerals and they do not take part in MOF formation directly. A representative example of such a procedure is the heterostructure of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ covered with ZIF-8. The iron oxide precursor was injected in the oil phase as a microreactor, with the MO core being formed in the first step, on top of which in the next step a MOF shell was created, resulting in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\textcircled{\textmd a t e m F}{-}8$ composite structures.265 Another example is introduced by Zhao et al.266 They coated different polymer fibres via the atomic layer deposition (ALD) technique with a thin layer of $\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ , ZnO, or $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ for nucleation of highquality HKUST-1 crystals uniformly on top of the fibre surface coatings. The MOF was prepared in a solvothermal reaction in a solution of metal salt precursors and the respective organic linker, where MO was not used as a self-sacrificial layer. It was shown that the MOF crystal size depends on the coating used, increasing from $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ over $\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . This is explained through a difference in wettability, surface roughness, and isoelectric point of the different ALD coatings. The thus achieved different ‘MOF-PP/ALD’-fibres were then investigated for adsorption of hazardous gases like ${\bf N H}_{3}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ .
In the following we discuss reports in which only MO was used as a self-sacrificial template and metal source for the MOF formation.
## 4.2. ZnO as a metal precursor
One of the first successful MO-to-MOF conversion approaches was reported by Zou et al.267 A zinc wafer was activated by $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to form surface-hydroxide layers, on which a MOF film was subsequently deposited by a hydrothermal reaction. Although this approach was not a direct application of MO, it allowed avoiding the use of salts as a metal source and thus it eliminated the problems associated with acidic anions. In the end, the formation of $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathbf{BTC})_{2}$ films exhibited spike-like uniformity with large scale orders. In the same year Frisˇˇcic´ et al.268 used specifically MOs as precursors due to their low-cost and availability. In the single-step neutralization of MOs with dicarboxylic acids the MOF structure was formed, with water as a sole by-product. Their approach based on $z\mathrm{no}$ and fumaric acid in a liquid-assisted grinding (LAG) reaction led to the formation of materials varied in morphology by using different grinding liquids, i.e. ethanol (3D MOF), mixture of ethanol and water with molar ratio 1:1 (2D MOF) and various amounts of water (1D zigzag or linear polymers). Interestingly, Lin et al.269 presented the formation of ZIF-8 (in their article described as MAF-4) by solvent-free grinding of $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ or $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ . At room temperature, substrates did not react; however, after heating at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ ZIF-8 was obtained. It was possible to shorten the reaction time to $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ by heating the mixture to $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Powder X-ray diffraction indicated that increasing the temperature resulted in sharper and weaker reflexes, which are typical for the guest-free ZIF-8 structure. This method allowed fabrication of high purity ZIF with $100\%$ yield, specific surface area of $1450~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and with relatively homogeneous sub-micrometre crystals. In further reports MOFs with higher surface area such as $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{mim})_{2}$ $(1961~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and new $\mathbf{Co}(\mathbf{mim})_{2}$ $(2070\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ were also prepared. $^{270}\mathrm{Zn}$ and Co imidazoles were obtained in a direct acid–base reaction between $\mathbf{ZnO/CoO/Co(OH)_{2}}$ . Synthesized ZIF network topologies revealed dependence on the presence of small amounts of co-solvents such as 1-butanol, pyridine or 4-methylpyridine, as well as on the heating rate. Addition of a third species can lower the topological density leading to open-framework compounds. In the case of temperature, the increase enhances ligand deprotonation, which directly leads to homoleptic complex formation. Heating rate, however, if kept slow provides higher crystallinity of $z\mathrm{n-}$ and Co-derived MOFs, as well as larger size of crystallites.
Stassen et al.260 also focused on ZnO with the aim of the transformation of the resulting two- and 3D MO patterns to the ZIF-8 pattern by a convenient two-step procedure via sputtering or electrochemical growth, respectively. It was conducted through a green solvent-free reaction of $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ precursor films and patterns with a melted organic linker (Hmim). Variation of film thickness and density allowed the control of the ZIF-8 crystal size due to the change of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ supply. In a follow up work uniform $z\mathrm{no}$ thin films of precisely controlled thickness were deposited using ALD technology, both on a flat substrate and high-aspect-ratio features, which then have been transformed into ZIF-8 using chemical vapor deposition (CVD), providing the linker as a vapor instead of a solution.271 Different from the CVD method, solvothermal transformations need precise matching of MO dissolution and MOF crystallization rates, as slight local differences in dissolution rate, coupled with fast diffusion of the released metal ions can lead to the formation of rough films with many defects. Cross-sectional analysis via TEM allowed the study of the MO-to-MOF conversion efficiency: complete transformation could be observed for precursor films of less than $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ thickness. Thicker films do not show full transformation because of the diffusion limitations of the organic linker to the oxide and/or of zinc to the growing crystal interface. However, the expansion of around $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ film thickness is expected due to the fabrication of highly porous ZIF-8 from the dense ZnO. The thickness increase indicated linkage accordingly to the theoretical value based on the ratio of the crystallographic volume occupied per mol of zinc in MO and MOF.
To show the general applicability of the MOF-CVD approach, ZIF-61 (imidazole), ZIF-72 (4,5-dichloroimidazole), and the Co-based ZIF-67 (using CoO films and Hmim) were deposited under similar experimental conditions, as well as $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})\cdot$ dicarboxylates using copper hydroxide films as precursor films and fumaric acid and BDC as organic linkers.272 A different approach for obtaining ZIF-8 membranes was suggested by Li et al.273 from $\mathrm{Zn(OH)}_{2}$ nanostrands. It is liquid-based synthesis in which hydroxide and Hmim are left in ethanol and water solution (1 : 4 by volume) at room temperature for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . To achieve well-defined and intergrown morphology sodium formate was added. It resulted in a uniform layer of rhombic ZIF crystals. Increasing the alcohol volume in the water:ethanol ratio led to smaller and more loosely packed ZIF-8 crystals. However, using mainly water as a solvent caused formation of more free-standing crystals in the solution, instead on the surface. $^{274,275}$ Out of $900~\mathrm{{nm}}$ thick precursors $800~\mathrm{{nm}}$ of ZIF-8 was formed. In the case of membrane syntheses often diffusion limitation occurs, thus the reaction continues to the point of achieving compact structures, which will impede metallic cations and organic linker diffusion.
Zhan et al.276 used $z\mathrm{no}$ rods to build semiconductor@MOF core–shell heterostructures via a self-template synthetic strategy in a solvothermal reaction, whereby the MO templates themselves provided metal ions initiating ZIF-8 growth without the requirement of any surface modifications. The growth process of the ZIF-shell involved firstly growth of ZIF-8 nanoparticles on the MO’s surface, which then formed a continuous layer during prolonged reaction time. Similar to Stassen et al.271 a saturation of ZIF-8 growth due to diffusion limitations could be observed. The ZIF-8 shell was found to be very compact without any pinholes and no free-standing ZIF-8 nanoparticles were found at any reaction time under optimized reaction conditions. Changing of the reaction temperature and solvent composition led to huge variations in the quality of the MOF-shell and amount of free-standing ZIF-8 nanoparticles, as the balance between dissolution and coordination rate was disturbed. It has to be noted that a solvent mixture of DMF and water is crucial because Hmim works both as an etching agent to dissolve $z\mathrm{no}$ providing $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions and as a ligand for the ZIF-8 formation. In pure DMF the etching ability of the ligand is too weakened for ZIF-8 formation to occur, whereas in pure water the dissolution rate dominates, and thus free-standing nanoparticles of dense MOFs are the dominant species.
Khaletskaya et al.261 used ALD and magnetron sputtering for localized deposition of MO thin films, which were then converted into thin MOF membranes. The resulting thin films were analysed in cross-sectional view (prepared by focused ion beam [FIB] milling) using TEM, scanning transmission electron microscopy-electron energy loss spectroscopy (STEM-EELS) and energy-dispersive X-ray elemental mapping (STEM-EDX). The film fabrication approach was then extended to amorphous $\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ layers pre-deposited by ALD, which were converted into a dense homogeneous $\mathrm{[Al(OH)(1,4\mathrm{-NDC)}]}_{n}$ film. ZnO particles (as well as functionalized $z\mathrm{nO}$ particles) can also be used as heterogeneous nucleation agents for the rapid growth of a number of different MOFs, such as MOF-5 (terephthalic acid), IRMOF-3 (2-aminoterephthalic acid), IRMOF-8 (2,6-naphthalenedicarboxylic acid) and IRMOF-10 $^{(4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyl dicarboxylic acid) (Fig. 18). In a solution of dissolved metal salt and organic linker, ZnO particles of varying size (micro- and nanoparticles) were immersed leading to different MOF types.277
It was shown that the use of nano-sized nucleation agents allowed a decrease in nucleation time, as well as the temperature required for the MOF synthesis.277 Further, it was demonstrated that the ZnO seed-induced rapid MOF formation is not simply driven by an increase in the pH (which facilitates ligand deprotonation) of the bulk reaction solution. In fact, increasing the number of seeds resulted in faster nucleation and an increased number of MOF crystals but decreased in crystal size were observed. Using paper support and immersing one side in a solution of $z\mathrm{no}$ nanoparticles and the other side in a solution of dissolved MOF precursors paired with a lateral flow of the solutions induced by capillary forces led to MOF nucleation at the contact interface. Further, ZnO seeds were used to precisely control the position of MOF crystal formation within microchannels (Fig. 19).
Meckler et al.278 used the ZnO-to-ZIF conversion to fabricate a layered composite of sub-micron-thick ZIF-7 and ZIF-8 films on polymers of intrinsic microporosity (PIMs). It was found that parameters like temperature, ligand concentration and reaction time failed to give control over film growth and quality; however, changes in solvent composition enabled significant control. Reaction mixtures of DMF-to-water with up to $50~\mathrm{vol\%}$ water yielded relatively smooth and continuous ZIF-8 films, suggesting the formation of many ZIF-8 nuclei and comparatively slow crystal growth. At water content beyond $50~\mathrm{vol\%}$ , significantly less ZnO was visible in cross-sectional SEM images and the ZIF-8 grain size increased, resulting in faceted, isolated crystals adhered to the polymer surface instead of a continuous film. Uninterrupted, nearly pure ZIF-8 coatings could be obtained from $15\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick ZnO films with $50\ \mathrm{vol\%}$ water reaction mixtures. Variant preparation of substructures for ZIF-8 was suggested by Al-Kutubi et al.279 They synthesized ZIF-8 thin films on top of an electrochemically grown $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ rod array. They were fabricated through the deposition of the linker on top of the rod array, with subsequent solvent evaporation to form a thin uniform linker film. Heating the sample in an oven resulted in the formation of a ZIF-8 film on the ZnO arrays. Liu et al.280 prepared a $\mathrm{ZIF{-}8{-}Z n A l{-}N O_{3}}$ layered double hydroxide (LDH) composite membrane by partial conversion of the $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{l}\mathbf{-N}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ LDH membrane in a one-step reaction for enhanced $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}/\mathrm{{CH}_{4}}$ separation. Zhang and coworkers281 converted ZnO to ZIF-8 as precursors/sacrificial templates to construct Zn-based nanoarchitecture electrode materials for high energy storage lithiumion batteries. ZIF-8 was thereby transformed into an amorphous carbon framework and $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ quantum dots in a thermal decomposition process. Del Rio et al.282 used a sacrificial ZnO coating which is transformed into ZIF-8 to create polystyrene-ZnO-ZIF-8 core-double-shell particles with an intermediate shell of residual ZnO. Increasing the reaction time and temperature did not show any influence on $z\mathrm{no}$ conversion into ZIF-8, suggesting interrupted growth due to the diffusion limitations after a certain point. Maserati and coworkers252 studied the formation of M-MOF-74 series $\left(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\right)$ , $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g} $ , Mn, Co, Ni, Zn) using different bivalent MOs as precursors in a solvothermal reaction, following a dissolution-crystallization mechanism, with the MO dissolution being the rate-limiting factor.258 The use of MOs as precursors allowed them to reduce reaction times by several orders of magnitude, achieving MOF formation in a matter of minutes.
Sun et al.283 reported the formation of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}$ -derived MOF shells: ZIF-8 and ZIF-71 on ZnO particles as cores, reacting with Hmim and 4,5-dichloroimidazole, respectively. To prevent aggregation of minerals their surfaces were modified with PVP, which additionally promoted retention of the released metallic cations near the template. Three types of colloidal ZnO particles with different sizes were fabricated: $95~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ , $230~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ and ${320}~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Synthesized MOF product from $95~\mathrm{nm}~\mathrm{ZnO}$ showed a rhombic–dodecahedral shape with $270~\mathrm{{nm}}$ diameter. TEM and TGA revealed that ZnO remains inside the structure, with $12.8\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ of non-converted oxide. It was caused by the small pore apertures present in ZIF, resulting in the above-mentioned $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ diffusion limitations. The sizes of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{ZnO}\ @\mathbf{ZIF}$ core–shell particles were dependent on the template size, diffusion and nucleation/growth rates, which were mainly influenced by the ratio of water/DMF. Water enhances ZnO dissolution, whereas DMF reduces it. Both, however, if used separately cause heterogeneous nucleation of MOF nanoislands on the mineral core. The solvent effect was investigated manifesting hybrid core–shell particles in aqueous solution. Changing the thickness of the ZIF-8 shell was possible with DMF addition. Treatment of reactants with only DMF led to the fabrication of partially enshrouded structures, with one or several MOF domains on the mineral template. Furthermore, limited $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ diffusion slower than nucleation and growth ended up in surrounding the core with a thin ZIF layer and thereby further thickening of the shell was terminated. Size-dependent ZIF formation is linked to the nucleation energy requirements. It was suggested that lower energy is needed for the growth of ZIF-8 on the oxide surface than its nucleation. Thus smaller ZnO particles form fewer nucleation sites, ending up with more homogeneous shells. In consequence, if the ZnO precursor particle is larger, longer distances have to be overcome between nucleated MOF crystallites and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ from MOF-free sites. Therefore, it is preferable to form more nuclei on the larger core, leading to intergrown crystal domains. Successful approach of combining MOF from MO with different types of material was introduced by Mazaj et al.284 They hybridized MOF-5 and HKUST-1 with polyHIPE by using ZnO and CuO as precursors, respectively. MO nanoparticles embedded into polymer walls formed a nanocomposite, which was subjected to organic ligand solution under solvothermal conditions. TGA and XRD measurements confirmed nearly entire MO-to-MOF conversion into cubic MOF-5 and octahedral HKUST-1. Applying porous polymers without acidic anions stabilized final materials, by avoiding direct exposure of the formed MOF crystals to monomeric solution.
There are several proposals of a mechanochemical top-down approach for ZIF-8 synthesis from ZnO grinding with Hmim. Reduction or total absence of solvents unlocked novel prospects for prominent composites with MOF. Yue et al.285 synthesized both ZIF-8 nanoparticles and ZIF-8 thin films from the corresponding ZnO nanoscopic precursors through solidstate reactions without the involvement of conventional dissolution processes. According to the authors, this transformation process can be understood as an anion-exchange process, involving the displacement of the lattice oxide anions with imidazolate anions. The dependence of the phase purity on the molar ratios of Hmim to ZnO was shown, e.g. producing phase pure ZIF-8 nanoparticles using a molar ratio of 5 : 2 (Hmim to ZnO).286 Building upon this work, Yue et al.287 prepared a multi-walled carbon nanotube/zeolitic ZIF-8 composite by a template free ‘brick and mortar’ method: ZnO nanoparticles were adsorbed onto the surface of the tubes and then reacted with the organic linker. Strong interactions between the linker and carbon surfaces led to the nucleation and growth of the ZIF-8 crystal solely on the tube’s surface. ZnO precursor nanoparticles with an average size of $35\ \mathrm{~nm}$ and no ZnO signal was observed in XRD measurements after the reaction. Involving new materials in milling during the MO-to-MOF transition was suggested by Li et al.288 in 2019. They introduced mechanochemistry-assisted solvent-free synthesis involving incorporation of Pd nanoparticles into the MOF structure for catalytic purposes. Using MO/hydroxide without solvents is highly preferable for bulk-synthetic industrial routes. Although it seems efficient it is often difficult to synthesize MOF nanomaterials distinguished in morphology via top-down strategies.
## 4.3. ${\bf A l_{2}O_{3}}$ as a metal precursor
Besides $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ another often applied MO for MOFs as a substructure and a source of metallic cations is alumina $\left(\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}\right)$ . Reboul et al.289 used $\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ in the form of a 2D honeycomb pattern and a 3D inverse opal structure as a metal reservoir and mesoscale SDA for construction of a mesoscopic MOF architecture (namely, coordination replication). $\mathrm{[Al(OH)(1,4\mathrm{-NDC)}]}_{n}$ was formed in a microwave-assisted reaction in an aqueous solution of the organic linker; MIL-100(Al) in a solution of $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{BTC}$ ; MIL-53(Al) in a solution of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{BDC}$ . In 2013 Mao et al.290 presented synthesis of oriented 3D MIL-110 rod arrays on a porous anodic aluminum oxide membrane (AAO) in the company of the hetero-metal hydroxide strands, which in this case were made of $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ . The hexagonal column structure of the MOF was maintained when the $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ strand layer was utilized; the absence of such a layer led to only sheet-like products. Inductive coupled plasma emission spectrometry (ICP-AES) indicated remaining copper ions in solution after the procedure; thus, in the end pure MIL-110 was formed. Also $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{OH})_{2}$ and $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{OH})_{2}$ were tested as SDAs with the successful outcome. Nevertheless, the density of the MIL-110 rod arrays was highly dependent on the kinds of metal hydroxides used. Most dense were rods after $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ application, less dense after $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{OH})_{2}$ and the least after $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ . Another morphology impactful parameter was the time of the reaction. Proceeding the synthesis for $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ led to irregular particle layers with Cu. Continuing the reaction to about $^{6-9\mathrm{~h~}}$ resulted in dissolving copper-related structures, whilst thick and straight MIL-110 fibres with shrubbery-like morphology were obtained. After $24\mathrm{~h~}$ well-oriented rod arrays of the MOF were formed. Selecting an appropriate solvent was equally important for the material shape and size. For example, using octanol led to formation of sheet-like Cu-BTC belts, whereas a mixture of DMF and ethanol ended up in small, loosely packed HKUST-1 crystals. Thus, it was concluded that using only water promoted MIL-110 nucleation and further kept the material stable without the formation of different MOF impurities, in spite of the presence of other cations than ${\mathbb{A}}^{1^{3+}}$ (e.g. $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ .291
Nakahama et al.292 introduced alumina as a metal source and SDA for fibrous macroscopic architectures of $\operatorname{Al}(\operatorname{OH})(\operatorname{L}).$ where L is 1,4-naphthalene dicarboxylate (NDC) or BDC. The synthesis was performed in aqueous solution at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and for only 1 min because of the acceleration effect of microwave irradiation. Morphology control was realized by changing the $\mathsf{p H}$ of the mixture. Low $\mathrm{pH}2.1$ highly increased dissolution of the mineral, resulting in small fragmented MOF fibres. At pH 4.0, needle-like ‘rose thorns’ crystals were formed. Slightly higher $\mathsf{p H}\ 4.3$ led to thickening of needles to a fibrous morphology, whereas rectangular crystals of fibres formed at $\mathrm{pH}5.4$ . Fabricated Al-derived MOF structures were thicker than the $\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ template due to the lower density and thus bigger volume. Furthermore, the structural deformation phenomenon was noticed when the reaction was continued for $^{72\mathrm{h}}$ at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Block-type crystals of Al(OH)(BDC) transformed from closedform to open-form, which is usually observed only for bulk crystals.293
Bechelany et al.294 made use of the so-called ‘reactive seeding’ approach, by converting a MO thin film $\mathbf{(}\mathbf{ZnO}/\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3},$ deposited uniformly on polymer fibres using ALD) to a MOF by a solvothermal reaction (using both conventional and microwaveassisted heating) with a simple solution of the organic linker with different concentrations. ZnO coatings were converted into ZIF-8 in a methanolic solution of Hmim with conversion rates of up to $86\%$ for microwave-assisted heating and up to $96\%$ using conventional heating; $\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ coatings were converted into $\mathbf{MIL}{-}53{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ in an aqueous solution of 2-aminoterephthalic acid with conversion rates of up to $66\%$ for microwave-assisted heating and up to $94\%$ using conventional heating. Microwave-assisted reactions did not exhibit any influence on the crystal morphology or MO conversion regardless of Hmim concentration or the reaction time. Slight influence occurred when different 2-aminoterephthalic acid concentrations were used. Reactions using conventional heating led to non-uniform crystal growth and fibre coverage and showed a high dependence on both, linker concentration and reaction time, which resulted in the increased crystal size and conversion rate.
Li et al.295 utilized three different aluminium compounds, $\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ , $\mathbf{Al(OH)}_{3}$ and boehmite, as stable metal sources for MIL53(Al) formation via hydrothermal synthesis. In comparison to the traditional synthesis with aluminium salt, mineral sources proved sufficiency with higher yields, even if the molar ratio of metal source/organic linkers was lower. Additionally, very common pH decrease caused by proton release from $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{BDC}$ ligands and acidic anions from salt was eschewed. Furthermore, pH remained constant or even slightly basic due to the acid-based neutralization. Each substructure varied in shape and size, thus MOF morphology was different. After the synthesis, a ball-like $\mathbf{Al(OH)}_{3}$ cluster with a size above $100~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ transformed into MIL-53(Al) whose size was less than $5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . A lamellar sheet of boehmite was converted to a rod-like MOF. At the early stage of conversion, $\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ blocks were covered with a MOF structure, but after $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ when the source material depleted, only crystal rods of MIL-53(Al) were noticeable in SEM images. Interestingly aluminaderived MOF indicated the highest specific surface area compared to the other substructures. Thus, it could be potentially a better candidate for industrial synthesis when porosity and yield are considered.
## 4.4. Cu-based sources
An unconventional proposition of preparation of the most known copper based MOF, HKUST-1, was presented by Guo et al.296 They prepared an HKUST-1 membrane out of Cu nets (thread diameter and void size were both $20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The primarily metallic net was crystallized from $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2},$ and then oxidized to CuO before hydrothermal synthesis. It was found that the use of CuO was crucial for the continuous $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ dissociation and homogeneous HKUST-1 crystallization. The SEM image indicated arranging of a defect-free structure of octahedral crystals $\left(5{-}10~\upmu\mathrm{m}\right)$ on the net, which formed a $60~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ thick membrane. It led to the conclusion that a transfer of MO-to-MOF preserving morphology is also possible in the case of copper-derivatives.296,297 Majano and Pe´rerez-Ramı´rez298 synthesized HKUST-1 through a room temperature procedure in $5\mathrm{{min}}$ . The conversion rate of the hydroxide after such a short period of time was $82\%$ , whereas full conversion took place after $^\textrm{\scriptsize5h}$ . Both MOF crystal size and growth were dependent on the initial $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ crystallite size $(800\ \mathrm{nm}-1\ \upmu\mathrm{m})$ for both reaction times. However, morphology changed completely from layered elongated $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ crystals to the MOF octahedra. It is worth mentioning that the whole procedure was carried out in an aqueous–ethanolic solution, without DMF which impedes crystallization kinetics. Okada et al.299 presented HKUST-1 synthesis from $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ tubes. The high rate of the above-mentioned processes aggravates control of $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{BTC})_{2}}$ size. After 1 s MOF with $<200\ \mathrm{nm}$ average dimension was formed. At 25 s the first octahedral crystals appeared. After 1 min hydroxide tubes became shorter and $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{BTC})_{2}}$ crystals became larger in size with a rougher surface. At 300 s crystals with $200{-}400~\mathrm{nm}$ diameter were detectable which kept growing for a total of $1200\mathrm{~s~}$ (Fig. 20). After that no changes were observed.
Another preparation of well-established size of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{(BTC)}_{2}$ was presented by Takahashi’s group. MOF crystals were prepared by conversion of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ belts previously coated on a gold electrode.300 The ratio of ethanol:water has a high impact on $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{(BTC)}_{2}$ crystal size due to co-solvent polarity (dielectric constant), which affects ligand deprotonation. Higher hydroxide conversion of MOFs and larger octahedral crystals from several hundreds to more than $3\upmu\mathrm{m}$ were obtained in $2:5$ ratio (ethanol : water). In a more ethanolic mixture ( $5:2$ ethanol : water) smaller crystals were formed. This observation implies that crystallization is dominated specifically by Ostwald ripening. More polar solvents like water (dielectric constant – 80.1) increase the acidity of a mixture due to the enhanced proton release from organic linkers. Less polar ethanol (dielectric constant – 24.5) limits deprotonation, which further leads to a decrease in $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ dissolution rate and thus a shorter diameter of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{(BTC)}_{2}$ .
In 2017 Falcaro et al.301 prepared centimetre-scale, aligned 2D and 3D MOF films directly from a $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ substrate. Following a heteroepitaxial growth mechanism, the synthesis was facilitated by precise matching of the structure metrics of the organic linker and the lattice parameters of the metal hydroxide substrate. MOF crystals being oriented in one direction, the out-of-plane one, have been previously synthesized using liquid phase epitaxy; however, MOF crystals being orientated in all three directions have not been demonstrated. Orientation along all three crystallographic axes could be achieved by conversion of a crystalline metal hydroxide surface.
Zhan and $\mathrm{Zeng^{302}}$ proposed applying $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as a metal source for HKUST-1 formation in a solution based synthesis with co-solvents of DMF/ethanol, which indicated a nanoscale ‘domain growth’ mechanism. After MO dissolution the starting solution became heterogeneous in metallic cations; however, the presence of oxygen in the medium allowed converting the oxidation state of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ to $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ via the following reaction: $2\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{O}_{2}+8\mathrm{H}^{+}\to4\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ Oxidized copper ions were able to react with the organic linker to form crystallites, which in time aggregated, producing larger MOF domains. Using $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ cubes with uniform size and shape (average edge size of $60\mathrm{nm}\dot{}$ ) led to the formation of 3D networks of nanocrystal MOF domains $\left(\sim20\ \mathrm{\nm}\right)$ . It was stated that for successful MO conversion into MOF networks it is necessary to provide a specific concentration of PVP (e.g. $0.48\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1}\big]$ as a structuredirecting agent. Also the polar co-solvents affected organic ligand dissociation and oxidative dissolution of ions from minerals. Under the optimized reaction temperature and time, it was possible to generate HKUST-1 with hierarchical porosity. Further investigation on the shape and size control demonstrated that ratios of MO/organic linker and solvent/co-solvent are crucial for the crystallization process and thus morphology of the final product. It was found that the required ratio of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ and water/ethanol for fibres was $1:76$ and $1:0.43$ , respectively; for rod-like crystals it was 1 : 38 and
1 : 1.2, respectively. A further decrease of organic ligand amount to $1:19$ ratio led to the growth of thin nanosheets with a rectangular shape.303
## 4.5. Other MOs as metal reservoirs
The concept of MO-derived MOFs was further investigated for only several oxides such as $\mathbf{V}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{5}$ , $\mathbf{MgO}$ , MnO, ${\bf B i}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ , CoO, NiO, and CdO. Vanadium PCPs, due to their porosity and electronic properties, can be applied in separation, sensing, and catalysis. The first approach of vanadium based polycrystalline macrostructures $[\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{OH})\mathrm{NDC}]_{n}$ fabricated from $\mathbf{V}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{5}$ was introduced by Reboul and coworkers.304 PCPs were obtained in a short $10\mathrm{min}$ microwave treatment from a water solution of ${\bf V}_{2}{\bf O}_{5}$ and naphthalene dicarboxylic acid $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{NDC}\right)$ , in the presence of ascorbic acid as a reductant. Ascorbic acid not only changed the oxidation state of vanadium from ${\boldsymbol{\nabla}}^{\mathbf{v}}$ to $\mathbf{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , but also improved the solubility of the mineral.259 A combination of applied radiation and mineral precursor ended up in covering the entire surface of the oxide with nuclei. $\mathbf{V}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{5}$ -derived MOFs showed a rod-like shape and had smaller and more uniform particle size than $\mathrm{vcl}_{3}$ -derived ones.
Wang et al.305 presented a new synthetic strategy for Mg-MOF-74 films from MgO using solvothermal conditions. Magnesium nitrate salts were in situ produced during synthesis, notwithstanding MgO was used as a seeding agent hooked onto the ${\bf A l}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ substructure. MgO released $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ ions, and promoted both nucleation and growth of MOF thin films. The resulting MOF membranes demonstrated no defects in their structure, despite the presence of nitrate ions. The effect of the amount of the mineral precursor on the morphology of MOF was investigated by Maserati and his group in 2017.306 With different ratios of MgO to $\mathrm{\mathbf{H}}_{4}$ dobpdc $[4,4^{\prime}$ -dihydroxy- $(1,1^{\prime}$ -biphenyl)- $^{\cdot3,3^{\prime}\mathrm{.}}$ dicarboxylic acid] they presented two synthetic routes for nanocrystals (16 : 1) or microcrystals (2 : 1). In the case of not fully converted MgO to MOF, acetic acid was used as a mild etchant to separate product from MO. The final morphology of the two products was rod-like. These rods revealed an average crosssection crystallite size of $\sim12\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and a greater surface area than bulk structures. Another interesting approach used seemingly mechanochemically inert MgO in a one step LAG method. Grinding MO with carboxylic acid drug derivatives, such as ibuprofen or salicylic acid in the presence of water led to a complex self-assembly process. It involved coordination to water molecules with subsequent acid–base neutralisation and molecular inclusion. In 2010 Chow et al.307 synthesized a series of diverse MOF networks via the same method by varying the amount of water coordinated to the $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ centre. Similarly to previous structures a bismuth subsalicylate MOF was synthesized by using ion- and liquid-assisted grinding (ILAG). The environmentally friendly method using ${\bf B i}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ as a metal source and coordinating drugs allowed preparation of novel metallodrugs. The volume of solvent had a high impact on the final network, thus hydrated 2D crystal structure varied depending on the water coordinates.308 To reduce the amount of water coordinates and to avoid temperature treatment it is feasible to conduct a LAG procedure also in non-aqueous solution e.g. in DMF.
Such an example was obtained by Wang and coworkers for CdO-derived MOFs.309 Milling CdO with isonicotinic acid (HINA) led to the formation of an interpenetrated diamondoid network of $\mathrm{\big[Cd\big(INA\big)_{2}(O H_{2})\big]}$ DMF. MO-to-MOF conversion efficiency in this case is very small leaving a lot of unreacted CdO.
3D and mesoporous ZIF-67, Co-MOF-74 and Ni-MOF-74 hybrid arrays were successfully synthesized by Cai et al.,310 by using self-sacrificial CoO and NiO arrays on Ni foam with Hmim and 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid as the organic linkers. Firstly a MO precursor on Ni foam was prepared. Next, it was placed in a mixture of water, ethanol and DMF with 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid as the linker. Poor solubility of MOs at times can be still ‘too fast’, thus for the shapepreservation procedure it needs to be lowered. In this case using only water led to too fast dissociation and nucleation in the solution, whereas for morphology control epitaxial growth is required. Hence, the addition of ethanol slowed the reaction down and enabled well-defined MOF formation, meaning a slow rate of dissolution was important for morphology control for this material. ZIF-67 morphology was directed by changing the pristine shape of the mineral, e.g. applying CoO wires led to rod formation, whereas CoO walls formed $\scriptstyle\mathbf{CoO}\ @\mathbf{ZIF-}67$ walls. The presented growth mechanism revealed the phenomenon of shell thickening in spite of complete core surrounding. After $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ of reaction, the size of CoO core/ZIF-67 shell changed from $100/40~\mathrm{{nm}}$ to $53/180~\mathrm{nm}$ . Continuation of the reaction led to core corrosion, but it did not stop further ZIF-shell formation. This means that organic linkers were able to deprotonate themselves and enhance CoO dissolution; on top of that they could easily penetrate ZIF pores and add up to its structure. Interestingly, replacing CoO with $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ did not lead to the formation of ZIF-67 on the oxide $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ .311
The solution-mediated techniques are rather expensive and environmentally unfriendly. These problems prompted Frisˇcˇi´c et al.311,312 to develop an ‘accelerated ageing method’, which imitates geological biomineralization (mineral weathering/ mineral neogenesis). MOF materials are generated from the exposure of minerals (e.g. MgO, MnO, NiO, CoO, CuO, PbO) to organic molecules (oxalate ligands) without solvent. Motillo et al.307 also introduced ‘‘accelerated aging’’ for ZIF-67 and ZIF-8 preparation.313,314 This technique enabled formation of ZIFs from CoO and $z\mathrm{no}$ ; however, it took many days (up to 11 days in some cases) to gain any hybrid structure. Additionally, besides linkers (Hmim or HEtim), salt additives are needed, such as $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}\mathrm{SO_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{KHSO_{4}}$ , $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{Im}\right)_{2}\mathbf{SO}_{4}{\cdot}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{(Hcaf)(HSO_{4})}$ , (Hcaf) $\mathrm{(HSO_{4}){\cdot}H_{2}O}$ or $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BIm}\right)_{2}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})$ , which for the morphology control purposes can be unreasonable, if once stated acidic by-products might occur or no well-defined shape and size of the final material is obtained.
## 5. Controlling morphology of metal– organic framework-derived carbon
Since the pioneering work by Xu and coworkers315 in 2008, MOF-derived carbon (MDC) has been of particular research interest in the past decade.316–324 The characteristic features of MOFs, such as designable porosity and ordered structure with highly developed surface area,6,325 are partially transferred to derived carbons, so that MDCs can have tailored physicochemical properties which are difficult to realize by other porous carbons. MOFs are now considered as ‘‘all-in-one’’ templates/precursors that contain both inorganic and organic constituents which can be pre-selected for preparation of targeted carbon materials.326 Inorganic metal species function as sacrificial porogens, catalysts for in situ graphitization, and active components that are useful for energy storage and heterogeneous catalysis.327–329 Organic ligands serve as mainly carbon and heteroatom sources, as well as pore forming agents. They decompose to gaseous products and/or cross-link to form porous (heteroatom-doped) amorphous carbon. In situ generated gas outflux can sometimes create additional mesopores and even macropores.330–332 The well-defined periodic arrangement of metal nodes and organic motifs in MOFs often leads to a homogeneous distribution of metal or MO nanoparticles in the carbon matrix.320,333 This feature even allows preparation of single atomic metal catalysts and metal-N-C active sites throughout the whole structures, which is useful for various applications.334
MOFs can be further functionalized, either by guest incorporation into the porous MOF hosts or post-synthetic modification of MOFs, to achieve improved MDC material performance.335 For instance, secondary carbon sources (furfuryl alcohol, glucose, phenolic resins) and heteroatom sources (dicyanamide for nitrogen, glyphosine and triarylphosphine for phosphorus, and dimethyl sulfoxide for sulfur) can significantly improve the textural and electronic properties of MDCs, respectively.319 Addition of secondary metal ions and other inorganic precursors enables preparation of diverse metallic and multi-metallic nanocatalysts.320,333 The reaction between MOF and sulfur, selenium, or phosphorus containing chemicals at high temperature leads to the formation of carbon composites with metal chalcogenides and phosphides.336,337 Apart from MOF precursors, the final properties and structures of MDCs can also be controlled by simply varying the pyrolysis conditions such as temperature, ramping rate, retention time, and gas atmosphere.338–341
Another important key advantage of MDCs is controllable particle morphologies ranging from simple 0D, 1D, 2D to complex 3D and hierarchical architectures. Carbon morphology control is essential to understanding the fundamental morphology–performance relationships and therefore could provide new insights for the rational design of carbon materials that are highly desired in real world applications.342 Morphology control also expands the scope of possible applications of carbons to cutting edge biomedical, energy, and environmental technologies.316–321 In this respect, MDC is highly advantageous due to the straightforward pseudomorphic transformation of the parent MOF morphology into MDCs. In addition, complex yet well-defined 3D hierarchical architectures can be realized by MOF deposition on the external templates with predefined structures and morphologies. The pre-synthesized
MOFs can also be processed into desired morphologies through various top-down fabrication techniques (refer to Section 4).
Although some excellent reviews on morphology and size control of MOFs,264,343–345 preparation of MOF-derived materials,252,337,346,347 and applications of $\mathbf{MDCS}^{316-321}$ have been published, a comprehensive overview on controlling morphologies of MDCs is still missing. This MDC section covers the carbonization mechanism, morphology control methods, and future outlook, so that general trends and future direction of MDC can be sketched. We focus on strategies for MDC morphology control, with particular emphasis on ‘‘solid metal oxides as primary architecture directing agents (i.e. reactive external templates)’’ for MDC preparation.
## 5.1. Carbonization mechanism
The main scope of this section is to provide key information on thermal transformation mechanism of MOFs into carbons upon heat treatment under an inert gas atmosphere. For the other metal-based nanomaterials, such as MOF-derived MOs, carbides, chalcogenides, and phosphides, the readers can refer to various reviews in the literature.326,337,348 The factors that affect the physicochemical properties of MDCs will be discussed with particular emphasis on the role of metal ions, organic ligands and pyrolysis conditions.
Investigating thermal stability of MOFs is the first step to understand their thermal transformation mechanisms. Thermal stability and MOF decomposition profiles were traced by thermogravimetric analysis, temperature programmed-mass spectrometry,338,350 and pyrolysis-gas chromatography/mass spectrometry.332 Zhang et al.338 systematically investigated the decomposition of MOF-5 $\left(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}\right)$ for the first time. The guest solvents vaporized, and then MOF-5 started to decompose at temperature $>400~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ . The decomposition products were identified as CO, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , and benzene without any other hydrocarbons. Fig. 21a shows the proposed MOF-5 decomposition mechanism, consisting of (a) $Z\mathrm{n-O}$ bond breaking between the $\mathrm{{zn}_{4}O}$ cluster and carboxylic group, (b) O–C bond breaking of the carboxylic group, and (c) C–C bond breaking between the carboxylic group and benzene ring. MOF-5 decomposed through either $(a+c)$ breaking $(75\%)$ or $b$ breaking $(25\%)$ . Both pathways work cooperatively to form $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , $z\mathrm{no}$ nanoparticles, and benzene-derived carbon species, thereby resulting in ZnO/amorphous carbon composites. Carbothermal reduction of $z\mathrm{no}$ to Zn metals started at $750^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , accompanying the release of CO and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ gas $(\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}+\mathbf{C}\rightarrow\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}+\mathbf{C}\mathbf{O}$ or $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\cdot$ .350 Subsequent heating at higher temperature (e.g. $900~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}_{\mathcal{I}}^{\prime}$ led to vaporization of in situ formed Zn metals. This process is analogous to the chemical activation and produces a large number of micropores in MDCs. The kinds of metal ions and organic ligands significantly influence the coordination strength and thermal stability of MOFs, and therefore their transformation behaviors. For instance, the carbonization process of ZIF-8 – the representative example of robust MOFs with high thermal stability – begins at $>600\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ , releasing methyl side groups followed by nitrogen and vaporized Zn metals at higher temperature. Thus, the framework decomposition and carbonization rate of ZIF were relatively slower than those of carboxylate ligand-based MOFs.339
Two major components of MOFs, metal ions and organic ligands work cooperatively in determining the properties of the final MDCs. Metal species act as sacrificial pore forming agents, catalysts for in situ graphitization, and active components for target applications. During the pyrolysis of MOFs, the metal ions/clusters converted to either MOs or metals. Das et al.349 suggested a standard reduction potential of metal ions as a criterion to predict formation of metal or MOs in the carbon matrix (Fig. 21b). The threshold reduction potential was proposed as $-0.27\mathrm{~V~}$ . The metal ions (e.g. Co, Ni, Cu) with reduction potential $>-0.27\mathrm{~V~}$ form pure metal nanoparticles, while those $<-0.27\mathrm{~V~}$ (e.g. Mg, Al, Mn, Zn, Cr) combine with the oxygen of the organic ligands to form MO nanoparticles during thermolysis under ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $900^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.$ . The Gibbs free energy of oxidation of metals can also help to estimate the temperature needed for carbothermal reduction of specific oxides.351 The Ellingham diagram – widely used in extractive metallurgy – shows the relationship between the formation free energy of oxides versus temperature, so that the stability of oxides and the associated reduction temperature can be derived.351 In situ generated metal nanoparticles play multiple roles in controlling the properties of MDCs. Volatile $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}$ is easily vaporized during carbonization $>900~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ , leaving a number of pores (mostly micropores but even meso- and macropores) and thereby greatly increasing the porosity and specific surface area of MDCs. On the other hand, metallic Co, Ni, and Fe can induce catalytic graphitization, increasing the degree of graphitization of carbon frameworks. The controlled pyrolysis of such metal containing MOFs enabled the formation of oriented-carbon nanotube (CNT) assemblies,328 and porous graphene.352,353 To achieve synergistic effects from each metal component, bimetallic or multi-metallic MOFs were designed and used as precursors for direct preparation of functional MDCs.354,355 The residual metal species either contribute to the formation of additional pores through chemical etching356 or work as active catalysts for specific applications.327
Organic ligands work as porogens as well as carbon and heteroatom sources (e.g. imidazolate and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -functionalized ligands). During pyrolysis under an inert atmosphere, they decomposed to gaseous products and condensed/cross-linked to form (heteroatom-doped) amorphous carbon. This process typically leads to the formation of random mesopores in MDCs, yet can be modified to afford foam-like macroporous structures as well. Kitagawa and coworkers330 reported a ligand-assisted foaming process by using MOFs constructed by $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and ligands with nitro group substituents. During carbonization, the nitro group rapidly decomposed/released a large amount of $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ which acts as a foaming agent to create hierarchically porous carbon networks. Recently, Zhao et al.331 reported a similar foaming effect in Zn-based triazole-rich energetic MOFs. Kim et al.332 utilized labile, thermally less stable aliphatic carboxylate ligands as mesopore-forming agents. Aliphatic ligands were first transformed into organic substances with high boiling point (even higher than $550~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) and then subsequently evaporated to generate numerous mesopores throughout the whole carbon matrix.340,341 In addition, the inherent characteristics of ligands greatly influence the textural properties of the resulting MDCs. Kurungot and coworkers357 directly carbonized various Zn-MOFs with different ligand aromaticities, such as MOF-2, MOF-5, Zn-BTC (Zn-1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid), Zn-NDC (Zn-2,6-naphthalene dicarboxylic acid), Zn-PAA (Zn-1,4-phenylenediacetic acid), and Zn-ADA (Zn-1,3-adamantanediacetic acid) and observed that the surface area of MDCs increases with increasing aromaticity of the MOF precursors.
Several attempts have been made to correlate the properties of parent MOFs with those of daughter MDCs, yet there is no general consensus on such relations. Earlier studies suggested that the porous structures and surface area of MDCs are mostly inherited from MOF precursors.316 For instance, Lim et al.358 carbonized a series of Zn based MOFs with different structures and compositions, and found a linear correlation between the $\mathrm{Zn/C}$ ratio of MOF precursors and the surface area of the resulting MDCs (i.e. the higher the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn/C}}$ ratio, the larger the surface area). However, this finding was difficult to be generalized, because many other factors (metal ions, ligands, bond strength, framework topology, MOF preparation methods) were not considered. Recently, Moon and coworkers326 made a more comprehensive survey of the literature, and found that there are no apparent trends between the surface area of MOFs and MDCs, nor between the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn/C}}$ ratio of MOFs and the surface area of MDCs.
The carbonization mechanisms are highly dependent on the kinds of metal ion–organic ligand pairs and their coordination strength, as well as pyrolysis conditions. The physicochemical properties of daughter MDCs are not only partially inherited from parent MOFs but also greatly affected by the multiple factors as aforementioned. The relationship between the nature of the parent MOF and that of daughter MDC still remains rather controversial. Therefore, it is highly recommended to investigate the underlying thermal transformation mechanisms to prepare MDCs with desired properties (e.g. surface area, pore size, pore volume, degree of graphitization, and heteroatom doping level). The universal trends/rules that govern the thermal transformation process and associated pore formation mechanism are ongoing subjects of research.
## 5.2. Strategies for morphology control of MOF-derived carbon
We categorize the reported MDC literature into the following four general morphology control strategies: (1) MOF itself as a self-template, (2) non-reactive external template method, (3) reactive external template method, and (4) top-down fabrication method (Fig. 22 and Table 3). In the MOF self-template method, the morphologies of parent MOFs are inherited to daughter MDCs without the assistance of other templates or microfabrication techniques. Higher level of morphology control can be achieved by integrating MOFs with external templates with predefined structures and morphologies. This external template method can be further divided into two categories, non-reactive and reactive ones. The former mainly relies on the coordination between the surface functional groups of templates and the metal ions; thus surface modification is usually required to induce preferential MOF deposition on non-reactive templates. The latter uses insoluble solid matters (e.g. MOs) as reactive self-sacrificial templates. The solid MOs function as metal ion reservoirs and specific nucleation sites, which directly react with the solid/liquid/gas phase organic ligands at the interfaces. Finally, the pre-synthesized MOFs are utilized as building blocks for various top-down processing techniques to prepare MOF/MDC with desired morphologies. Based on these four categories, we further sub-divided the MDCs according to their dimensions, i.e. 0D (dot, polyhedra, hollow- and multi-shelled polyhedra), 1D (rods and tubes), 2D (sheets, ribbons, sandwiches), 3D and hierarchical morphologies (arrays, superstructures, hierarchical architectures), and discuss their formation mechanism as well as their thermal transformation process. We place particular emphasis on ‘solid MOs as reactive external templates’, and comprehensively review the available literature on this strategy as a versatile tool to obtain complex yet sophisticated carbon architectures with multiple levels of hierarchies (see also Section 4).
5.2.1. Conformal transformation of MOF self-template. The inherent crystal morphologies of MOFs can be transferred to the derived MDCs, namely, conformal transformation or pseudomorphic transformation. The morphologies of MDCs are inherited from MOFs without the use of additional templates and processing techniques, i.e. the MOF selftemplating method. This approach is simple, straightforward and economical. Despite considerable progress in morphology and size control of MOFs,343 morphologies of MDCs are still rather restricted to a few simple single crystal morphologies such as polyhedra, rods, and sheets.
Zero-dimensional carbons. Direct carbonization of MOFs can produce MDCs with different 0D morphologies such as polyhedra, dots, and hollow and multi-shelled structures. Polyhedral ZIFs are by far the most widely used MOFs for conformal transformation because of their high thermal stability.339,365 Thus, most common morphologies of MDCs are polyhedra (hexahedral, octahedral, and dodecahedral shape). Carbon dots can also be prepared by pyrolysis of small ZIF-8 nanoparticles of $<20~\mathrm{{nm}}$ at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in Ar flow for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ .366
Particular attention has been paid to hollow and multishelled MDCs.367 ZIF-67 is commonly used as a single precursor for direct preparation of hollow graphitic carbons. The formation of highly active Co metal nanocatalysts via controlling the pyrolysis temperature and gas atmosphere is the key to obtain hollow N-doped CNT particles (N-CNTs). Xia et al.368 converted ZIF-67 to hollow N-CNTs through pyrolysis under a reducing $\mathbf{H}_{2}/\mathbf{N}_{2}$ gas atmosphere in a wide temperature range $\left(600-900\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\right)$ . $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ rapidly generated highly active Co metal nanoparticles which catalyzed the growth of N-CNTs. Because of the preferential surface-enrichment of the Co metal, the subsequent catalytic growth direction of N-CNTs is from inside to outside, resulting in the formation of hollow structures. Controlled low temperature pyrolysis of various Co-MOFs near the decomposition temperature of MOFs $(430^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ under Ar flow led to hollow N-CNT assemblies.328 In situ generated reducing gas turned the surface Co ions/clusters to uniformly distributed metallic Co nanoparticles which resulted in similar inside-to-outside growth of N-CNTs.
Heterogeneous contraction during thermal treatment is a simple and efficient strategy for preparation of hollow particles.367 Liu et al.362 synthesized hollow N-doped microporous carbon $\textcircled{2}$ Co-N-doped graphitic carbon $\mathbf{\widetilde{NC}}\mathbf{@Co-NGC})$ nanocages via external rigid-shell-induced outward contraction (Fig. 23a). The difference in thermal stability between ZIF-8 core and ZIF-67 shell (ZIF-8@ZIF-67) precursor is the key factor. The less stable ZIF-67 shell decomposed first to form rigid shells of CoO/carbon around ZIF-8 cores above $500~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ . The strong connection between CoO/carbon and ZIF-8 provided an outward adhesive force to pull the ZIF-8-derived carbon to adhere on the CoO/carbon shell. Following carbonization at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and catalytic CNT growth on Co metals resulted in hollow nanocages with NC@Co-NGC shells (Fig. 23b–e). This strategy is also applicable to the other types of rigid shells, such as silica369 and polymers.370 Recently, multilayer solid ZIFs, prepared by stepwise multiple epitaxial growths on preformed ZIF-seed, were converted to multishell hollow metal $\textcircled{2}\mathbf{NC}$ with precisely controlled shell number and composition.371
One-dimensional carbons. MOFs with intrinsic 1D crystal morphologies were the straightforward choice of precursors for carbon nanorods and nanotubes. However, there are only a few 1D MOFs available, such as M-BTC $\mathbf{\hat{M}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , Zn),372,373 Fe-MIL-88,374 and Al-MOF $\mathrm{\big(Al(OH)(1,4\mathrm{-NDC})\cdot2H_{2}O\big)}$ .329 Interestingly, carbonization and HF etching of Al-MOF led to sponge-like carbon nanorods made up of randomly assembled nanosheets having tunable large surface area and pore volume.329
Anisotropic 1D MOFs can be prepared by a coordination modulation in which certain monodentate modulators (e.g. monocarboxylic acid) competitively interact with organic ligands and by that change the coordination equilibrium and promote/suppress the relative growth rates of MOF-crystal faces.375 Zn-MOF-74 nanorods were synthesized by using salicylic acid as a coordination modulator, and then conformally transformed to 1D carbon nanorods.376 Different from conventional modulators, the solvent itself can function as both a structure directing agent isolating particular MOF polymorph and a crystal modulator controlling the aspect ratio of nanorods (Fig. 23f).363 In an ambient temperature synthesis of $[\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{2}(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C})_{2}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{O}]_{n}$ $\mathbf{(DABCO\A,1,4)}$ -diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane), dimethylformamide (DMF) and methanol induced the selective formation of the kinetic polymorph with a 1D hexagonal rod morphology. Addition of methanol decreased the rate of nucleation and thus led to the formation of larger rod-MOFs. Methanol also suppressed the crystal growth along Zn-DABCO [001] directions by forming hydrogen bonds with the nucleophilic DABCO ligand, thereby inducing the morphology change from 1D hexagonal rods to 2D nanoplatelets. Thus, 1D hexagonal rods with different size and aspect ratio can be easily prepared. The structure formation also has a significant effect on electrochemical applications (refer to Section 6).
Two-dimensional carbons. 2D MOFs are usually prepared by top-down (exfoliation of layered bulk MOFs; kindly refer to Section 5.2.4) or bottom-up approaches (surfactant or coordination modulator-assisted anisotropic crystal growth). Only a few 2D MOFs, such as ZIF-L with 2D leaf-like morphology (a polymorph of ZIF-8)377 and recently developed 2D Zn-hexamine coordination frameworks378 were employed as precursors for N-doped porous carbon nanosheets.
Surfactants and coordination modulators have been widely used to regulate the growth of bulk crystals into anisotropic 2D morphologies. Cao et al.364 reported a surfactant assisted synthesis of 2D porphyrin paddlewheel framework-3 (PPF-3) nanosheets and their derived CoS/N-doped carbon nanosheets (Fig. 23g–i). PVP attached on the surface of MOF, promoted the anisotropic growth of MOFs and finally formation of nanosheets with tunable thickness. Because of relatively low thermal stability and significant framework shrinkage during carbonization, the 2D square-like morphology was only retained when sufficiently thick $\left(c a.\ 40\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ MOF nanosheets were used. Ultrathin PPF- $3<10~\mathrm{{nm}}$ was completely destroyed after carbonization. However, this approach may not be suitable for large-scale synthesis because of the high cost of porphyrin ligands. He and coworkers379 developed a high-yield $(>65\%)$ synthesis of ultrathin MOF/MDC nanosheets (ca. $2.5\:\mathrm{nm}$ ) by using low-cost ligand sources (benzimidazole and sodium acetate). Sodium gluconate modulators restricted the growth of the layered MOFs in the direction perpendicular to the layers. Likewise, Mn-BDC nanosheets were prepared by simply adding monodentate benzoate modulator into the synthesis solution of bulk Mn-BDC cubes.380
Three-dimensional and hierarchical carbon. For preparation of 3D and hierarchical architectures, several novel approaches based on controlling the thermal transformation process and MOF crystallization pathway have been reported in recent years. Xiong and coworkers381 reported the formation of 3D dandelion-like $\mathbf{CoO}(\mathbf{\widehat{a}}\mathbf{C}$ spheres through the pyrolysis of metal– organic layers with 2D framework topologies. Upon thermal annealing, the ligand-bridges decomposed to form 1D chains of $\mathbf{CoO}_{6}$ octahedra which in turn converted to 1D nanowires. Meanwhile, morphology evolution proceeded from flake-like crystals to dandelion-like spheres composed of 1D nanowire arrays through thermally induced recrystallization and selfassembly. However, this approach needs a specific MOF with special 2D topologies and therefore lacks versatility as it is not applicable to other MOFs.
Guest incorporation can change thermal transformation mechanisms and create new synergistic effects that induce dramatic morphological transformation. Guest@MOF has shown great potential as a versatile precursor to achieve hierarchical carbon structures. In 2016, Zhu et al.383 explored this concept for the first time, and developed honeycomb-like porous carbons with embedded $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ nanoparticles. Decomposition of encapsulated thiourea and cobalt chloride within the MOF not only led to the formation of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ nanoparticles but also released a large amount of N, S-containing gases which induced heteroatom doping and expansion of the pores. Smoukov and coworkers384 found that carbonization of molybdate@HKUST-1 yielded natural diatom-like carbons with multi-level hierarchies, (i) outermost polyhedral boundaries ${\bigl(}15\ \upmu\mathrm{m}{\bigr)}$ composed of (ii) webs $\left(0.5{-}1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\right)$ , (iii) fibres inside the diatom $\mathrm{\Delta}^{\circ}100\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter), and (iv) mesopores $\left(20{\mathrm{~nm}}\right)$ . Upon pyrolysis, Cu migrated to the outer surface of carbons due to weak Cu–C interactions, while Mo stayed in the carbon matrix due to strong Mo–C interactions. A small fraction of Cu in proximity to Mo can synergistically catalyze the growth of web- and fibre-like carbon nanostructures, which cannot be achieved by Cu or Mo alone. Thus, the fibres grew only inside of the carbon cages. The surface accumulated Cu could not contribute to fibre formation and left mesopores after acid washing.
Of particular interest in this research area is the development of hierarchical carbons with well-defined 3D superstructures, which can show novel collective properties that are considerably better than the sum of their parts. However, the examples as such are still very rare. Xu and coworkers382 fabricated a spherical superstructure of carbon nanorods with hierarchical pores (Fig. 24a). Under a hydrothermal reaction condition, the presynthesized spherical Zn-MOF-74 $\left(\mathrm{Zn_{2}(C_{8}H_{4}O_{6})}\right)$ nanoparticles were firstly transformed into $\mathrm{Zn(H_{2}O)_{2}(C_{8}H_{4}O_{6})}$ nanorods having different crystal structures, and then self-organized to chestnutlike-superstructures. The use of Zn-MOF-74 (a precursor), water (a solvent), and urea (a crystal modulator) was critical for controlled dissolution and recrystallization to obtain the final 3D superstructures with radially oriented 1D nanorods (Fig. 24b–e). Very recently, Hwang et al.340 reported hierarchically porous 3D flower-like carbon superstructures through direct thermal transformation of bio-inspired MOF mesocrystals (Fig. 24f). The initially formed 2D nanosheet acted as a primary building block that underwent oriented attachment and mesoscale assembly. The secondary nucleation and growth on the preformed layers resulted in the formation of 3D flower-like mesocrystals composed of 2D nanosheets. Nitric acid-mediated kinetics control enabled the morphogenesis from 2D nanosheets to 3D flower-like superstructures with controllable particle size and dimensions (Fig. 24g–j). Finally, the mesocrystals were converted into porous carbon materials.
Challenges and opportunities. The use of MOFs as the sole precursor for MDCs still suffers from the following problems. High temperature thermal transformation often leads to a partial or complete collapse of the original MOF morphology, and is sometimes accompanied by particle agglomerations and loss of ordered pore structure. These drawbacks could be partially overcome by stabilizing MOFs with the well-defined external templates, which will be discussed in the following sections. In this regard, development of thermally stable MOFs with various compositions and structures (other than ZIFs) is highly desired. Furthermore, direct fabrication of MOF with multiple levels of structural hierarchy still remains a great challenge because of the single crystalline nature of MOFs and incomplete understanding of their crystallization mechanism. In a classical crystallization theory, the crystal morphologies likely reflect the replication/amplification of the unit cells. Although the SBU concept has been well established to predict/determine the framework topologies, SBUs cannot be simply considered as growth units because they are oriented in the network in a complicated way.385 Therefore, a further understanding of a standard growth unit of MOFs and associated crystallization mechanism is highly required.
To circumvent this issue, a nonclassical crystallization pathway for morphology control of MOFs has been recently proposed.340,386 Natural mineralization often follows this pathway to create sophisticated 3D mesocrystals via particle-mediated growth and attachment, as observed in various biominerals (e.g. bone, nacre, sea urchins).387,388 Mesocrystal is a kind of superstructure consisting of individual nanoparticles in the crystallographic register with defined order.387,388 Inspired by nature, Schmidt and coworkers386 reported double hydrophilic block copolymer-mediated morphogenesis of MOF mesocrystals with high level of structure control, such as kinetic formation of MOF mesocrystals and their recrystallization to MOF mesocrystals with a thermodynamic structure, as well as hybridization of two distinct MOFs. This approach opens a new avenue for morphogenesis of MOFs and thus possibly MDCs. Along this line, we also believe that in situ hybridization of MOF and other components into single architectures (e.g. MOF–MOF, MOF-polymers)355,389–391 is a promising strategy for morphology control of MDCs, by controlling the
MOF architectures themselves and/or by changing the thermal transformation process.
5.2.2. Non-reactive external templates. In situ growth of MOFs on external templates can mitigate some of the inherent drawbacks of the MOF self-template method. The most obvious advantage of the external template is the straightforward control over the size and morphology of resulting carbon materials. In addition, MDC can remain relatively well-dispersed without significant aggregation because the parent MOFs are anchored and stabilized on the external templates during thermal transformation. External templates either generate internal voids through their removal or contribute as functional components in the final MDC composites. In the case of a non-reactive external template, surface modification is usually required to increase the interaction/affinity with metal ions and therefore to achieve a homogeneous and uniform MOF coating. For example, functionalization with carboxyl/sulfonyl groups or polymers (e.g. PVP) is conducted before MOF deposition.
Zero-dimensional carbons. Typically, carboxylate (or PVP)- terminated poly(styrene) (PS) beads392 were mixed with a solution of metal ions/organic ligands to obtain core–shell PS@MOF microspheres (Fig. 25a). Surface carboxyl groups interacted with metal ions and initiated the growth of MOFs. The resulting $\mathbf{PS}\ @\mathbf{MOF}$ hybrids were subjected to carbonization under an inert gas atmosphere, during which the MOF shell transformed into porous carbon and the PS core decomposed to form hollow interiors. Several PS@ZIF-8,392,396 PS@ZIF-67,397 PS@MIL-101/ZIF67398 were used for the preparation of hollow carbon spheres with or without metal nanoparticles embedded in the carbon shell. Interestingly, fast pyrolysis of PS@ZIF-67 at a rapid heating rate $(>5\mathrm{\partial~^{\circ}C\ m i n^{-1}})$ 1 yielded ‘‘single-holed’’ hollow carbon spheres.397 Thermal decomposition of the PS core generated a strong hydrocarbon gas outflux which created a large single hole in each shell. Zhang et al.398 prepared MIL-101/ZIF-67 composites shells on PS spheres (PS@MIL-101/ZIF-67) by functionalizing the surfaces of both PS spheres and MIL-101(Fe) nanoparticles with PVP. Thus, the preformed secondary MIL-101 could be homogeneously distributed within the dual-MOF nanoshells. The subsequent pyrolysis produced CoFe alloy@N-doped hollow carbon spheres.
One-dimensional carbons. 1D template-directed growth of MOFs and their transformation into 1D carbon nanofibres and hollow nanotubes were demonstrated. Yu and coworkers399 utilized ultrathin tellurium nanowires (TeNWs) as hard templates to induce in situ growth and assembly of ZIF-8. Without surface modification, TeNWs were directly immersed in methanolic $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}/$ 2-methylimidazole (Hmim) solution at ambient temperature, leading to the formation of TeNWs@ZIF-8. The concentrations of TeNWs and reactants were precisely controlled to inhibit solution phase growth of bulk ZIF-8. Porous carbon tubules were obtained after pyrolysis at $1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , during which TeNWs were completely vaporized to form hollow interiors. ZIF-8 derived carbons can also be grown on CNTs.400 For controllable ZIF-8 deposition, CNTs were treated with a mixture of nitric acid and sulfuric acid at $80~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ to generate surface carboxylic groups which serve as nucleation sites for ZIF-8 growth. Subsequent pyrolysis at $1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ yielded N-doped porous carbons/CNTs with controllable large surface area. By using PVP-modified $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanorods as templates, Tian et al.393 synthesized a series of 1D Mo-based carbon nanorods and nanotubes (Fig. 25b). Amide carbonyl groups of PVP can form weak coordination interactions with metal ions in solution and allow uniform ZIF coating on $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanorods. Controlled pyrolysis at different temperatures resulted in either carbothermal reduction to form $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}/\mathbf{C}$ or a reaction with carbon to form $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}$ nanorods. The inner $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ species were dissolved and dispersed in the porous carbon matrix by HCl washing, leading to the formation of hollow $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}/\mathbf{C}$ nanotubes.
Two-dimensional carbons. Scalable synthesis of high quality 2D MOFs has been considered as a difficult task and thus only a few 2D MOFs are available for preparation of MDCs so far. This problem can be largely alleviated by using 2D external templates. Graphene has been most extensively used as a 2D template because of its excellent thermal, electrical, and mechanical properties. As nicely reviewed by Zheng et al.401 and Liu et al.,402 a wide variety of MOF/graphene composites have been prepared to combine the advantages of individual components. ZIF-derived graphene based N-doped porous carbon was prepared by the in situ growth of ZIF-8 on graphene oxide (GO) sheets.403 PVP functionalization facilitated the uniform nucleation of ZIF-8, resulting in the formation of dispersive 2D sandwich-like ZIF-8/GO composites. Without the use of PVP, the surface deposition of ZIF-8 was incomplete and inhomogeneous. Excess Hmim ligand was also necessary for the uniform ZIF growth on GO. Finally, thermal annealing of ZIF-8/GO at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in Ar flow generated N-doped porous carbon sheets.
ZIF-8/GO sandwiches can also be prepared by controlling the addition sequence of the precursors without any surface modification of GO (Fig. 25c–e).394 In the proposed synthetic route, GO dispersion was added to Zn ions/Hmim ligands solution where ZIF clusters were already formed. The cluster readily attached to the GO layer due to a strong interaction between metal ions of cluster and the oxygen functional groups from GO. The uniform deposition of clusters and their confined growth to ZIF nanocrystals on the GO surface allowed formation of ZIF-8/GO sandwiches with ZIF nanocrystals $\left(20{\mathrm{~nm}}\right)$ fully covering the GO. ZIF-derived carbon acted as a spacer to alleviate the significant agglomeration of GO during hightemperature treatment. In contrast, when Zn salts in methanol were firstly added to the GO solution, the Zn ions were located both on GO and in the solution because of insufficient numbers of oxygen groups in GO to capture all the Zn ions. This inhomogeneous nucleation resulted in the mixture of discrete ZIF-8 crystals and ZIF-8/GO composites.
Three-dimensional and hierarchical carbon. 3D carbon arrays, derived from MOF nanowires grown on various conductive substrates (e.g. metal foam/mesh/foil, carbon cloth, and CNT fibres) are the most common examples of external templated materials.404–407 The substrates provide additional functions such as high electronic conductivity, mechanical stability, and flexibility. Such 3D aligned architectures are of particular interest in electrochemical applications, as they can provide large electrochemical surface area which facilitates electrolyte penetration and diffusion of ionic species, and avoid the use of extra polymeric binders or conductive additives.408 In 2014, Qiao and coworkers404 fabricated MOF-derived $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ carbon porous nanowire arrays for the first time. Co-NDC with a layered crystalline structure was directly grown on Cu foil through a hydrothermal reaction at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ , and then converted to $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}/$ /carbon hybrid arrays with a high surface area $(251~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ and large carbon content $\left(52.1~\mathrm{wt\%}\right)$ via pyrolysis at $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. Inspired by this pioneering work, researchers have developed a wide variety of 3D carbon-based arrays by employing different combinations of substrates and MOFs.405–407 Instead of arrays, a surfacemounted MOF (SURMOF), a typical MOF thin film prepared by a layer-by-layer growth process, was employed as a precursor for 3D hierarchical carbon.409 A ZIF-67 thin film was grown on hydroxyl-group-terminated 3D macroporous melamine sponge and then converted to vertically aligned N-doped CNTs via pyrolysis at various temperatures in the range ${700}{-}1000\ {^{\circ}}\mathrm{C}$ .
Preformed MOFs are also promising external templates that enrich the possible particle morphologies of MDCs. Different from typical MOF-on-MOF epitaxial growth, PVP-modified MOFs can be directly integrated with other MOFs via in situ assembly and growth. Guan et al.410 introduced PVP-functionalized MIL-88B (Fe) nanorods into the reactant solution of ZIF-8, and obtained uniform MIL-88B@ZIF-8 particles in which a number of 1D MIL-88B nanorods were co-assembled and encapsulated in ZIF-8 polyhedron (i.e. MOF-in-MOF). This dual-MOF with tailored morphologies and compositions enabled preparation of hierarchically structured $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{}\mathfrak{Q N}$ -CNT assemblies confined in a porous carbon matrix.
Shen et al.411 developed a novel approach toward singlecrystal ordered macroporous ZIF-8 (Fig. 25f). ZIF-8 precursors (Zn salt and Hmim) were filled into 3D ordered PS spheremonoliths, and then soaked in a $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ mixed solvent. ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ induced rapid crystallization of the precursors while $\mathbf{CH}_{3}\mathbf{OH}$ stabilized the precursors and adjusted the balance between nucleation versus growth of ZIF-8. 3D-ordered macroporous (3DOM) ZIF-8 was liberated from PS templates by repeated soaking in tetrahydrofuran. PS templates imposed unique shaping and templating effects which guided the morphological evolution of 3DOM-ZIF-8 into a tetrakaidekahedron, in contrast to typical rhombic dodecahedra. In the following work, Zhou et al.395 reported 3DOM-ZIF-8-derived hierarchically porous N-doped carbons as high performance anode materials in potassium ion batteries (Fig. 25g–j).
Challenges and opportunities. Non-reactive external template methods usually rely on time-consuming and laborious multistep procedures, including preparation of templates (sometimes their bottom-up assembly into specific shape), surface modification, MOF deposition, carbonization, and template removal (if necessary). Another challenge in this process is the uniform MOF deposition with a controlled thickness. Non-reactive external templates lack the interaction sites that can tightly bind the metal ions. Part of adsorbed metal ions can diffuse into the ligand solution to form bulk MOFs. Thus, it is difficult to confine heterogeneous growth on the surface of templates exclusively. To date, only a few MOFs can be directly coated on the templates and their detailed growth mechanism is not fully understood yet. Thus, the exploration of new synthetic approaches for direct construction of MOFs on external templates will be the important subject of future research. In addition, development of new templates with predesigned morphologies and multi-level hierarchical structures (e.g. reactive external templates) is of urgent importance.
5.2.3. Reactive external templates. Pre-shaped, solid state MOs can be transformed into morphology conserved carbons via MOF as intermediate precursors (i.e. reactive external template method or solid-to-MOF-to-carbon pathway).259 Different from typically used solvent-soluble metal salts, the solvent-insoluble solid precursors can perform multiple roles in MOF/carbon synthesis: (i) architecture directing agents (templates): the morphologies of solid precursors are replicated to the derived MOF and the final carbon materials; (ii) cation reservoirs (metal ion source): solid precursors provide metal ions by slowly dissolving themselves with the assistance of solvents and organic ligands under specific conditions, or directly react with organic ligands under solvent-free conditions. Thus, an additional metal ion source is not needed; (iii) specific reaction sites for heterogeneous nucleation: the growth of MOF/carbon is localized/confined on the surface of solid precursors. Tedious surface modification steps typically needed in nonreactive external template methods are no longer required; (iv) functional components enhancing physical/chemical properties of the final carbon products. Typical synthesis procedures consist of mainly three steps: preparation of shaped-MOs, conversion to desired MOFs by reacting with organic ligands, and thermal transformation into carbon or carbon-based composites.
The key advantage of the reactive template method (e.g. MOs as self-sacrificing templates) lies in its ability to create complex morphologies with multiple levels of structural hierarchies. There are a wide variety of well-established approaches for controlling morphologies of MOs from nano- to micro- to macro-scopic length scales (refer to Section 3).209,210,412–417 In addition, the MOs can be directly grown onto various functional substrates and host materials, and pre-installed on specific locations before their transformation into MOFs. These capabilities enable the preparation of patterned MOFs in a spatially controlled manner, which in turn leads to the formation of sophisticated, higher-order carbon architectures such as 3D arrays and multi-dimensional hierarchical assemblies.
Zero-dimensional carbons. Song et al.418 demonstrated a synthesis of 0D hollow Co and N co-doped carbon nanopolyhedra (H-CoNC) by using $z\mathrm{no}$ nanospheres as self-sacrificing templates. Zn/Co-ZIF shell epitaxially grew on the surface of
ZnO nanospheres in a mixed solvent of ${\bf D M F/H_{2}O}$ 1 $\mathbf{\bar{\rho}}_{3:1\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}}$ ratio) at $50~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.$ The $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO}\ @\mathrm{ZIF}$ precursors were converted to H-CoNC after carbonization at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere and HCl washing. For comparison, solid Co and N-codoped carbon nanopolyhedra (S-CoNCs) without a hollow cavity were prepared by pyrolysis of conventional bimetallic ${\bf Z}{\bf n}/{\bf C}{\bf o}–{\bf Z}{\bf I}{\bf F}$ (Fig. 26). It was revealed that the $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ mediated-strategy had several advantages over the template-free conformal transformation. In situ evaporation of the excess Zn species during pyrolysis can (i) etch the carbon shell and enlarge the pores, leading to the formation of a well-developed hierarchical pore system ranging from $9\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $290~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ; (ii) help to retain more Co and N contents in carbon frameworks. In addition, the excess Zn supply could break the tradeoff between Zn and Co contents, which is observed in conventional S-CoNC. Thus, the Co and N contents increased with an increase of the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO/ZIF}$ mass ratio; and (iii) allow more homogeneous ${\bf C o-N}_{x}$ distributions throughout the structures. The XPS depth profile analysis revealed that the chemical distributions of Co and N in H-CoNC remained constant from the outer shell to inner core, whereas a concentration gradient was observed in S-CoNC. As a result, H-CoNC showed excellent materials properties superior to those of S-CoNC; for instance, enlarged hierarchical pores (9–290 vs. $20{-}160~\mathrm{nm}$ ), high Co (0.97 vs. $0.35\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) and nitrogen contents (9.75 vs. $3.24~\mathrm{at\%}$ ), and homogeneously distributed $\mathbf{Co-N_{x}}$ sites. Similarly, Chai et al.419 prepared hollow N-doped carbon materials through multiple transformation steps from amorphous $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTC coordination polymers to ZnO microspheres to $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ to final hollow N-doped carbon spheres. Hollow carbons with $25\ \mathrm{\nm}$ -thin walls composed of highly porous carbon polyhedra were elaborately constructed.
The direct chemical bonding of organic ligands and MOs under ‘solvent-free’ conditions has been developed to prepare carbon coated MOs. Mai and coworkers420 reported $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}/\mathbf{Co}(\mathbf{\overline{{a}}})$ carbon via a thermal solid phase reaction of hollow $\mathrm{CoSn(OH)_{6}}$ cubes and excess 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid. The coordination reaction occurred at the exterior surface of the $\mathrm{CoSn(OH)_{6}}$ cubes at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and the unreacted organic ligands vaporized at $320^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under air. The uniformly coated MOFs were converted to carbon shells after pyrolysis under an Ar atmosphere. Similarly, N-rich carbon wrapped $\mathrm{CoSnO}_{3}$ particles were prepared by heating a mixture of ${\mathrm{CoSnO}}_{3}$ and Hmim in a Teflon-lined autoclave at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ without the solvents.421 Upon heating, the Hmim was melted and impregnated into the ${\bf C o S n O}_{3}$ , and reacted with ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ to form ZIF-67 at both internal and external surfaces of the ${\bf C o S n O}_{3}$ nanoboxes. The original morphologies were well-retained after MOF growth and carbonization at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under an Ar atmosphere. As the amount of Hmim increased, the carbon $(7.5\small{-}24.6~\mathrm{at\%})$ and nitrogen contents $\left(2.4\mathrm{-}7.6\mathrm{\at\%}\right)$ ) increased accordingly. This strategy eliminates the solvent-related synthesis steps (e.g. washing, separation, drying) and associated problems (e.g. uncontrollable MOF growth in the bulk solution), therefore could be an attractive alternative for MOF/carbon coating on various MO substrates.
One-dimensional carbons. 1D N-doped porous carbon nanotubes with embedded core–shell $\mathbf{Co}\ @\mathbf{CoO}_{x}$ nanoparticles $\scriptstyle\left(\mathbf{Co}\ @\mathbf{CoO}_{x}/\mathbf{NCNTS}\right)$ ) were prepared by one step carbonization of bimetallic $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO@\2n/Co-Z}$ IF nanowires at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\mathrm{~2~h~}}$ under a ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere (Fig. 27).422 Here the $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanowires (ZnONWs) acted as templates, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ion sources and specific nucleation sites for bimetallic Zn/Co-ZIF growth. In addition, in situ evaporation of Zn during pyrolysis mitigated the undesired aggregation of the carbon particles and generated hierarchical pore structures as well as tubular morphology. Notably, ZnONWs also played a crucial role in the in situ formation of $\mathbf{Co}\ @\mathbf{CoO}_{x}$ nanoparticles by providing excess oxygen species which directly reacted with metallic Co nanoparticles during one-step pyrolysis. In conventional ZIF pyrolysis, such $\mathbf{Co}\ @\mathbf{CoO}_{x}$ core–shell structures can be prepared only after an additional heat treatment under an oxidizing gas atmosphere.
Other types of metal containing 1D-solid matter have been employed for directing the growth of MOF and carbonaceous materials. Starting from cobalt oxalate microtubes, Ahn et al.423 synthesized Co- and N-doped 1D carbon microtubes composed of hollow nanospheres and CNTs. Due to the presence of oxalate species, the formation of ZIF-67 nanocrystals was largely reduced and was confined in the cobalt oxalate microtubes. The cobalt species selectively reacted with Hmim in an ethanol solution for various conversion times, which greatly affected the final tubular morphologies. As the conversion time increased from 4 to $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the diameter, the surface roughness, and the aspect ratio of carbon microtubes increased. In situ formed Co metallic species during pyrolysis at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ catalyzed the growth of tiny CNTs, and left behind hollow nanospheres after HCl etching.
Chen et al.424 used electrospun poly(acrylonitrile) (PAN)/ cobalt acetate $(\mathbf{Co}(\mathbf{Ac})_{2})$ nanofibres for fabrication of hierarchical CNT/hollow $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ microtubes. The proposed multi-step routes consist of multiple steps, i.e. the surface growth of ZIF-67 nanocrystals on $\mathbf{PAN/Co(Ac)}_{2}$ nanofibres in an ethanolic Hmim solution, dissolution of the $\mathbf{PAN/Co(Ac)}_{2}$ core in DMF, thermal annealing in an Ar $\left(95\%\right)/\mathrm{H}_{2}\big(5\%\big)$ atmosphere at $750~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and calcination in air at $360~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Two-step reduction–oxidation treatments are important for the generation of unique hierarchical structures. $\mathbf{Ar/H}_{2}$ treatment completely reduced the $\mathbf{CoO}_{x}$ species to Co metals which led to the formation of multiwalled CNTs throughout the microtubes. The subsequent mild oxidation treatment converted Co solid nanoparticles into $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ hollow nanoparticles with size of $15{-}30~\mathrm{nm}$ due to the Kirkendall effect. The resulting hierarchical microtubes were composed of hollow $\mathbf{CNT}/\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles with inner diameters of $3.2/10.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively.
In a similar manner, $\mathrm{PAN/Zn(Ac)_{2}/C o(A c)_{2}(\overline{{\Omega}})}$ bimetallic ${\bf Z}{\bf n}/{\bf C}{\bf o}{-\bf Z}{\bf I}{\bf F}$ nanofibres were also prepared to obtain N-doped
1D carbon hollow tubules with tunable porosity and a large fraction of graphitic layers.425 The $\mathrm{PAN/Zn(Ac)_{2}/C o(A c)_{2}}$ core turned into carbon $/\mathrm{ZnO/Zn_{x}C o}$ , which was then etched away via carbothermal reduction and evaporation. In situ TEM study during carbonization confirmed that the formation of ZnO – derived from $\mathbf{Zn}(\mathbf{Ac})_{2}$ – was only localized in the fibre core. This preparation process enabled the selective etching of the carbon core, which led to numerous internal voids. Therefore, the final porous/hollow structures were largely influenced by the amount of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{Ac})_{2}$ in the initial precursor solution. Indeed, the morphology changes from solid fibres to hollow tubules even with a few carbon nanosheet-shells were clearly observed with increasing amount of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{Ac})_{2}$ .
Two-dimensional carbons. A 2D porous ZnO nanosheet was the first choice to demonstrate the preparation of 2D ZIF-8 and carbon materials with hierarchical porosity.427 On the other hand, layered double-metal hydroxide (LDH) and metal hydroxide have great potential as 2D scaffolds and selfsacrificial metal ion sources for in situ MOF growth as well. LDHs inherently possess a plentiful amount of coordinatively unsaturated metal sites on their surface, providing specific anchoring sites for MOF deposition. Indeed, in situ ZIF-67 deposition to CoAl-LDH nanoplatelets was demonstrated by Wei and coworkers.428 In their contribution, however, surface Co ions in CoAl-LDH served as only preferential nucleation sites and an additional Co source was needed for controllable ZIF-67 growth.
The use of LDH as a true reactive external template was demonstrated by Yamauchi and coworkers426 for preparation of 2D honeycomb-like carbon flakes. ZnCo-LDHs with a lateral size/thickness of $1\upmu\mathrm{m}/10\mathrm{nm}$ were dispersed in a mixed solvent of ${\bf D M F/H_{2}O}$ ( $\mathbf{\dot{9}}:1{\mathbf{v}}/{\mathbf{v}}$ ratio), and subsequently transformed into ZnCo-ZIFs through the reaction with Hmim at $50\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . ZIF polyhedra with a diameter of $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ began to grow on the flat surface of ZnCo-LDH after $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , and they formed densely packed flake-like networks after $^\mathrm{~8~h~}$ (Fig. 28a–d). The $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn/Co}}$ molar ratio in parent ZnCo-LDH was comparable to that in daughter ZnCo-ZIF, suggesting the homogeneous reaction between dissolved metal ions and Hmim. 2D-interconnected carbon flakes with abundant honeycomb-like macropores were produced via carbonization at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and acid etching, during which ZnCo-ZIF suffered from inhomogeneous framework shrinkage (Fig. $28\mathrm{e-g}$ . This shrinkage is closely related to tightly packed networks of ZIF polyhedra. The connected edges that intimately adhere with adjacent polyhedra were stable and well-retained during carbonization. In contrast, the exposed single edges without intimate contact with the others were unstable and shrank toward the edge sides, thereby leading to the formation of honeycomb-like macropores. This formation strategy was also applicable to ZnCo-LDH with a different molar ratio and other layered metal hydroxides $(\mathrm{Zn}_{5}(\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2})$ , allowing the preparation of carbon flakes with similar morphologies and honeycomb structures but with different specific surface area $(226-860\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , pore volume $\left(0.75{-}1.45\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ , nitrogen content $\left(1.1{-}12.9~\mathrm{at\%}\right)$ ), and degree of graphitization.
Three-dimensional and hierarchical carbon. The methods to process MOs into complex morphologies and architectures are better established than those of MOFs, and thus could enrich the structure engineering tools for MOF-derived carbonaceous materials. Along this line, Song et al.429 employed pre-shaped ZnO templates with different morphologies (i.e. nanowires (1D), nanoplates (2D), and nanoflowers (3D)) as architecture directing agents for general dimension-controlled synthesis of hollow carbons. A versatile approach to synthesize core–shell $\mathrm{ZnO@ZIF-8@Zn/Co}$ (or Fe)-ZIF was presented. $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ was easily prepared by adding Hmim to a ZnO suspension. However, in the case of bimetallic ${\bf Z n/C o-Z I F}$ shell growth, the sequence of precursor addition plays a crucial role in determining the final crystal morphologies. The simultaneous addition of Co salt and Hmim to the ZnO suspension led to the rapid formation of a $\mathrm{{Co}(H m i m)_{4}}^{2+}$ complex in the bulk solution (pathway 1 in Fig. 29a). The released $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ directly reacted with the $\mathrm{{Co}(H m i m)_{4}}^{2+}$ and Hmim to form $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Zn}_{1-x}\mathrm{(Hmim)}_{4}^{2+}$ , rather than a $\mathrm{Zn(Hmim)_{4}}^{2+}$ complex. As a result, ${\bf Z n/C o-Z I F}$ nucleated either in bulk solution or on the rugged surface of $z\mathrm{no}$ . With increasing reaction time, ${\bf Z}{\bf n}/{\bf C o}{-\bf Z}{\bf I}{\bf F}$ grew to 0D polyhedra by continuously extracting the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions from ZnO until its full dissolution. Because of this inhomogeneous and uneven growth process, part of the $z\mathrm{no}$ was encapsulated by the ${\bf Z n/C o{-}Z I F}$ layers, resulting in the formation of $\mathrm{ZnO}(\mathrm{a})\mathrm{Zn}/$ Co-ZIF polyhedra (Fig. 29b). In contrast, one-by-one addition of Hmim followed by Co salt led to controlled epitaxial growth of ZIFs on $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ templates (pathway 2 in Fig. 29a). ZIF-8 seed layers of $\sim40\ \mathrm{nm}$ were rapidly formed on the surface of $z\mathrm{no}$ within a short time ( $\cdot<10\ \mathrm{min})$ . Subsequent addition of Co salt induced the formation of $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Zn}_{1-x}\mathrm{(Hmim)}_{4}^{2+}$ specifically on the ZIF-8 layers, and therefore ${\bf Z n/C o-Z I F}$ uniformly nucleated and deposited onto the ZnO. The secondary ${\bf Z n/C o-Z I F}$ layers prohibited the complete dissolution of inner ZnO templates, forming the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{ZnO(}{\mathrm{a}})$ - ZI $\mathrm{F}–8@\mathrm{Zn}/\mathrm{Co}–\mathrm{ZIF}$ heterostructures (Fig. 29c). The resulting $\scriptstyle\mathbf{ZnO(}{\mathrm{a}})\cdot$ - ZIF hybrids were transformed into hollow carbon/metal composites via one-step pyrolysis at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. ZnO was carbothermally reduced and evaporated upon pyrolysis, leaving hollow inner structures and macropores. Co and Fe metals catalyzed the formation of graphitic carbon and remained well-dispersed in the form of nanoparticles (Co) or single atoms (Fe). The particle morphologies inherited from parent $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ templates (1D nanowires, 2D nanoplates, 3D nanoflowers) were well-preserved without significant deformation (Fig. 29d–f).
Urchin-like nanorods of cobalt carbonate hydroxide (UNR CCH) were also utilized as architecture directing agents to prepare urchin-like nanotubes of Co single atoms/nitrogen doped carbon (UNT Co SAs/N-C).430 UNT ZIF-67 superstructures were firstly prepared by a hydrothermal reaction of UNR CCH powders with Hmim in a Teflon-lined autoclave at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , followed by HCl washing. They were subjected to thermal treatment at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a sealed vacuum environment, which can facilitate the formation of well-dispersed Co single atoms. Therefore, multi-dimensional hierarchical tubes composed of carbon dodecahedron with embedded Co SAc were successfully synthesized.
The MO and hydroxide arrays with different morphologies and compositions can be grown on a wide variety of substrates (e.g. carbon cloth, metal foam/mesh/foil, and fluorine doped tin oxide (FTO) glass) by means of a hydrothermal reaction and electrochemical deposition.408 They are readily described in a number of literature examples and therefore can be used as versatile self-sacrificing architecture directing agents to realize 3D carbon arrays with complex morphologies and unique multiple functions.281,310,431–434 For instance, 3D architectures ensure the facile mass transport ability and the easy accessibility to catalytic active sites, and also support the MOFs to maintain their structural integrities without significant particle aggregation/collapse during thermal transformation. The substrates enhance the mechanical stability, and serve as a consecutive conductive network for fast electron/ion transport. As a result, such 3D integrated carbon composites have been popularly adopted as binder-free, conductive additive-free selfsupported electrodes in energy storage and conversion devices, such as flexible supercapacitors, electrochemical water splitting, Zn–air batteries, and Li and Na ion batteries.281,310,408,431–434
In 2015, Duan and coworkers281 described the integration of MO arrays and a conductive substrate for the first time to develop $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnO}$ quantum dots $\mathrm{(QD)/C}$ core–shell nanorod arrays (NRA) on flexible carbon cloth (Fig. 30a–c). ZnO NRAs with diameter/length of $300\ \mathrm{nm/4.5-7\\upmum}$ were vertically grown on carbon cloth by a hydrothermal deposition route, and further served as a sacrificial template and $z\mathrm{n}$ ion source for ZIF-8 shell growth. They reacted with the Hmim ligand in a mixed solvent of ${\bf D M F/H_{2}O}$ ${\big(}3:1\ \mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}$ ratio) at $70\ {}^{\circ}\mathbf{C},$ , yielding $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ NRAs. Thermal annealing under ${\bf N}_{2}$ gas flow at $650~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ led to the formation of $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnO}$ QD/C with multidimensional hierarchical architectures. Starting from identical $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ NRA on carbon cloth, the same group also fabricated N-doped carbon bubbles supported on carbon tube arrays via catalytic deposition of ethanol and reduction–evaporation process.432 The ethanol vapor penetrated the ZIF-8 shell to access the surface of the $z\mathrm{no}$ core where the catalytic ethanol decomposition-carbon deposition occurred at high temperatures $\left(500-800\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\right)$ . During this process the continuous ZIF-8 shell layer cracked into discrete ZIF-8 nanocrystals. The subsequent $\mathbf{H}_{2}/\mathbf{Ar}$ gas reduction at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ rapidly generated nano-sized Zn metal, which was completely removed by evaporation without additional acid etching. This process directly converted the $\mathrm{ZnO@C@ZIF{-}}8$ to hollow carbon tubes@N-doped carbon bubbles. Yang et al.433 further employed $\mathrm{ZnO@2IF{-}}8$ NRAs as versatile precursors for fabrication of other types of hybrid materials with tailored chemical structures and compositions. $\mathbf{ZnO}(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{C}(\mathbf{\widehat{a}})$ CoNi-LDH arrays were prepared by pyrolysis of $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ and subsequent electrochemical deposition of CoNi-LDH nanoplatelets on the external surface of $\mathbf{ZnO}(\mathbf{\widehat{a}C},$ In parallel, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}@\mathbf{C}$ NRAs were synthesized by converting $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ to $\mathrm{Fe(OH)_{3}@Z I F–F}$ e through Zn-Fe cationic exchange in aqueous solution at room temperature, followed by pyrolysis in a ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ The resulting $\mathbf{ZnO}_{\mathbf{\widehat{a}}}\mathbf{C}_{\mathbf{\widehat{a}}}$ CoNi-LDH and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}@\mathbf{C}$ were used as self-supported electrodes for fabrication of asymmetric flexible all-solid-state supercapacitors.
Solid cobalt materials (cobalt carbonate hydroxide (CCH) and cobalt oxide) were integrated with conductive metal substrates to prepare similar 3D architectures having different chemical compositions. ZIF-67 was directly grown on wellaligned CCH nanorods in a $\mathrm{Hmim/H_{2}O}/$ triethylamine solution. Subsequent treatments of oxidation, sulfurization, carbonization and phosphorization gave rise to hierarchically structured $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}(\mathfrak{a}\mathbf{X}$ composites (where $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ , CoS, C, and CoP).336 Huang et al.435 modified this procedure to prepare a CoP nanoarray wrapped with N, P-doped carbon on Ti foil. Instead of CCH arrays, Wang et al.436 used hierarchical CoO nanowires on Ni foam to synthesize CoO@ZIF-67 and in turn $\operatorname{CoO}({\mathfrak{a}}$ N-doped carbons.
Jiang and coworkers310 demonstrated fabrication of a series of well-defined MOF arrays/carbons grown onto various substrates (Fig. 30d). Different kinds of solid metal arrays (CoO, NiO and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH}_{2})$ were directly grown on diverse substrates (Ni foam, Cu mesh, Cu foil, and Fe mesh) by a hydrothermal method, which then reacted with related organic ligands to form ZIF-67, Co-MOF-74 and HKUST-1. Pyrolysis under $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ gas flow at $500~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ yielded the corresponding MO@carbon on substrates with well-retained 3D array architectures. The authors found that the choice of appropriate solvents and solid metal precursors is critical for controlled MOF growth. For instance, the dissolution of CoO in ethanol is too slow to provide enough ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ ions for ZIF-67 formation, while that in water is too fast to confine the ZIF-67 nucleation on CoO nanorods exclusively. Furthermore, the use of $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ instead of CoO only led to poorly defined heterostructures, possibly because of its limited dissolution under the given synthesis conditions. The successful preparation of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{CoO}\ @\mathbf{ZIF-}67$ was achieved by using a mixed solvent of ethanol and water ${\bf(1:1~v/v}$ ratio). Therefore, it was revealed that the balance between the MO dissolution and MOF precipitation rate is crucial for the preparation of desired hybrid MOF/carbon arrays, which is also important for applications in the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) (refer to Section 6).
Despite the progress made, a versatile approach for continuous MOF deposition with controllable thickness and dimension is still considerably lacking. One possible strategy to overcome this challenge is electrodeposition of MOFs on shape-controlled MO/conductive substrates. Shao and coworkers431 demonstrated electrosynthesis of ZIF films on commercial conductive substrates (FTO glass, carbon fibre cloth, and Ni foam) (Fig. 30e–j). ZIF-8, ZIF-67 and bimetallic ${\bf Z n}/{\bf C o}–{\bf Z I F s}$ with various molar ratios and thicknesses were electrochemically deposited on conductive substrates (working electrodes). $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ generated from $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ on the working electrodes rapidly reacted with a $\mathrm{{\bfM}(H m i m)_{4}}^{2+}$ complex to form $\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{mim})_{2}$ solids within tens of seconds at ambient temperature. The authors also reported electrodeposition of ZIFs to the nanostructured solid metal arrays with different morphologies (1D ZnO nanorod array, 2D CoAl-LDH nanowall array and 3D CoNi-alloy nanoframework), and their thermal transformation into hierarchically structured CNTs. In addition, electrodeposition could be highly attractive for the case in which direct growth of MOFs is not possible because of the incompatibility between the conductive substrate and MOFs. For instance, Feng et al.437 demonstrated electrodeposition of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ on a carbon fibre paper to deposit ZIF-67 in a controlled manner, which is not achievable by a direct MOF growth method. Very recently, Chen et al.438 vertically grew 2D Co-LDH nanosheets on ${\bf H N O}_{3}$ treated carbon cloth via a fast electrodeposition process, and utilized them as self-sacrificial templates that underwent a coordination reaction with Hmim in $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}/\mathbf{MeOH}$ ( $\mathrm{1}:1.5\mathrm{{v/v}}$ ratio) to induce ZIF67 crystal growth and associated structural reconstruction. ZIF-67 dodecahedra tended to grow/graft on top of the nanosheets, resulting in the formation of 2D ZIF-67 nanosheets grafted with 3D ZIF-67 dodecahedra (2D/3D ZIF-67). They were transformed into ultrafine Co nanoparticle embedded 2D N-doped porous carbon nanosheets and 3D hollow N-doped porous carbon polyhedra, respectively.
Solution phase MOF deposition often involves the precise choice of solvents, solid metal precursors, substrates, ligands, and reaction temperature as well as potential risks for substrate corrosion and contamination. These factors can impose limitations on controlling the thickness and morphology of the MOF layer. To address this issue, solvent-free MOF growth techniques such as hot pressing,439 steam assisted conversion,440 and MOF-CVD271 have been developed in recent years. MOF-CVD has shown promise as a versatile method that conformally transforms complex 3D structures into continuous MOF films with uniform and controlled thickness by simply exposing the substrate to ligand vapor at a moderate temperature (e.g. $100~^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ . Inspired by this, Young et al.434 reported MOFCVD mediated synthesis of MOF arrays on carbon cloth. Hydrothermally grown $z\mathrm{no}$ and $\mathrm{Co(CO_{3})_{0.5}(O H)\cdot0.11H_{2}O}$ arrays were exposed to Hmim vapor in a Teflon-lined autoclave at various temperatures $\left(90-120\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\right)$ and for various times $\left(3{-}24\ \mathrm{h}\right)$ to deposit ZIF layers with controlled thickness. Two-step carbonization and oxidation treatments led to their conversion to MO/carbon composites (e.g. $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N}$ doped carbon arrays on carbon cloth).
Challenges and opportunities. The reactive external template methods usually rely on multi-step procedures and thus are rather complicated and costly. However, the associated disadvantages could be largely compensated for by development of multi-functional, high-value added carbon materials that are otherwise not accessible. The fast and economical routes for fabrication of sophisticated architectures would be highly appreciated in the future. Solid precursors from large-scale industrial production or earth-abundant natural minerals are attractive low-cost raw materials. In addition, to date, the chemical composition of parent MOs is restricted to a few substances, mostly Zn and Co based ones. Many others still remain unexplored because of the lack of general criteria that govern the solid-to-MOF transformation. In the case of solution phase syntheses, the balance between the release rate of metal ions from the solid matter and the coordination rate of metal ions with ligands is critical for the controlled MOF growth. The solvent composition and reaction temperature also need to be optimized to localize the MOF nucleation only at the surface of MOs. However, an understanding of the underlying growth kinetics and thermodynamics is still considerably lacking. In this regard, high throughput methods are needed to rapidly screen the potential solid precursors and to establish the suitable transformation conditions. Taken together, there are still great opportunities to develop multi-functional carbonaceous materials constructed from hierarchically assembled multi-component building blocks (MO, MOFs, carbon, and substrates) through the reactive external template methods.
5.2.4. Carbon derived from top-down processed MOF. The aforementioned three strategies control the bottom-up assembly of metal ions and organic ligands in combination with various templates and substrates. In contrast, top-down processing uses the ‘pre-synthesized MOFs’ as building blocks for microfabrication (e.g. electrospinning,441 3D printing442), chemical etching443 and exfoliation444 to obtain desired morphologies. Thanks to the recent progress in the development of new processing technologies and the nano-MOFs, top-down MOF fabrication has become more easily accessible, thereby boosting the development of MOF-derived carbonaceous materials with unique structures and morphologies.
Zero-dimensional carbon. Chemical etching is a typical topdown approach to generate voids inside of solids. In recent years, controlled etching of preformed MOF crystals has attracted great interest as a means to prepare meso/macroporous MOF, hollow MOF, and complex multi-shelled MOF,367 opening up new opportunities for preparation of void-engineered carbon materials. Zhang et al.443 reported MOF nanobubbles with monocrystalline frameworks and their transformation to hollow carbons via pyrolysis (Fig. 31a). By simply immersing the preformed ZIF-8 in tannic acid solution for $5\ \mathrm{min}$ , hollow ZIF-8 nanobubbles with a thin shell of $15\ \mathrm{nm}$ were obtained. Tannic acid acts as both an etching agent and a surface protecting layer. It releases free $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ ions which can destroy the framework. Tannic acid itself is too large to penetrate into the MOF and thus adsorbs on the exposed surface of only MOFs, protecting the outer part from complete etching. In this way, the free $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ ions selectively etch the inner core of MOFs, generating hollow ZIF-8 nanobubbles. A particular advantage of the etching strategy is its ability to maintain inherent single-crystalline nature of parent MOFs, ensuring a highly ordered crystallographic orientation. Although other hollow MOFs have been prepared by direct growth/assembly of MOFs on spherical templates, they are composed of a number of aggregated MOF nanocrystals (i.e. polycrystalline) with ill-defined particle orientation and non-uniform size. In contrast, the etching-derived ZIF-8 nanobubbles retain monocrystalline nature as well as uniform and smooth surface which cannot be obtained from the conventional template methods. Moon and coworkers445 synthesized adamantane shaped, hierarchically micro- and macro-porous, N-doped carbon nanoframes via acid etching of ZIF-8 in an aqueous xylenol orange tetrasodium salt/ HCl solution. Preferential etching along the h211i direction led to the formation of unique adamantane-like particle shape.
One-dimensional carbon. Electrospinning is a simple and straightforward top-down approach for production of 1D nanofibres, and is by far the most extensively used method for processing MOFs/MDCs into 1D nanowires and nanotubes (Fig. 31b).441 In addition, the nanofibres can be directly deposited onto certain substrates or processed into functional textiles and free-standing papers. Combined with a large variety of nanoMOFs, electrospinning has great potential in the development of a number of new 1D porous carbon and carbon-based composite materials. Typical fabrication steps involve: (i) preparation of the precursor solutions of preformed nano-MOFs and polymers, (ii) electrospinning to MOF-based nanofibres, and (iii) carbonization. The intimate interaction between MOFs and polymeric binders is necessary to maintain the structural integrity of 1D nanofibres with uniformly distributed/confined MOF nanocrystals. For this purpose, polymers with functional moieties that can interact/coordinate with the exposed metal ions on the surface of MOFs have been widely used (e.g. PVP,449 PAN,323,446 poly(vinyl alcohol) $\left(\mathrm{PVA}\right)^{450})$ . For instance, the surface $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions in ZIF-8 can coordinate with the $\mathbf{C}{\equiv}\mathbf{N}$ moieties present abundantly in PAN,446 which enables homogeneous and uniform distributions of ZIF-8 nanocrystals in the PAN matrix. Upon pyrolysis, PAN-derived carbons were generated at the early stage of thermal transformation, tightly encapsulating ZIF-8 nanocrystals to change their pyrolysis behavior. Because of the strong cohesive interfacial interaction between ZIF-8 and PAN-derived carbon, ZIF-8 decomposed with significant shrinkage from inside to outside, thereby generating uniform mesopores or hollow structures (Fig. 31c and d). In addition, several functional 1D porous carbon materials have been prepared from ZIF-8, ZIF-67 and bimetallic Zn/Co-ZIF.446,449–451 Interestingly, MOF (ZIF-67 and MIL-88) nanoparticles strung on the thin PAN fibres can be fabricated by carefully controlling the electrospinning conditions (Fig. 31e).447 The PAN strings bridged the MOF joints and entangled well to form an interconnected network, which led to the formation of free standing carbon necklace papers after pyrolysis under a ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere.
MOF 2D subunits to 2D nanocarbon stacks. Xia et al.324 prepared N-doped porous graphene by thermal exfoliation of ZIF-L in the presence of LiCl/KCl salts under an inert atmosphere at ${700-900}^{\mathrm{~\circ~}}\mathrm{~C~}$ . The $\mathrm{LiCl/KCl}$ salts acted as an exfoliator and etching agent. The metal ions from molten salt $\left(>369\mathrm{~\}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\right)$ inserted into the crystal layers first, and then induced thermal exfoliation and formation of micro- and mesopores via vaporization at higher temperatures $\left(>700\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\right)$ . Final 2D ultrathin carbon nanosheets were obtained after removal of residual metal salts and impurities through acid washing. Alternatively, Xu and coworkers376 combined the conformal transformation of MOF with chemical exfoliation to obtain few-layered graphene 2D nanoribbons. The MOF-derived solid carbon nanorods were exfoliated to two- to six-layered graphene nanoribbons through a KOH-assisted sonochemical treatment and thermal activation at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This novel strategy enabled the preparation of highquality graphene nanoribbons with high yield $(>75\%)$ and purity $\left(>90\%\right)$ , which are higher than those obtained from the exfoliation of CNTs.
Three-dimensional carbon. 3D printing, also known as additive manufacturing, has attracted significant attention both in industry and academia.453 It allows direct fabrication of prototype-objects and patterns based on the digitally controlled deposition of successive layers of a material. Recently, this technology was successfully applied for manufacturing 3D complex structures of MOF-embedded polymers454 and MOFderived hierarchically porous carbon frameworks.442 The printable ink was firstly prepared by mixing the Co-MOF (leaf-shape ZIF polymorph) and Pluronic F127 triblock copolymer in water, and then printed as fine filament layers to form a 3D Co-MOF framework (Fig. 31h).442 The 3D-printed mesh architectures enabled a uniform shrinkage to retain good mechanical stability without cracking during carbonization at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The resulting 3D carbon frameworks have a hierarchical pore system ( $200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ pores from inter-weaved filaments, meso- and micropores from Co-MOF-derived carbon flakes), uniformly embedded Co nanoparticles, high electrical conductivity and mechanical stability (Fig. 31h–k).
Challenges and opportunities. Top-down processing opens up new possibilities toward designable MDC architectures. Chemical etching enables the preparation of single-crystalline MDCs with tailored pore structures, yet it is highly dependent on the stability of MOFs against the etchant solution. Exfoliations are mostly applicable to a few layer-structured MOFs. The construction of high-quality 2D MOFs and their conformal transformation without aggregation, restacking and structural collapse still remain great challenges. Therefore, development of low-cost, high yield, and versatile top-down methods is highly desired. Integrations of MOFs with microfabrication technologies are attractive approaches to obtain MDCs with sophisticated morphologies. For instance, the use of emerging technologies, e.g. biomimetic 4D printing,4 55 may allow programmed fabrication of bio-inspired complex 3D architectures that are pre-encoded with unique functions.
## 6. Applications
## 6.1. General aspects
The control of material morphology as well as understanding processes associating with material application is extremely important due to the inherent connection between physical and chemical properties. The whole purpose of demonstrating noteworthy affinity between seemingly different materials (MO, MOF and porous carbon) ultimately has to convey to their utility. We must accentuate that besides chemical designability (e.g. chemical composition) of materials, physical designability (e.g. morphology) of materials is another essential factor. Physical designability of porous materials gives the opportunity to improve the material performance via design of the physical form rather than altering the chemical component, for instance, chitin assemblies build butterfly wings with nanostructures to impart hydrophobicity for a fly in rain and at the same time glaring colours to discourage predators. It is baffling to create a general scheme that directly links material’s specific shape and size with its physical properties as both are mutually connected with the chemical composition. However, it is doable to show some pros and cons in certain applicability cases. For instance, it has been suggested that the size of nanomaterial particles has a strong influence on their toxicity, more specifically smaller particles might be more toxic per mass-based concentration.456 It led straight away to biomedical applications whereas surrounding particles such as cells and tissues or topical use can determine what nanomaterial size is required.457 The size of particles is also significant in catalysis, where along with the lowering of the size, the number of active sites increases, which is desired for catalyst efficiency.458 All in all, the application of porous materials is usually highly affected by the structuring and the morphology of the material, as both affects the optical properties, conductivities of electrons and ions, and the diffusion of gasses and liquids through the materials that have a significant impact on larger scale application for gas storage, membranes and their use for chemical transformations, be it as a catalyst itself or as a support.
## 6.2. MOF applications
MOF nanoparticles and their more complex structures found applications in many areas such as adsorption,225,459 separation,460 catalysis,461 sensing,462 optics,463–465 magnetics,466 drug delivery,467 bioimaging,468,469 tribology,470 patterning,241 as well as data-,471 energ y-250 and gas472 storage. Generally, for each application homogeneity of nanocrystals is mainly required. Nevertheless, to obtain detailed information about the dependence of morphology and implementation, it is important to present and distinguish how e.g. length of rods or size of globules affects process parameters. Thus, here we report examples of different applications of MOF materials that are affected by both the shape and size of their particles.
MOFs are well known due to their porosity and high specific surface areas; thus, they are a natural choice of materials for adsorption and separation.473 Size and morphology are also valid for such processes, especially in terms of kinetics.474 Homogeneous and smaller particles are usually more preferred to minimize diffusion limitations,475 and along with that adsorption equilibrium time decreases.476 Non-spherical anisotropic crystals are highly required when it is necessary to obtain proper orientation in films or membranes.477 Peculiar shapes are often less stable, whereas for sorption procedures defect-free surfaces are mostly needed.280 Thicker films are usually more rigid, but they permeate much less gas or liquid. For that the synthesis of MOF-membranes with thickness less than $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ has to be considered.478
The shape of the MOF crystals could also have an impact on the application of membranes in adsorption and separation processes. Some of them have a tendency to agglomerate which leads to a decrease in membrane selectivity. Thus, a compromise must be involved between choosing sophisticated or durable membranes without defects, such as pinholes or cracks,479,480 which ends up in either better selectivity or permeability.481 An interesting example of how tuning MOF crystals morphology affected membrane applicability was presented by Zuo and Chung.482 Their goal was to obtain a naturemimetic hydrophobic membrane for seawater desalination, by covering a pristine ceramic tube surface with $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–53\big(\mathrm{Al}\big)$ . Depending on the source two different types of crystals were fabricated, flower-like and ordered nanocrystals (Fig. 32). The first type was characterized with a high aspect ratio which promoted effective membrane surface area, but the nanocrystals were poorly aligned. The second MOF morphology made of small crystals was more suited for the set goal since nanocrystals were small enough to cover the whole surface leading to lower defect probability. The influence of the MOF crystal morphology is particularly noticeable in MMM (mixedmatrix membrane) composites, consisting of MOF particles in a polymer matrix. Rodenas et al.483 introduced a copper-based MOF (CuBDC) with different morphologies like nanosheets and bulk crystals into the matrix. The prepared composites exhibited very different efficiency in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation. More uniform dispersion of nanosheets in the form of lamellae-like structures showed higher separation factors.
MOF storage capabilities are strictly the result of welldeveloped specific surface area and morphology ensuing from the crystalline facets. Gases, drug particles, pollutants or electrolytes differ in particle size. Due to the wide range of MOF crystal shapes, it is possible to control pore accessibility and dictate the way how such particles will be adsorbed, dispersed, bonded and stabilized. In the research of Li et al.484 it was presented how sulfur performance in Li–S batteries was improved by using ZIF-8 with different sizes. In the range between $20~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $1\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ , the smallest MOF particles were the most efficient with sulfur implementation; however, the best cycling stabilization was achieved with average ones ( $\sim200\ \mathrm{nm})$ . Most beneficial dimensions of the particles were called ‘golden size’, which definitely are difficult to discover and match with proper utility; nevertheless, it should be fundamental for every conducted research. In another study, it was suggested that defined morphology made of MOF crystals can improve gas storage. $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr^{-}}}$ and Cr-MOFs within electrospun nanofibres revealed higher loadings towards $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ than single crystals.485
Bioapplication of MOFs is mainly focused not only on drug delivery, but also on cancer cell targeting.486 For the sake of biocompatibility, non-toxicity and safe decomposition of the selected OBUs and IBUs, they must be precisely investigated towards metabolic processes and reactivity of other biocompounds. Besides the composition, undoubtedly the morphology of the drug carrier has to be considered. It can enhance the dispersion of the API (active pharmaceutical ingredient), stabilize the API-MOF system and also have an impact on materials motion in human body fluids. The comparison of loading anti-cancer drug oridonin into nano- and micro-sized MOF-5 revealed higher capacities and later better drug release from the smaller particles.487 In the research presented by Lin et al.488 nanoplates of DBP-UiO (Hf-porphyrin based MOF) exhibited exceptional behavior in photodynamic therapy for head and neck cancer. Results suggested that specific MOF morphology and porosity was helpful in reactive oxygen species generation, and further cancerous tissue atrophy.
Atomically dispersed active sites are the major attribute of MOFs for their exquisite catalytic performance including heterogeneous catalysis,489,490 photo- and electrocatalysis (i.e. water splitting,491,492 $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction,493,494 organic transformations,495 and oxygen reduction reactions496). To increase the catalytic activity of materials enhancing their surface area is the key and can be executed in two ways: synthesizing either very small nanoparticles or super-thin films.497 Also changing the shape of the MOF shell in the core–shell structures via modulating kinetic factors (nucleation/dissolution) enables unusual size selectivity of the products in many catalytic processes.498 When it comes to applying 2D materials such as nanosheets in catalysis, features like rigidity, porosity and smoothness are highly dependent on their thickness. Jia et al.499 presented bivalent CoFe-MOFs with a thickness of $1.3~\mathrm{\nm}$ . By adjusting the membrane or film thickness it became possible to create suitable catalysts for specific purposes. It is surprising that thicker films containing mesopores in the structure are less catalytically active than non-porous thin films. Ultrathin films made of only a few atomic layers have easily accessible active sites, which is crucial in electrocatalysis. However, such thin sheets are more potent to damage or disperse in various solvents. Additionally, ultrathin films lack roughness, thus they are much smoother than thick membranes and often the development of the porous network in their structure is not observed. Han et al.500 presented the influence of Zn-MOFs $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]$ morphology on electrochemical reduction of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . The shape of materials achieved varied from rod-like to sheet-like and spherical. A sheetlike Zn-MOF had the most beneficial influence on current density, increasing it mostly due to its largest electroactive surface area. Therefore, it was the most efficient in the reduction process. Another morphology-dependent case of MOF application was the photodegradation of methylene blue by coordination polymer Prussian white (PW).501 The specific shape of the hollow cubic structures increased decomposition of the organic dye compared to cubic crystals, almost 3 times after 5 min and 2.5 time after $25~\mathrm{min}$ (Fig. 33).
For electrochemistry and sensing, MOF materials are perfect not only because of their porosity which allows ion storage and transport but also electrochemical stability and guest/host interactions.502 Once again low-dimensional crystallites favor increasing electrode surface area to shorten diffusion paths. For instance, Zhou et al.503 presented a 2D few-layer structure of NiCo-MOF and black phosphorus, which simplified charge transfer and increased battery efficiency. Depending on usage, rigid or flexible MOFs are highly required. Chemical and mechanical stability are important especially when it comes to operating devices where the dangerous short circuit may occur. Choosing short and rigid linkers with redox-inactive nodes reinforces the MOF material, and by obtaining the appropriate size of the nanoparticles all together it can amplify stability. It is particularly necessary for electrodes where dendrite formation happens. However, flexibility and malleability are handy in systems where materials’ volume expands and contracts. In this situation long and stretchy ligands that can form crystals with the smallest structural tensions are preferable.504
Formerly, bulky MOF materials limited their applicability in highly sensitive sensors. Now, when it is feasible to obtain single nanocrystals it became possible to use them in detecting very small molecules.505 Morsali et al.506 compared bulk sample and plate-like nanocrystals of $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{OBA})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DPT})_{0.5}]{\cdot\mathrm{DMF}}$ (TMU-34) for Al(III) sensing. The nanomaterial exhibited not only a faster response but also a higher detection limit. The plurality of ligands and cations allows creating MOF crystals with the required composition and morphology that will be able to trap a nanoparticle with a simultaneous reaction in the form of a structural change. For example, rare bundle-like morphology of Eu based MOFs was used as a platform to detect styrene volatile vapors.507 Fluorescent MOFs are an interesting case, proving how sensitive these materials can be. It was suggested that the response to the stimulus may be changing the distance between nodes by shortening/lengthening the linkers, and as a result changing MOF fluorescence.508 Similarly to previous applications, the size of the nanoparticles matters in sensing. It was presented by Nenoff et al.509 for ${\mathbf I}_{2}$ detection by using diverse MFM-300(X) materials $\mathbf{\tilde{X}}=\mathbf{Al}$ , Fe, In or Sc with biphenyl- $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylate as linker). The amounts of adsorbed and then polarized $\mathbf{I}_{2}$ molecules were different depending on the quantum size and morphology of MFM-300, which ultimately led to a distinct electrochemical response.
## 6.3. MOF-derived carbon applications
As already mentioned in the preceding sections, MDCs are of significant interest for various applications, e.g. electrochemical energy storage, electrocatalysis, nanomedicine or sensing. In particular, the opportunity to tailor the obtained carbon material via the precursor morphology, i.e. in 0D, 1D, 2D and 3D, has a profound effect on materials properties and applicability. As such the approach going from solids to MOF to carbon is an effective way to introduce additional versatility in the finally obtained carbon structures.
In energy-related applications, high specific surface area is one of the required features of MDC electrode materials because it considerably influences charge and discharge rates, which are significant performance indicators in modern energy storage.316 Moreover, through tailoring the structure of MDC pores that mediate ion access to electrodes, exchange kinetics can be improved. For example, the ratio of micropores to mesopores is crucial to ameliorate electrochemical properties of $\mathbf{MDCS}^{321}$ or the accessibility of active sites.510 The morphology of MDCs also has a significant impact on the overall efficiency of energy-related devices as the material needs to sequester charge carrying species.510 Therefore, 2D nanostructures are of interest due to their high surface area and good compatibility with device integration.342 In addition, MDCs contain residual metal ions that can have a positive effect on performance but might also interfere with catalytic reactions at the surface.511
The well-developed surface area of materials is especially important in catalysis and sensing because it affects reaction rates and sensitivity.512 One of the possible approaches to maximize the number of active sites on solid catalysts is to lower the size of the their particles.513 Additionally, the morphology of materials has a significant influence on molecular transport at the surface in diffusion-controlled processes. As such, tuning the shape of the catalyst particles can remarkably alter the catalytic activity and selectivity due to the significant variations in the surface atomic configurations. Particularly for catalysis, spherical structures are advantageous because of short pathways for the diffusion of species, dispersion enhancement and easy attachment to supports. Moreover, catalyst nanoparticles with an anisotropic morphology can greatly mediate the reaction properties by selectively exposing desired crystal facets.514 The morphology can affect the mechanism of the metal–support interaction as well, which is one of the most fundamental issues in heterogeneous catalysis. In nanomedicine, drug loading and designed release show a significant relation to high surface area.515 Additionally, pore structures, sizes and connections can be utilized to modulate and design release characteristics.516 Spherical systems are of particular use as they minimize viscous effects and can be functionalized homogeneously. Moreover, for smaller particles with a higher surface-to-volume ratio, therapeutic amounts of drugs can be loaded on their surfaces.517 Nevertheless, shape-anisotropic nanoparticles have been found to exhibit specific interactions with biological cells depending on particle morphology.518 Furthermore, metal ions in the final product can be useful for photothermal therapy and diagnostic tasks, e.g. imaging and sensing.
Adsorption is one of the classical applications of MOFs and MDCs, which is mainly related to their porosity and surface chemistry.316,519 Surface chemistry can be easily tailored via MO/MOF compositions. It should be noted that especially nitrogen-doped MDCs are promising adsorbents for gases including $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .316 The pore connectivity and sizes, as well as the morphology of carbon materials also have an impact on the rate of adsorption and selectivity.
Carbon materials are often used in the design of supercapacitors that are of particular interest in portable electronics.321,520 The capacitance and energy density of supercapacitors rely to a significant extent on the porosity and surface area of the utilized electrode material. Moreover, pore sizes and structures have a profound influence on supercapacitor activity, where the utilization of well-defined precursors, i.e. MOFs have a tremendous impact.521–523 In regard of future applications as devices, the development of appropriate supercapacitors is of utmost importance. In order to tackle that challenge, Shao and coworkers433 fabricated flexible carbonbased electrodes as mentioned in Section 5.2.3. To obtain a supercapacitor two nanorod arrays were generated and combined, namely ZnO@C@CoNi-LDH (as anode) and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}(\overline{{\mathbf{a}}}\mathbf{C}$ (as cathode). The nanorod array itself served as a template to obtain high surface areas including abundant and accessible active sites. After addition of PVA–KOH as a solid electrolyte, a flexible supercapacitor was designed. A high energy density of $1.078\ \mathrm{mW}$ h $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ was obtained with an outstanding cycling stability of $95\%$ capacity retention over 10 000 cycles. In a similar way, Wang and coworkers524 assembled a flexible supercapacitor from MOF-derived nanocarbon arrays on carbon cloth (Fig. 34a and b). Finally, active $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ or ${\bf B i}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ was deposited on the carbon substrate. A 2D Cu-MOF-based approach was developed by Li et al.208 A 2D MOF was synthesized from ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and 1,4-bis(2-methyl-imidazol-1-yl)butane) as a ligand to afford stacked lamella. Subsequently, the material was calcined, etched with HCl and activated by KOH to obtain carbon nanosheets with ultrahigh porosity (BET surface area of $2491\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ . The defined layers with an interspace of the MOF precursor were translated into the final carbon material, which was reflected in an excellent specific capacitance $(260.5\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.5\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\big>$ ) and energy density $(18.38\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1})$ . The correlation between morphology and capacitance was investigated by Schmidt and coworkers.363 Plate-like 2D MOFs and rod-like hexagonal MOFs were synthesized as described in Section 5.2.1 and carbonized to form the respective 2D or rod-like carbon structures. The performance of the material in supercapacitors was evaluated. Interestingly, a distinct difference in volumetric capacitance was observed, while the specific capacitance was almost the same for both carbon structures. This result shows that even the overall shape of the carbon has an influence on performance, which is a good starting point for future developments of more sophisticated architectures.
In addition to supercapacitors, batteries constitute another important type of device in electrochemical energy storage. Here, carbon materials are frequently utilized as electrode materials as well. Similar to supercapacitors, porosity, morphology and high surface area are the key parameters to obtain superior charge/discharge characteristics. Nevertheless, one of the major challenges is long-term stability and high performance at high current rates.522 In particular, Zn-based batteries are frequently investigated.525,526 Sun and coworkers527 fabricated a hierarchical electrode material based on $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ nanorods for $Z\mathrm{n}$ -batteries. At first $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ nanorods were synthesized via the hydrothermal method and coated with PVP as a support for MOF synthesis. Subsequently, ZIF-8 was formed on the surface of PVP and carbonized via thermal treatment in the final step that led to a partial reduction of $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ to a porous $\mathbf{MnO}_{\mathrm{x}}$ structure. The porous $\mathbf{MnO}_{x}$ structure facilitated ion transport and acted as a buffer space to alleviate volume expansion/ contraction during discharge/charge processes, which is a significant feature for long-term stability. Meanwhile, the MOF-derived N-doped carbon enabled efficient electron transport. For electrochemical performance, Zn was utilized as an anode and $\mathbf{MnO}_{x}@\mathbf{N-C}$ as a cathode as well as reference materials $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{M}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{n}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{O}}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{MnO}_{y}$ ). Due to the porous structure and conductive carbon network, superior electrochemical properties, e.g. cycling stability, high capacity and rate capability, were observed. In depth studies on the charge/discharge mechanism showed that the superior performance at high rates is due to the short length of ion diffusion and rapid electron transfer. In addition to Zn batteries, Li-ion batteries belong to the most important types of batteries.528,529 Thus, Duan and coworkers281 studied MOF-derived anodes for Li-ion batteries, as described in Section 5.2.3. The resulting $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnO}$ quantum dots/carbon core–shell nanorod arrays on flexible carbon cloth showed high specific capacities and rate capabilities, which was ascribed to the porous shell layer and singlecrystal $z\mathrm{no}$ core. In the area of sodium batteries, Goodenough and coworkers425 employed nitrogen-doped carbon anodes formed from MOF precursors (Fig. $34\mathrm{c-e}$ , Section 5.2.3). First, PAN was combined with zinc acetate/cobalt acetate and nanofibres were obtained via electrospinning $\mathbf{\left(PAN/M(Ac)_{2}\right.}$ film). After introduction of Hmim, a ZIF network was formed on the nanofibre surface $\big(\mathrm{PAN/M}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}$ film) and the whole composite material was carbonized to undergo a transition from carbon nanofibres (CNF) to carbon hollow tubules (CHT). Finally, the N-doped carbon material was utilized as an anode in a sodium battery with excellent rate capability, high capacity (up to $346\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ ) and cycle performance (over 10 000 cycles). The superior performance was attributed to the structural features. For example, the enlarged spacing of carbon layers facilitated effective $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ storage, while the high surface area and porous structure enabled short diffusion distance for $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ ions. Thus, sufficient contact between the active material and electrolyte was introduced for a fast chargetransfer process. Moreover, the long-aspect ratio of tubular structures improves 1D electron transport and electrical percolation. The presence of defects in MDC further assists $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ diffusion from outer to inner carbon for a rapid activation of the whole carbon material.
In contrast to the ORR that is utilized in fuel cells, electrochemical water splitting is an important process to generate hydrogen, i.e. the HER on the cathode and the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) on the anode.536,537 Here also MOF-derived carbon is utilized frequently to improve the electrocatalytic process due to conductivity from carbon and catalytic centres from metal ions.538,539 As mentioned in Section 5.2.3 Jiang and coworkers310 introduced a method to fabricate well-aligned MOF arrays and turned them into self-standing electrodes. The porous N-doped carbon arrays with incorporated Ni and CoO were utilized for the HER and OER. A superior performance compared to reference materials e.g. CoO@CoNC on Ni foam was observed. Moreover, the catalytic material showed only negligible deactivation in the OER and HER after 3000 cycles. The improved electrocatalytic performance was attributed to the porous nanoarray electrode architecture, in situ carbon incorporation, increased accessibility of active sites, accelerated release of gas bubbles, robust structure as well as improved mass and charge transport. Single W atoms on N-doped carbon were utilized by Wang and coworkers for the HER (Fig. 35d and e).531 A UiO- $66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ -based MOF was formed from $\mathrm{wcl}_{5}$ , 2-amino-benzenedicarboxylic acid and $\mathrm{zrCl_{4}}$ . After pyrolysis and HF etching, well-defined porous N-doped carbon with homogeneously dispersed W atoms (W-SAC) was obtained, which was subsequently used as an electrode in the HER. In the electrochemical investigation, a small overpotential was found with an overall activity similar to $\mathbf{\rho}_{\mathrm{Pt/C}}$ . DFT calculations showed that the $\bf{W}_{1}N_{1}C_{3}$ unit played the most important role in HER performance, which opens up new opportunities for further improvement in the future.
One of the common applications of MOFs is sensing.470,547,548 Both enzymatic and non-enzymatic sensors are described in the literature. Moreover, carbon materials derived from MOFs are utilized in sensing as well, which can be ascribed to several factors.549–551 On the one hand, MDC presents a defined environment for analytes that might be useful for the selectivity of the sensor. On the other hand, the defined environment enables a precise deposition and implementation of the (bio)recognition site, e.g. a reactive group or enzyme. Finally, the high surface area of MOFs and MDCs allows increasing number and rate of interactions with the analyte, which leads to improved sensitivity. As such, improved structural control has a profound influence on sensor performance, i.e. sensitivity and selectivity, which also leads the direction for future improvements. Thus, the MDC approach allows specific tailoring of the environment for the respective sensing application, e.g. with respect to analyte concentration, presence of competing analytes and rate of analysis. Dong and coworkers541 described MDC nanocomposites for enzyme immobilization (Fig. 36c). $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ and ${\mathrm{La}}^{3+}$ were combined with 2-aminoterephthalate to form three MOFs. After carbonization, acetylcholinesterase was immobilized in the carbon/MOF structures to perform biosensing of methyl parathion. The ${\mathrm{La}}^{3+}$ -derived structures showed the best performance, which was attributed to their hierarchical and highly porous architecture. As such, increased amounts of the enzyme could be immobilized, electron transfer enhanced and diffusion length of the analyte on the electrode surface shortened. Therefore, biosensors with a linear detection range for concentrations from $1.0~\times~10^{-13}$ to $5~\times$ $10^{-19}~\mathrm{~g~}\mathrm{~mL}^{-1}$ and the lowest detection limit of $5.8\ \times$ $10^{-14}\ \mathrm{g\mL^{-1}}$ were fabricated. A non-enzymatic glucose sensor was described by Fransaer and coworkers.552 HKUST-1 was grown on a Cu-foam substrate and pyrolyzed to obtain carbon particles with embedded Cu nanoparticles on the Cu-substrate. The fabricated electrode showed high sensitivity, low detection limit and good stability for the non-enzymatic detection of glucose. The superior performance was benefitted from the hierarchical structure of the electrode, the synergistic effect of Cu and carbon as well as their close contact. A molecularly imprinted sensor was presented by Ma and coworkers.553 On a glassy carbon electrode, carbon nanotubes and MOF-5 derived carbon were deposited. After cleaning, pyrrole was electropolymerized on the carbon surface in the presence of cysteine to obtain molecular imprinting for the cysteine molecule. The sensors could be utilized for enantioselective detection of cysteine enantiomers and then regenerated via an overoxidation process and pH adjustment.
Nanomedicine is another important area of application for MOFs.554–556 Hence, MDCs found some application in nanomedicine as well with particular areas as controlled drug delivery, bio-imaging and photothermal therapy.515 The MDCs bring several features that are useful for nanomedicine. For example, improved drug loading and delivery due to welldeveloped surface area, tunable pore size and functionalizable surface. Metal incorporation enables therapeutic effects, e.g. photothermal energy conversion. In addition, the combination with fluorescent material facilitates diagnostic tasks, like imaging and sensing. MDCs provide promising supports to combine diagnostic and therapeutic tasks in one system. Liu and coworkers557 described the sonodynamic therapy via MDC. ZIF-8 was coated with mesoporous $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ to prevent aggregation, and pyrolyzed and etched to obtain mesoporous carbon nanoparticles. The key for sonodynamic therapy was the porphyrinlike metal centres formed during the pyrolysis process. Next, sonodynamic therapy was studied in vitro and in vivo. The particles were introduced into tumor tissues via the enhanced permeability and retention effect. Upon irradiation with ultrasound, reactive oxygen species were generated, e.g. singlet $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as well as hydroxyl radicals that have the therapeutic effect to induce tumor cell apoptosis. Due to the high surface area and pore structure a strong cavitation effect in combination with ultrasound was observed that greatly enhanced the sonodynamic therapy efficiency. In the realm of drug delivery, Yamauchi and coworkers558 studied ZIF-8 derived carbon nanoparticles as intracellular carriers. A significant loading of cis-platin drug and a zeroorder release profile in the first $20\mathrm{~h~}$ were observed. The carbon nanoparticles exhibited excellent biocompatibility even at doses above $100\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ and intracellular uptake was observed in HepG2 cells. Fan et al.559 described MDC nanosheets for bacteria eradication and anti-infective therapy (Fig. 37). ZIF-8 was grown on GO sheets, carbonized subsequently to obtain $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}.$ -doped carbon on graphene and finally, oxidation led to ZnO on graphene. Next, poly(N-isopropylacrylamide) was grafted on the surface to introduce a photothermal response and obtain the final therapeutic material $\left(\mathrm{TRB-ZnO@G}\right)$ . Overall, the obtained materials entailed three modes to eradicate bacteria, i.e. $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ release, mechanical cutting of the cell membrane due to the sheet like structure and photothermal hyperthermia via NIR irradiation. In the area of bioimaging, an example of MDC was described by Xu and coworkers.366 Carbon nanodots were formed via carbonization of ZIF-8 precursors. Fluorescent particles were obtained and used for cell imaging, which is an important application in biomedical diagnostics and cell biology. Water and gas purification is a major issue in environmental technology560 in which carbon materials found application.561 For efficient adsorbents a high surface area and surface chemistry are needed, which can be implemented via the MOF precursor.
Yamauchi and coworkers562 utilized a Co-MOF as a precursor for carbon adsorbents. ZIF-67 was synthesized and carbonized to obtain microparticles. The formed particles were tested in the removal of methylene blue from aqueous solution, which showed a significant adsorption capacity (up to $500\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ) and an overall incorporation of $99.9\%$ after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Moreover, the methylene blue loaded particles could be separated from water easily via a magnetic field. The adsorption of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ was studied by Li and coworkers.563 MOF-5 was synthesized and combined with urea to form N-doped porous carbon materials. Efficient $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture was observed $\mathrm{3.7\mmol{g}^{-1}}$ at $0{}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ and 1 atm), which was ascribed to the high surface area and the introduced functional groups. The interaction of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the carbon structure was studied via DFT, showing the interaction with pyridinic, pyrrolic and amino functions that gave rise to the high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption performance.
The carbon materials have useful properties that enable various applications. Moreover, these properties can be enhanced via structuring and synthetic control.564 An approach starting from well-defined and tailorable materials like MOFs facilitates a broad variety of properties and applications, even more so if the palette of morphologies is increased by MO-derived MOF templating. Due to the formidable structural control enabled that way, in-depth investigations of structure– property relationships become viable. Overall, it is obvious that MOFs, MO-derived MOFs and MO/MOF combinations are significant tools to fabricate tailor-made carbon materials for various applications. Certainly, important developments in the field can be expected in the future.
## 7. Conclusion and perspectives
Structural and morphological control over multiple length scales is one of the most fascinating and important goals of modern solid-state and materials chemistry. Chemists have learned how to control chemical and structural features with atomic precision and thus the properties of a large variety of porous materials. The chemical control over porous molecular structures of MOF materials has reached a level of sophistication that allows for a wide spectrum of applications. Such porous materials can be developed in the form of films, membranes, monoliths, submicron particles or powders that offer many different physicochemical properties that are obtained by combining their porous texture (accessibility, pore volume, specific surface area, surface area-to-volume ratio) with the intrinsic characteristics of the matrix (mechanical properties, adsorption, chemical functionality, etc.). Although these hybrid materials have unprecedented degree of structural and functional tunability, which should lead to breakthroughs in many key technologies, they have been rarely considered in industry so far.
In general, structuring porous materials (i.e. morphology control at different length scales) gives the opportunity to improve the material performance via design of the physical form rather than altering the chemical component. Besides the unique chemical designability of MOF materials at the molecular and nano length scales, we wish to foster pronounced attention for the creation of architectures at the mesoscopic/ macroscopic scale by the development of innovating bottom-up and/or top-down strategies in order to increase the physical designability of those materials.
This aspect was taken into consideration by using MO as a precursor for synthesizing MOF structures, as they provide new opportunities for morphology control of MOF/MDC. Thereby, MOs play multiple roles, including architecture-directing agents (templates), cation reservoirs (metal ion source), preferential reaction sites for heterogeneous nucleation, and functional components in the final composite materials. As we have shown in this review the morphology control of MO materials at various length scales is an extremely large and sophisticated area, an impressive plethora of symmetrical and asymmetrical 0D, 1D, 2D, 3D, and hierarchical architectures can be precisely synthesized. In addition, MOs can be easily integrated with various functional substrates and host materials, and pre-installed on specific locations to make desired patterns.
Starting from morphology control of MO itself, the transformation of MO into MOFs and finally MDCs enables unprecedented structures and morphologies. As the predefined structures and morphologies of MOs can be transferred to MOFs and MOF derivatives, the MO-to-MOF conversion strategy will enable the preparation of MOFs/MDCs with a high level of morphological diversities. Of particular advantage in this approach is the ability to fabricate complex morphologies with multiple levels of structural hierarchies which may be otherwise difficult to achieve.
In catalysis and sensing, the surface area is of particular importance for high accessibility of the active centres of catalysts or sensors. Therefore, high reaction rates and sensitivity can be introduced to MDCs during the MO-to-MOF-to-MDC transformation. Moreover, the material morphology has a remarkable impact on molecular transport around the surface, which is a way to regulate diffusion-controlled processes. The pore structure is of considerable interest for tailoring substrate specificity or sensor selectivity, e.g. to allow pore access only for specific molecules.
Morphology controlled MDCs have shown great potential in a number of applications such as supercapacitors, batteries, fuel cells, catalysis, sensing, nanomedicine and adsorption. In energy-related applications surface area is a major feature for MDC electrode materials as it significantly influences the charge and discharge rates – one of the major performance indicators in modern energy storage. MDCs are of particular utility for tailored porosity and pore structures that mediate ion access to electrodes and allow improving exchange kinetics. The morphology of MDCs has a significant influence on the overall efficiency of energy-related devices especially when the volume of the device is relevant.
In nanomedicine, drug loading and designed release show a significant relation to the well-developed surface area and pore structure, allowing the design of release characteristics through structural tailoring by means of functionalization. Furthermore, in the MOF/MDC scaffold, incorporated metal ions can be utilized directly for photothermal therapy and enable application in diagnostic tasks, e.g. imaging and sensing. The MDC systems are a promising approach for theranostics. The classical application of adsorption is mainly related to surface area and surface chemistry of MDCs. This feature can be easily tailored via MO/MOF compositions. With the specificity of adsorption being closely related to surface chemistry and pore structures, respectively, MOF precursors allow the transfer of designed adsorption processes. Regarding the relation of MO/MOF/MDC with the aforementioned applications, a deep understanding of structure–property relationships is missing so far, where future research will have a significant impact. Although considerable progress has been made in this research field over the past decade, there are still many challenges and limitations that need to be resolved in the future.
Higher level of control over morphology, size and composition of MOFs/MDCs is greatly desirable. A considerable part of the literature relies on the use MO nanorod-arrays grown on 2D substrates to form 3D architectures. Future research should focus on the development of sophisticated 3D architectures constructed by various building blocks (MO, MOFs, carbon, and substrates). For instance, bio-inspired complex hierarchical architectures that are pre-encoded with unique functions are of great interest. In addition, controllable design and integration of different kinds of MO/MOF/MDC are essential to achieve collective and synergistic effect from the individual components for target applications. However, in many cases, the parent MOs remain in the final composite materials after the transition to MOFs or MDCs, but barely contribute to performance enhancement. They merely occupy the volume of the composites (devices), and this dead volume is a significant problem in applications where volumetric performance is relevant, e.g. in portable electronics. In this regard, precise material design is desired to merge the advantage of each component into one hybrid material.
In-depth understanding of kinetic and thermodynamic aspects of MO-to-MOF conversion is highly required for the preservation of the morphology of the parent material, with many competing rates influencing the conversion kinetics and hence the transition mechanism (dissolution crystallization vs template-assisted transition). In particular, the balance between the release rate of metal ions from MO and the coordination rate of metal ions with ligands is critical for controlled MOF deposition on MOs. The other reaction parameters (e.g. solvent composition, temperature and pH) as well as the inherent nature of MOs (e.g. chemical composition, morphology, size, surface roughness, and surface charge) also greatly affect the MO-to-MOF conversion. As a result, only a few MOs (e.g. ZnO, $\mathbf{CoO}_{x}$ ， $\mathbf{CuO}_{x}.$ ) are widely utilized for the fabrication of ‘typical’ MOFs so far, such as ZIFs, MILs and HKUST-1. A combination of theoretical calculations and experimental approaches is highly desired to expand the range of possible MOs. High throughput methods would allow for rapid screening of potential solid precursors and help establish the suitable transformation conditions.
At the moment, the MO-to-MOF conversion strategy is still rather complicated and expensive hampering large scale applications. Development of fast and economical conversion pathways would be highly appreciated and a considerable driver for the development of the field. Direct conversion of low-cost raw materials (e.g. earth-abundant bio-minerals, solids from large-scale industrial production) into value-added MOF products will attract great attention, both in academia and industry.
In addition, the relationships between MOF/MDC morphologies and their properties further need to be established. Although a wide variety of MOFs/MDCs with different compositions and morphologies are reported, there is no systematic investigation of the morphology–property relationships. To study the effect of morphology on MOF/MDC properties, all the other factors, e.g. crystallinity, particle size, porosity, surface area, configuration of carbon and heteroatom, doping level, degree of graphitization, and so on, should remain constant. However, those factors are often mutually interconnected with the morphology itself, so it is challenging to investigate the ‘true morphology effect’ exclusively. Thus, systematic and precise morphology control is highly required to understand how MOF/MDC morphology really affects the properties of the materials in order to optimize the application performance.
As highlighted in this review, morphology control (physical designability of materials) is an effective and versatile way to control the intrinsic physical and chemical characteristics of materials without changing their chemical compositions. MOF and MDC materials already showed extraordinary findings in research in the past decade(s) and preceding research results presented show great promise for further breakthroughs with the refinement of morphological control. Therefore, we believe that this precise control could highly improve the present physicochemical properties, allow the controlled synthesis of highly sophisticated composite systems and add a new dimension to the range of possible applications for MOFs and MDCs. As such, the MO-to-MOF and MOF-to-MDC conversion strategy will play a decisive role, because it enables direct access to complex morphologies that are difficult to achieve otherwise. Although considerable advancements have been achieved in recent years, this research field is still in its infancy, and there are still great opportunities to be explored in the future. We expect that this review will provide a general overview of the previous contribution so that the future direction of morphology control can be foreseen. We believe sophistication of transition mechanisms allowing the controlled transformation of parent structures into desired products over all levels is going to unravel breakthroughs in the development of MOFs/MDCs with defined structures and morphologies that are pre-designed for target applications and device fabrications.
